hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1801","And the like of that is what was narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, of his saying to Hunayy his freedman when he sent him over the preserve: And beware the lord of the small herd of camels, and the lord of the small flock of sheep. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. And what we mentioned shows the difference of the owned among the sons of Adam and those besides them in what we mentioned. And someone said: The owned among the sons of Adam were only forbidden this saying to one who owns them because they entered the covenant Allah took over the sons of Adam by His saying Exalted and Almighty: {And when your Lord took from the sons of Adam, from their backs, their offspring, and made them witness over themselves: Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes, we witnessed — lest you say on the Day of Resurrection: Indeed we were of this heedless} — so the owned of the sons of Adam are of those over whom Allah Almighty took this covenant as He took it over the rest of the sons of Adam besides them; and beasts are not so, nor was taken over them the like of this covenant. So thereby it was released to say to the owned besides the sons of Adam the saying we mentioned, and barred in the sons of Adam; for it was taken over them that Allah is their Lord, so giving them the like of this saying to other than Him, Exalted and Almighty, and others giving them therein the like of that, is rivalry — so they were forbidden that. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1802","252 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Whatever is cut from a living being is dead}. 1572 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Waqid al-Laythi, he said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while people were cutting off camels' humps and severing sheep's tails. So he said: Whatever is cut from an animal while it is alive is carrion}."
"1803","1573 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal and Miswar ibn al-Salt narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar — al-Miswar said: from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri — {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about cutting off camels' humps and sheep's tails. So he said: Whatever is cut from a living being is dead}. Then someone said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when in it is what necessitates that what is cut from an animal of hair or wool while alive is dead, while Allah Almighty's Book repels that? Allah said: {And Allah made for you of your houses rest, and made for you of the hides of livestock houses you find light on the day of your travel and day of your residence, and of their wools and furs and hairs furnishings and enjoyment for a time} — so Allah Almighty informed us He made wools, furs and hairs enjoyment for us; how is it permitted they be carrion when Allah made them enjoyment for us? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that what is in the two hadiths we narrated in this chapter does not contradict what is in the verse we recited in it; for what is in those two hadiths is only upon camels' humps and sheep's tails cut from them while alive, of what had they died before that, those things would have died with their death; while hair, wool and fur are not so, for they do not die with their death; and humps and tails show in them the attributes of death with the death of whose they are from — their corruption and change of smells — while wool, hair and fur are not so, for all that is absent in them. So what is of what the attributes of death occur in with their occurring in what it is from — of humps and of tails — it has the ruling of what is in these two hadiths; and what the attributes of death do not occur in with the death of what it is in, is outside that and inside the verse we recited. And what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) points to that:"
"1804","1574 - Of what Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, that he said: {A dead sheep was passed with before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which he had given to a freedwoman of Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Why did you not benefit from its hide? They said: O Messenger of Allah, it is carrion. He said: Only eating it was forbidden}."
"1805","1575 - And of what Yunus also narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1806","1576 - And what Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except he said: Only its meat was forbidden. Abu Ja'far said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that what was forbidden of the sheep by its death is only what is eaten of it. That shows that what besides the eaten of it, since it was not forbidden of it, remains after its death upon what it was before its death. So in what we mentioned is what points to the meaning of the first two hadiths, and to what death forbids of animals and what it does not forbid of them; and that what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the two hadiths we narrated is not outside the verse we recited. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1807","245 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about greeting when a man stands at his brother's door — how many times is it? 1577 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the Ansar; when he came to the Ansar's houses, the Ansar's boys would circle around him, so he would supplicate for them, wipe their heads and greet them. Then he came to the door of Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah and greeted them: Peace upon you and Allah's mercy and blessings. So Sa'd responded without letting the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hear, three times; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not exceed three greetings — if permitted, else he left. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, and Sa'd came hastening and said: O Messenger of Allah, you did not greet a greeting but I heard it and responded, but I wanted you to increase us in greeting and mercy. So enter, O Messenger of Allah. So he entered and sat, and Sa'd brought him food, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took of it. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to leave he said: The righteous ate your food, and the fasting broke fast with you, and the angels prayed upon you}. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) teaching people not to exceed in greeting at standing at doors over three times; for that informs the greeter that in that house is one whose returning his greeting is permissible, of men, so he waits for him — or that in it is one whose returning greeting to him is not permissible, of women, so he leaves. And this is an established practice and fine etiquette, which should not be transgressed to other than them. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1808","Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about seeking permission — how many times is it. 1578 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, that Busr ibn Sa'id narrated to him that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: We were in a gathering at Ubayy ibn Ka'b's when Abu Musa al-Ash'ari came angry until he stood, and said: I adjure you by Allah, did any of you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Seeking permission is three times; if permitted, else return}? So Ubayy said: What is that? He said: I sought permission from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, yesterday three times and was not permitted, so I returned; then I came to him today and entered upon him and informed him I came to him yesterday, greeted three times, then left. So he said: We heard you while we were then busy; why did you not seek permission until permitted? He said: I sought permission as I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say. So he said: By Allah, I will surely strike your belly and back, or you bring me one who testifies for you to this. So Ubayy ibn Ka'b said: By Allah, none will stand with you except the youngest of us in age to answer you. Stand, O Abu Sa'id. So I stood until I came to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say this."
"1809","1579 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri and Sa'id — Abu Ja'far said: meaning Ibn Yazid al-Azdi Abu Maslamah — narrated to us, they said: We heard Abu Nadrah narrating from Abu Sa'id, he said: Abu Musa came and sought permission from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab once, then sought permission a second, then sought permission a third, and he did not permit him, so he returned. So 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said to him: You shall surely bring me proof for what you said, or I shall do to you. He said: So he came to the Ansar and said: Do you not know that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {When one of you seeks permission three times and is not permitted, let him return}? They said: None will testify for you except our youngest. Abu Sa'id said: So I came to him and narrated to him."
"1810","1580 - And Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Khayran al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and added: And I narrated to him while his shirt was touching my head."
"1811","1581 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, that Abu Musa sought permission from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was busy with some matter; when he finished he said: Did I not hear the voice of 'Abdullah ibn Qays? They said: He returned. He said: Bring him back. So he came, and said: What is this? He said: We were commanded with this in seeking permission three times. He said: You shall surely bring me proof for this, or I shall do and do. He said: So he came to the Ansar's gathering and informed them. They said: None will stand with you except our youngest. So Abu Sa'id al-Khudri stood with him and came and said: Yes. So 'Umar said: Was this of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) matter hidden from me, while bargaining in markets distracted me? Ibrahim said: I found on the back of my book: And clapping in markets distracted me. Abu Ja'far said: And this in our view does not contradict Anas ibn Malik's hadith about greeting which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter; and what is in Anas ibn Malik's hadith had been from Abu Musa before his seeking permission, while the transmitters of these reports left transmitting that, for their knowledge that it is of the practice to begin with greeting before seeking permission. And proof of that:"
"1812","1582 - That Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya al-Qurashi, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: I came to 'Umar's door, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Peace upon you, may 'Abdullah ibn Qays enter? I was not permitted, so I said: Peace upon you, may 'Abdullah ibn Qays enter? I was not permitted, so I returned. Then 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, woke up and said: Bring Abu Musa to me. So he came and said: Where did you go? I said: I sought permission three times and was not permitted, so I returned; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Let a Muslim man seek permission from his brother three times; if permitted, else let him return}. He said: You shall surely bring me a witness for what you said, or punishment from me shall befall you. He said: So I went out and met Ubayy ibn Ka'b and informed him. He said: Yes. So he came and informed him. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: O Abu al-Tufayl, did you hear what Abu Musa said from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. And I seek refuge with Allah Almighty that it be punishment upon the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And I seek refuge with Allah from that. Abu Ja'far said: What we mentioned shows that Abu Musa had begun with greeting before seeking permission; and we know with certainty that Abu Musa did not do that by opinion or inference, but did it by instruction from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him upon it; for the like of this is not taken from opinion nor by inference, but only from instruction — and instruction is found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah Almighty said in His Book: {O you who believe, do not enter houses other than your houses until you seek familiarity and greet their people} — and seeking familiarity here is seeking permission; likewise it is in the language of the people of Yemen, existing therein till now. Al-Farra' mentioned that, saying: The Arabs say: Seek familiarity — look whether you see anyone in the house — meaning: seek permission whether you see anyone in the house. Then someone said: In the verse we recited is putting seeking familiarity before greeting, while in Abu Musa's hadith is putting greeting before seeking permission. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that what is in the verse we recited is with them upon advancing and delaying — like what is in His saying Almighty: {After a bequest he bequeaths or debt}, upon advancing and delaying; and like what is in His saying Almighty: {O Mary, be devout to your Lord and prostrate and bow with those who bow}, upon advancing and delaying — for bowing in prayers is before prostration therein. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the hadith of Kaladah when he entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without permission, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go out or return, then say: Peace upon you, may I enter? And in that is proof of what we mentioned, and Allah knows best."
"1813","And it was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas about seeking familiarity. What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, about His saying Almighty: {Do not enter houses other than your houses until you seek familiarity and greet their people}: He said: The scribe erred; it is only: until you seek permission."
"1814","And what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1815","And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Seeking familiarity is seeking permission — and it is in my reckoning the scribe's hand erred. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1816","255 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about his commanding Kaladah, when he entered upon him without permission, to go out then say: Peace upon you, may I enter? 1583 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi Sufyan informed me, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Abdullah ibn Safwan informed me, he said: Kaladah narrated to us, that Safwan ibn Umayyah sent him at the time of the Conquest, or the year of the Conquest, to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with milk and a young goat and mountain asparagus, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the top of the valley. So I entered without greeting nor seeking permission. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go out or return, then say: Peace upon you, may I enter? Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of this in our view — and Allah knows best — is that since Kaladah's entering without greeting nor seeking permission was a disliked entering, his sitting upon that was disliked, since its cause was a disliked entering. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to cut the causes of the disliked entering, and to return and greet and seek permission, so his entering be praiseworthy and his sitting praiseworthy sitting. And with Allah is guidance."
"1817","256 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), {his saying to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud: Your permission from me is that the veil be lifted and that you hear my private talk until I forbid you}. 1584 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubaydullah, from Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from a man of Nakha', he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud: Your permission is that the veil be lifted and you listen to my private talk — meaning my secret — until I forbid you. Abu Ja'far said: My private talk: my secrets."
"1818","1585 - Husayn ibn Nasr and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubaydullah, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Your permission from me is that the veil be lifted and that you listen to my private talk until I forbid you} — except Husayn ibn Nasr said: Ibrahim ibn Suwayd said: And he said: my secrets."
"1819","1586 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Nakha'i, from Ibrahim ibn Suwayd, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. So Sufyan, 'Abdullah ibn Idris and Hafs ibn Ghiyath differed over Ibrahim the transmitter of this hadith: Sufyan said he is Ibn Zayd — meaning the jurist — while Hafs and Ibn Idris said he is Ibn Suwayd; both are of Nakha', and two are worthier of retention than one. Abu Ja'far said: The aspect of that in our view, and Allah knows best, is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) released to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud lifting the veil from him; that was from him permission to him sparing him seeking permission when intending to enter upon him. And in that is nothing barring him before that greeting as one intending permission besides him greets. And Allah knows best, and with Him is guidance."
"1820","257 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {A man's messenger to a man is his permission}. 1587 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id — meaning Ibn Abi 'Arubah — informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {When one is invited and comes with the messenger, that is permission for him}."
"1821","1588 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub and Habib, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {A man's messenger to a man is his permission}. Abu Ja'far said: We considered this hadith and found the best of what it bears to be that {a man's messenger to a man} — meaning the one sent to — spares the comer without message of greeting and seeking permission together before entering the house he intends to enter; for when he comes with a message from the house's owner to him with his messenger — and seeking permission is unavoidable for the messenger, since the sender's states are not secure to him, for it is permissible he sent him for what he sent him in while upon a state he would not dislike being seen upon, then he comes while upon other than that state, so he needs for that to seek permission upon him again for this meaning — while the one sent to is spared seeking permission and greeting by the sent messenger's seeking permission and greeting; for the sender knows that his messenger, when he returns to him, returns upon one of two stations: either the one he sent him for love of him has stayed back from him, so his messenger enters upon him after greeting and seeking permission that had been from him before his entering upon him; or he is with him, so his prior permission to him to come to him with him has preceded, so he comes with him — so his entering upon him with the messenger's seeking permission spares his greeting and his seeking permission before entering, then he greets after that the greeting of meeting. Then someone said: But you narrated from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what contradicts this:"
"1822","1589 - So he mentioned what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Dharr narrated to us, he said: Mujahid informed me, {that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to call for him the people of the Bench, in a long hadith mentioned in it: So I called them, and they came and sought permission, and he permitted them}. He said: In this hadith is the people of the Bench's seeking permission, though they came with the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) message to them — Abu Hurayrah — while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny their seeking permission, saying to them: You were spared this by your coming with my messenger to you to come to me — so this contradicts the first hadith. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that what is in the first hadith in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon the one sent to coming with the messenger to him — so that spares him seeking permission, as in the first hadith; while the second hadith is only in the people of the Bench's coming, with no mention in it that Abu Hurayrah was with them — so it is permissible they preceded and came without him, needing seeking permission. And among what shows that was so is Abu Hurayrah's saying: So they came until they sought permission and were permitted — he did not say: So we came and sought permission and were permitted. So by Allah's praise and blessing, neither of these two hadiths contradicted the other. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1823","258 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Among the signs of the Hour are greeting of acquaintance and greeting of the exclusive}. 1590 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Sayyar Abu al-Hakam narrated to us, from Tariq, he said: We were with 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud when his permission came, and he said: Prayer has stood. So he stood and we stood with him, and entered the mosque, and saw people bowing at the front of the mosque, so he said Allah is greatest and bowed and walked, and we did like what he did. Then a man passed hastening and said: Peace upon you, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. So he said: Allah Almighty spoke truth and His Messenger conveyed. When we prayed he returned and entered his family, and we sat in our place waiting for him till he comes out. Some of us said to some: Which of you will ask him? So Tariq said: I will ask him. So Tariq asked him, saying: The man greeted you, and you responded to him: Allah spoke truth and His Messenger conveyed. He said: So he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: {Before the Hour is greeting of the exclusive, spread of trade until a woman helps her husband in trade, severing of kinship, appearance of false testimony, and concealment of true testimony}."
"1824","1591 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, that he was with Masruq while Ibn Mas'ud was between them, when a Bedouin came and said: Peace upon you, O son of Umm 'Abd. So Ibn Mas'ud laughed and said: Why do you laugh? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Among the signs of the Hour are greeting by acquaintance, and a man passing by a mosque then not praying in it."
"1825","1592 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Abbar narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Masruq or another — so 'Umar said — he said: {A man entered the mosque while Ibn Mas'ud was in the mosque with a man, and said: Peace upon you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. So he said to him: And upon you; Allah is greatest; Allah and His Messenger spoke truth, Allah and His Messenger spoke truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the signs of the Hour is that a man greets a man only for acquaintance — or from acquaintance — or that he passes by a mosque its length and breadth, then does not pray in it two units; and among the signs of the Hour is that the barefoot naked — or he said: the naked barefoot — compete in building mud-brick structures, and that a youth sends an old man as courier between the two horizons}. Then someone said: But you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in his returning greeting to one who greeted him with an exclusive return by his saying: And upon you, peace — and he mentioned:"
"1826","1593 - What Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Yahya ibn 'Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad ibn Rafi' al-Zuraqi, from his father, from his grandfather, from Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he was sitting in the mosque with us with him, a man like a Bedouin entered, prayed and shortened his prayer, then left and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon you; return and pray, for you did not pray}."
"1827","1594 - And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah and al-Layth informed me, from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan, from one who informed him, from 'Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his uncle Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', he said: {We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man entered and prayed, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) watched him. When he finished he came and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: And upon you from me peace; return and pray, for you did not pray}."
"1828","1595 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us; and what 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah al-Qaysi informed us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal al-'Adawi narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, in the hadith of his Islam, he said: So I reached him — meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — after he and his companion had prayed — meaning Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him — and I was the first to greet him with the greeting of Islam, and he said: And upon you and Allah's mercy. He said: In this hadith, in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returning greeting with an exclusive return, not generalizing the greeter and others of people therewith, is what they deny — that greeting be exclusive greeting to one whom the greeter intends greeting upon, besides others of those he does not intend greeting upon. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that one greeting one of a group has upon him greeting upon each of that group as upon him is greeting to the one who greeted him; so his singling that one with that greeting besides their rest is wrong from him to their rest; for of a Muslim's right upon a Muslim is to greet him when he meets him — while returning from a Muslim is only return from himself, not from another; or return from a group he is of, as people of knowledge say in that of what they differ in of it. So return is upon one, so it is permitted to single him therewith besides others of people; so it is said to him: And upon you. And the peace from the comer to a group is greeting he must generalize the group therewith; so when he intends thereby one of them, he has fallen short of the rest of the obligation he has to them in that. And of what enters this chapter is what we mentioned earlier in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah when {the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ubayy ibn Ka'b while he was praying, and he did not answer him; when he finished he came to him and said: Peace upon you, O Messenger of Allah — and we mentioned it earlier in this book of ours — so that is exclusive greeting, and it is in our view not contrary to what we mentioned before it in this chapter; for it is permissible he greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alone, so he did not deny that to him. Then someone said: But Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated Abu Dharr's hadith we mentioned, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, differing with Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah therein."
"1829","1596 - So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, he said: Abu Dharr said to me... then he mentioned the hadith of his Islam. He said: So I said: Peace upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And upon you. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that it is possible Abu Dharr was with Abu Bakr while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was busy either with prayer or with circumambulation of the House — for that was at Makkah, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the House — so he had no need to greet Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, while his need was to greet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he intended his greeting to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that. And this shows it is permitted for one who comes to one man with none other with him to have his greeting to him: Peace upon you — contrary to what his greeting would be had he come to a man in a group, in his greeting generalizing them and him therewith. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1830","259 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {No deficiency in prayer nor in greeting}. 1597 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Bundar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Malik, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No deficiency in prayer nor in greeting}. And I heard 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz say: Abu 'Ubayd said: Deficiency is shortcoming — and he related it from al-Kisa'i. He said: And Abu 'Ubayd argued for that with al-Farazdaq's saying in his elegy of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf: scant — i.e. little. Abu 'Ubayd said: Its meaning in prayer is shortcoming in its bowing, prostration and purification; and in greeting we think it is to say: Peace upon you — or to return saying: And upon you — and not say: And upon you all. Abu Ja'far said: The shortcoming forbidden in greeting may be contrary to what Abu 'Ubayd said, and what is meant thereby may be shorting the group of greeting upon them, and intending in its place greeting upon one of them. And returning greeting is not of that at all, for what we mentioned necessitating difference of the ruling of greeting and returning greeting, in the chapter we mentioned before this chapter. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1831","260 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {You and your property belong to your father}. 1598 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Hiri and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud al-Asadi together narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Tinnisi narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that {a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have property and dependents, and my father has property and dependents, and he wants to take my property to his property. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You and your property belong to your father}. So I asked Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas about what is meant by this hadith. He said: What is meant thereby is present in it — that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said therein: {You and your property belong to your father} — so he joined therein the son and the son's property and made them to his father; his making them to his father was not upon his father's owning him, but upon his not departing from his father's word about him. Likewise his saying: Your property to your father is not upon the meaning of making him own his property, but upon the meaning of his not departing from his word about it. And I asked Ibn Abi 'Imran about it, and he said: His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith: You and your property belong to your father is like Abu Bakr's saying, may Allah be pleased with him, to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I and my property are only to you, O Messenger of Allah, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No property benefited me as Abu Bakr's property benefited me — meaning thereby."
"1832","1599 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No property ever benefited me as Abu Bakr's property benefited me. He said: So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I and my property are only to you, O Messenger of Allah. So Abu Bakr's intent, may Allah be pleased with him, by this saying of his was: That your sayings and doings are effective in me and in my property as the sayings and doings of owners of things are effective in things. Likewise the Messenger of Allah's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his questioner mentioned in this hadith is upon this meaning, and Allah knows best. And Allah's Book came with what uncovered for us the problematic in this answer from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what necessitates negation of the father's ownership of what the son owns. Allah said: {And those who guard their private parts, except over their wives or what their right hands possess, for indeed they are not blamed} — so what the son owns of slave-girls is lawful for him to have intercourse with them, and unlawful for his father to have intercourse with them. That shows his ownership in them is complete sound ownership, and his father in them is contrary to that. And He said Almighty in the verse of inheritances: {And to his parents, to each of them a sixth} — so He made for his mother a share in his property by his death; and it is impossible she deserves by her son's death a part of property belonging to his father besides him. Then He said Almighty: {After a bequest he bequeaths or debt} — so it is impossible that what is upon him of debt be fulfilled from property belonging to his father besides him, or that a bequest from him be permitted in property belonging to his father besides him. He said: And in what I mentioned of this is what points to what I described therein. Abu Ja'far said: And these two answers from these two shaykhs were sound, each supporting its companion. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1833","261 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Many a carrier of understanding to one more understanding than him, and many a carrier of understanding who is no understander}. 1600 - Abu Bishr 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Umar ibn Sulayman, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Aban ibn 'Uthman, from his father, that he heard Zayd ibn Thabit say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {May Allah brighten a man who heard a hadith from me and memorized it till he conveyed it to another; so many a carrier of understanding to one more understanding than him, and many a carrier of understanding who is no understander}."
"1834","1601 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at al-Khayf of Mina and said: May Allah brighten a man who heard my saying and retained it, then conveyed it to one who did not hear it; so many a carrier of understanding with no understanding, and many a carrier of understanding to one more understanding than him}."
"1835","1602 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: 'Abd al-Salam narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Then someone asked about the understanding intended in these two hadiths — what is it? Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that it is comprehension. From that is Allah Almighty's saying in His Book of what He related from His prophet Musa (peace be upon him): {And loosen a knot from my tongue, they may comprehend my saying}, and His saying Almighty: {And there is no thing but glorifies with His praise, but you do not comprehend their glorification} — i.e. you do not understand it. He said: Is every comprehender an understander? Our answer to him in that is that it is not said: every comprehender is an understander, even if he comprehended that thing he understood; for since understanding's worth is great, exceeding the measures of all other things of knowledges, its people were singled to be called: the understanders, and raised thereby over others of comprehenders — so it is not permitted to release to others of that what was released to them of it. And of what points to that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Understanding is Yemeni}."
"1836","1603 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: {Faith and understanding are Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni}."
"1837","1604 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani and 'Abdullah ibn 'Awn, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to us, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: {Faith is Yemeni, understanding is Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni}. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called that understanding, and distinguished it from all other comprehended things besides it — so he did not call them understanding. Likewise its people: it is released for them to be called understanders, while it is not released for others of comprehenders to be called understanders. And it is established thereby that every understander is a comprehender, while not every comprehender is an understander. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1838","262 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of {what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his writing the warranty he wrote for al-'Adda' ibn Khalid ibn Hawdhah, in his selling him a slave or slave-girl — the sale of a Muslim to a Muslim, no defect, no treachery, no unlawfulness}. 1605 - 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Qurashi, then al-'Attabi, Abu Khalid, narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad ibn Layth narrated to us, he said: {'Abd al-Majid ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: al-'Adda' ibn Khalid ibn Hawdhah said to me: Shall I not read to you a writing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for me? I said: Yes. So he brought out for me a writing, and in it was: In the name of Allah, the Merciful, the Compassionate: This is what al-'Adda' ibn Khalid ibn Hawdhah bought from Muhammad, Messenger of Allah: he bought from him a slave or slave-girl — 'Abd al-Majid doubted — the sale of a Muslim to a Muslim: no defect, no treachery, no unlawfulness}. Abu Ja'far said: And we had heard before that this hadith from more than one who narrated it to us from one who narrated it to him — 'Abbad this. And from them:"
"1839","1606 - Abu Umayyah narrated it to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad narrated to us... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1840","1607 - And from them Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated it to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad... then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1841","1608 - And from them Yazid ibn Sinan narrated it to us, he said: My brother Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad narrated to us... then they mentioned with his chain the like of it, except they did not say in their hadith: nor treachery. So we considered this hadith and found defects are understood to be illnesses; and found treacheries understood to be treacheries of the sold of blameworthy manners therein — of running away, thefts, and all blameworthy states by which others are taken unawares. From that is said: So-and-so killed so-and-so by treachery. From that is the Messenger of Allah's hadith (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {I intended to forbid ghilah — intercourse while nursing — until I remembered that Persians and Romans do that and it does not harm their children} — i.e. what befalls their carried children of what is to their mothers from their intercourse with them while so; so that was called ghayl, for it comes to their children whence they know not. And we shall mention that with its chains and what people of knowledge said therein later in this book of ours, if Allah wills. Likewise these things by which slaves take their owners unawares, of the kinds we mentioned. And we found unlawfulness: people said therein two sayings. One: that it is the blameworthy thing — and it is capture of covenanted people whose enslavement is unlawful, and ownership does not fall thereby upon them; so Ibn Abi 'Imran used to mention it to us from people of knowledge of that kind, relating no disagreement therein to us. As for others of people of knowledge of this kind, they used to say: Unlawfulness is blameworthy things. From that is Allah's saying: {Filthy women for filthy men, and filthy men for filthy women}, and from it His saying Almighty: {And what is filthy emerges only scantily}. They said: So every blameworthy is filthy; and these things we mentioned that they are treacheries are blameworthy, disliked — so everything of them with them is unlawfulness. Of proof in that for one who took Ibn Abi 'Imran's path is that treacheries, as they mentioned, are filths while they are treacheries; and that every filth is treachery, while not every treachery is filth. So returning of capture is no deed of slaves therein, as the blameworthy deeds we mentioned in treacheries are deeds of them. So treacheries were as we mentioned, while unlawfulness was of what slaves have no deed in — it is only others' deed in them. So thereby treachery and unlawfulness were distinguished for this meaning. And this in our view is liker than the other saying. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1842","263 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {The millstone of Islam will turn — or move — after thirty-five, or thirty-six, or thirty-seven}, and what he mentioned in the hadith narrated from him therein. 1609 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, from al-Bara' ibn Najiyah, he said: 'Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: {The millstone of Islam will move after thirty-five, or thirty-six, or thirty-seven years; if they perish, then the path of whoever perished; and if their religion stands for them, then seventy years. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Prophet of Allah, of what passed or of what remains? He said: No, rather of what remains}."
"1843","1610 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-'Awwam ibn Hawshab informed us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {The millstone of Islam will move at the head of thirty-five, or thirty-six, or thirty-seven; if they perish, then the path of whoever perished; and if they remain, their religion remains for them seventy years}."
"1844","1611 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from al-Bara' ibn Najiyah al-Muharibi, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The millstone of Islam will turn after thirty-five, or thirty-six, or thirty-seven; if they perish, then the path of whoever perished; and if their religion remains for them, then seventy years. 'Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, of what passed or of what remains? He said: Of what remains}."
"1845","1612 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mujalid, from 'Amir, from Masruq, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The millstone of Islam will move after thirty-five; if they reconcile among themselves without fighting, they will enjoy the world seventy years in ease; and if they fight, they will ride the ways of those before them}."
"1846","1613 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from al-Bara' ibn Najiyah al-Kahili, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... then he mentioned the like of Abu Umayyah's hadith from Qabisah which we mentioned in this chapter, except he said: The millstone of Islam turns. Abu Ja'far said: We considered these reports to stand upon what is meant thereby, if Allah wills. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The millstone of Islam turns or moves — he means thereby the affairs upon which Islam turns, likening that to a millstone and naming it by its name. And his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): After thirty-five, or thirty-six, or thirty-seven — is not upon doubt, but upon its being in what Allah Almighty wills of those years. So He Almighty willed it was in the year thirty-five: there befell the Muslims besieging their leader and withholding his hand from what he undertakes over them, despite his lofty worth — for he is of the rightly-guided rightly-guiding caliphs — until that became cause for shedding his blood, may Allah be pleased with him, and until that became cause for disagreement, split of word and difference of opinions. So that was of what, had they perished upon it, would have been a path of ruin, for its enormity and what befell Islam of it; but Allah covered and remedied, and left after His prophet among his community one who guards their religion upon them and keeps that for them. Then we considered what remains of these reports, and found in Masruq's hadith of them from 'Abdullah: {If they reconcile among themselves without fighting, they will enjoy the world seventy years in ease}; and found in place of that in the two hadiths of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and al-Bara' ibn Najiyah: If their religion remains for them, then seventy years. And that had come differing in Masruq's hadith and his two companions' hadiths. So what is in Masruq's hadith was worthier and liker to what people's affairs ran upon than what is in the other two's hadiths; for what is in Masruq's hadith: {If they reconcile among themselves without fighting, they will enjoy the world seventy years in ease} — and they did not reconcile without fighting, so the period they enjoy the world therein so would be seventy years, then cut off, so they would not enjoy it after it; but their affairs ran upon other than that of what fighting did not cease with them — so that was mercy from Allah to them and covering from Him upon them. So it ran upon that they enjoy the world without timing upon them therein. And what is in the two hadiths of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah and al-Bara' ibn Najiyah necessitates contrary to that, necessitating cut-off of their enjoying the world after seventy years. And we found them, by Allah's praise and blessing, enjoying it after that seventy years and seventy years and increase over that, with their religion standing upon its state. So we understood thereby that the basis of the hadith in that is as Masruq narrated therein, not as his two companions narrated; for there is no breaking what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1847","Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {No alliance in Islam, and hold to the alliance of pre-Islam}. 1614 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Ibn Abi Maryam together narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No alliance in Islam, and whatever alliance was in pre-Islam, Islam only increased it in strength}."
"1848","1615 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Tarsusi informed us, he said: Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So Yahya ibn Zakariyya and Ishaq ibn Yusuf differed over Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah in this hadith's chain as we mentioned in their differing therein. And Allah knows best the correct in that — except what hearts incline to therein is what Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated upon it, for his firmness, retention and lofty worth in knowledge — until Yahya al-Qattan said therein what Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Surayj al-Naqqal narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id say: None in Kufa is heavier upon me in disagreement than Yahya ibn Zakariyya. And sufficient for a man that Yahya ibn Sa'id says in him the like of this saying."
"1849","1616 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mughirah, from his father, from Shu'bah ibn al-Taw'am al-Dabbi, he said: {Qays ibn 'Asim asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about alliance. He said: No alliance in Islam, but hold to the alliance of pre-Islam}. Then someone said: How do you accept this, while you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he allied in Islam between the Muhajirun and Ansar."
"1850","1617 - So he mentioned what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, he said: 'Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allied between the Muhajirun and Ansar in our house. So it was said to him: Did not the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No alliance in Islam? So he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allied between the Muhajirun and Ansar in our house}. Sufyan said: The scholars explained it: He made brotherhood between them. He said: This objector we mentioned did not heed what we related to him from Ibn 'Uyaynah, from the scholars he related it from; and said: Allah's Book came with what informs there had been alliance in Islam — and he mentioned Allah Almighty's saying: {And to each We made heirs of what parents and near ones left, and those your oaths bound — so give them their share}. Our answer to him in that — by Allah Almighty's guidance and help — is that what he recited to us from Allah's Book is as he recited, but Allah Almighty abrogated it."
"1851","And that is that Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn 'Abdullah — and he is al-Hammal — informed us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Idris ibn Yazid narrated to me, he said: Talhah ibn Musarrif narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, about His saying Almighty: {And those your oaths bound — so give them their share}: He said: The Muhajirun, when they came to Madinah, would bequeath to the Ansar besides kin, for the brotherhood the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made between them. When the verse came down: {And to each We made heirs of what was left}, it abrogated it — {And those your oaths bound — so give them their share} — of help, advice and support, and he bequeaths to him; and inheritance is gone. So Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, informed that this verse was abrogated by another — meaning that His saying Almighty abrogated it: {And blood relatives, some of them are nearer to some in Allah's Book}. So Ibn 'Abbas informed in this hadith of his that what remains for them — meaning the allies — after this verse's coming is help, advice and bequest, and inheritance is gone. He said: When what is in this hadith and what is in Anas ibn Malik's hadith are joined, it shows there had been alliance there; and Allah Almighty's saying confirms that: {And those your oaths bound}. He said: In this is what contradicts what you narrated — that there is no alliance in Islam. It was said to him: It does not contradict it; for the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {No alliance in Islam} was only from him at his conquest of Makkah."
"1852","1618 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, he said: {When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah in the year of the Conquest, he stood preaching and said: O people, whatever alliance was in pre-Islam, Islam only increased it in strength; and no alliance in Islam}."
"1853","1619 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. So 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr informed that this saying was only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Makkah's conquest; and what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the Muhajirun and Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, of brothering between them in which he allied between them, was before that at Madinah. And what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his sermon on the day of Makkah's conquest, of what 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr mentioned, abrogates that. And there was not from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after his saying: {No alliance in Islam}, any alliance till Allah took him, Allah's blessings upon him. Then someone said: But something was narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab in the interpretation of Allah Almighty's saying: {And those your oaths bound — so give them their share}, contrary to what you narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas in that,"
"1854","And he mentioned what Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Allah Almighty said: {And to each We made heirs of what parents and near ones left, and those your oaths bound — so give them their share}. Ibn al-Musayyab said: This verse was only revealed for those who adopt men other than their fathers, bequeathing to them; so Allah Almighty revealed therein to make for them a share in bequest, and made inheritance for kin and agnates; and Allah Almighty refused to make for the claimed ones inheritance from one who claimed and adopted them, but made for them a share in bequest in place of what they contracted therein of inheritance, which Allah Almighty returned their matter therein. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that what we narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas in that is in our view worthier for the verse's interpretation, and Allah knows best. Rather in the verse is what points to what Ibn 'Abbas said, and to contrary of who opposed him; for in it is: {And those your right hands bound in covenant}. And alliance therein was oaths, while claiming and adopting had no oaths therein. So it was thereby understood that the interpretation 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas mentioned in this verse is worthier than what another mentioned in its interpretation. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1855","265 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what is done in jest of what frightens the one done to — is it permitted to its doer or forbidden to him? 1620 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad and Abu Umayyah together narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zam'ah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Shihab narrating, from 'Abdullah ibn Wahb ibn Zam'ah, from Umm Salamah, {that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, went out trading to Busra, with Nu'ayman and Suwaybit ibn Harmalah; and Suwaybit was over provisions. So Nu'ayman came to him and said: Feed me. He said: No, till Abu Bakr comes. And Nu'ayman was a laughing jesting man. So he said: I shall surely anger you. So he went to people who brought riding camels, and said: Buy from me an Arab lad, excellent; and he is trembling-tongued — and maybe he will say: I am free; if you will leave him for that, leave him for me — do not spoil my lad for me. They said: Rather we will buy him from you for ten young she-camels. So he came driving them, and came with the people till he hobbled them, then said: Here you are. So the people came and said: We bought you. So Suwaybit said: He lies; I am a free man. They said: He already told us your news. So they threw a rope on his neck and took him and went with him. Then Abu Bakr came, and he and companions with him went and returned the young she-camels and took him. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions around him laughed therefrom for a year}. Then someone said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions' laughing at what Nu'ayman did with Suwaybit mentioned therein, for a year. In that is proof of permitting frightening a Muslim by a Muslim in jest with the like of this. This questioner said: And the like of this is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith:"
"1856","1621 - So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed 'Alqamah ibn Mujazziz al-Mudliji over Khaybar, and sent a detachment, employing over it 'Abdullah ibn Hudhafah al-Sahmi — and he was a man with humor, with a kindled fire before them — so he said to his companions: Is not my obedience upon you obligatory? They said: Yes. He said: Then stand and plunge into this fire. So a man stood to enter it; then he laughed and said: I was only joking. So that reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he laughed and said: Since they did this, do not obey them in disobedience to Allah Almighty}."
"1857","1622 - And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except he said: 'Alqamah ibn Muhazziz with ha'. He said: In this hadith also is the like of what is in the first hadith, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny its doer. In that is what shows permitting the like of it in jest. Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that in neither of these two hadiths is permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to do the like of what is mentioned therein — that anyone do its like to anyone; but in the first of them is only the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions' laughing at that deed for a year. Like what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions would talk of pre-Islamic matters, and his companions would laugh thereat in his presence without his forbidding them that — though those deeds are not permitted them to do their like in Islam."
"1858","1623 - As 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: {I sat with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions more than a hundred times; his companions would recite poetry to one another and mention things of pre-Islamic matter, and he would perhaps smile with them}. And it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding frightening a Muslim:"
"1859","1624 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Let none of you take his companion's goods in play; and when one of you takes his companion's stick, let him return it to him}. Abu Ja'far said: And al-Sa'ib, father of 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib here, is al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid al-Kindi, one of Banu 'Amr ibn Mu'awiyah, an ally among Quraysh — and he is son of al-Namr's sister. Then someone said: What proof of abrogation of one of these two meanings by the other? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that proof of the abrogated of it..."
"1860","1625 - As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Yasar al-Juhani, from Abu Layla al-Ansari, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in some of his campaigns, and some of his companions took a man's quiver and hid it to jest with him. So the man sought it and missed it, and that frightened him; so they laughed at him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and said: What makes you laugh? They said: No, by Allah, except we took so-and-so's quiver to jest with him, and that frightened him — that is what made us laugh. So he said: It is not lawful for a Muslim to frighten a Muslim}. In this hadith is mention of what the mentioned man did of taking his companion's quiver to be frightened by its loss — upon his view that is permitted him — so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him at that: It is not lawful for a Muslim to frighten a Muslim. So his saying that to him after his doing what he did, of what is of the kind of what Nu'ayman did with Suwaybit, and what 'Abdullah ibn Hudhafah did in 'Alqamah al-Mudliji's hadith with his companions to laugh thereat — so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in Abu Layla's hadith to the doer of what he mentioned his doing therein: {It is not lawful for a Muslim to frighten a Muslim} — so that was forbidding from him of the like of that, and abrogation of what preceded it, of what we mentioned in this chapter, of what one attached to of those holding permitting its like — if it was permitted then. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"1861","266 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what is meant by Allah's saying: {To Allah belongs what is in the heavens and what is on earth, and whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it...} the verse. 1626 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Marjanah, he said: It was mentioned to Ibn 'Abbas that Ibn 'Umar recited this verse: {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it}. So he wept, then said: By Allah, if Allah takes us by it we shall surely perish. So Ibn 'Abbas said: May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman; the Muslims found thereof when it came down what he found, so they mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there came down: {Allah does not burden a soul but its capacity; to it what it earned, and upon it what it acquired} — of word and deed; and the soul's inner discourse was of what none owns nor any can."
"1862","1627 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn Marjanah, narrating: while he was sitting with 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, he recited this verse: {To Allah belongs what is in the heavens and what is on earth, and whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it}, the verse. So he said: By Allah, if Allah takes us by this we shall surely perish. Then 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar wept till his sobbing was heard. Ibn Marjanah said: So I stood till I came to 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, and mentioned to him what Ibn 'Umar recited and what he did when he recited it. So Ibn 'Abbas said: May Allah forgive Abu 'Abd al-Rahman; indeed the Muslims found thereof when it was sent down the like of what Ibn 'Umar found, so Allah sent down after it: {Allah does not burden a soul but its capacity; to it what it earned, and upon it what it acquired}, to the end of the surah. So Ibn 'Abbas said: And this whispering was of what Muslims have no strength for, so the matter came to Allah Almighty decreeing that the soul has what it earned and upon it what it acquired, in word and deed. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith was, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn Marjanah narrating — so that cast in hearts that Ibn Shihab may not have narrated it from Ibn Marjanah by hearing. So we looked into that to stand upon the reality therein, if Allah wills."
"1863","1628 - So we found Ahmad ibn Hammad al-Tujibi Abu Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Abu Marwan Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-'Uthmani narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from one who narrated to him, from Sa'id ibn Marjanah, then he mentioned the like of Yunus's hadith here. Abu Ja'far said: We thereby stood upon that Ibn Shihab only narrated this hadith from Ibn Marjanah by conveyance, not by hearing; so thereby this hadith is void, for its chain's voidness. Then we looked: was there narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this cause a hadith besides this hadith:"
"1864","1629 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: {When this verse came down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {To Allah belongs what is in the heavens and what is on earth, and whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it}, the verse — they knelt on knees and said: We cannot bear, we cannot do; we were charged of deeds what we cannot bear nor do. So Allah Almighty sent down: {The Messenger believed in what was sent down to him from his Lord}, to His saying Exalted and Almighty: {And to You is the return}. So they said: {We heard and obeyed; Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the return}. So Allah Almighty sent down: {Allah does not burden a soul but its capacity; to it what it earned, and upon it what it acquired. Our Lord, do not take us if we forgot or erred}. He said: Yes. {And lay not upon us a burden as You laid upon those before us. Our Lord, and burden us not what we have no strength for}, the verse. He said: Yes. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith was better than Ibn Shihab's hadith and sounder in chain. Then we considered it and found therein from the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) companions their saying: We cannot bear, we cannot do; we were charged of deeds what we cannot bear and cannot do. And that was from them in our view, and Allah knows best, upon its falling in their hearts that Allah Almighty informed them by this verse He will take them by their hearts' thoughts, which they cannot nor own of themselves. So He Almighty clarified to them in what He sent down after that, saying: {Allah does not burden a soul but its capacity; to it what it earned..."
"1865","As Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, about this verse: {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it}, the verse: He said: Of testimony. Abu Ja'far said: This interpretation in our view is not sound; and the first interpretation is worthier of the verse; for concealing testimony is of what is not forgiven, for it is a right of the one testified for. And in the verse is what bars that — His saying Almighty: {So He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills}. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1866","267 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about what was from his companions, may Allah be pleased with them, when recited to them: {The Messenger believed in what was sent down to him from his Lord}... to the end of Surat al-Baqarah, and what was from Allah of what He sent down upon His messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therefor, in answer to them. Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Kudaynah narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: When this verse came down: {The Messenger believed in what was sent down to him from his Lord}, to His saying Almighty: {Do not take us if we forgot or erred}: They said the like of that; Allah said: I did. {Our Lord, and lay not upon us a burden as You laid upon those before us}: They said the like of that; He said: I did. {Our Lord, and burden us not what we have no strength for}: Allah said: I did. {And pardon us}, the verse: They said the like of that; Allah said: I did."
"1867","1630 - Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Warqa' narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: {When this verse came down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {The Messenger believed in what was sent down to him from his Lord}: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it; when he said: {Your forgiveness, our Lord}: Allah said: I forgave you. When he said: {Our Lord, do not take us if we forgot or erred}: Allah Almighty said: I shall not take you. When he said: {Our Lord, and lay not upon us a burden as You laid upon those before us}: Allah Almighty said: I shall not lay upon you. He said: When He Almighty said: {And burden us not what we have no strength for}: Allah said: I shall not burden you. When he said: {And pardon us}: Allah said: I pardoned you. When he said: {And forgive us}: He said: I forgave you. When he said: {And have mercy on us}: He said: I had mercy on you. When he said: {So help us against the disbelieving people}: He said: I helped you. Abu Ja'far said: Then someone asked about what is meant by His saying: {Our Lord, do not take us if we forgot or erred}, saying: Forgetting is not of what they own of themselves — so how do they ask not to be taken thereby? Our answer to him in that, by Allah Almighty's guidance and help, is that the forgetting they do not own of themselves is forgetting of things that are opposites of remembering them — so that is of what they are not taken by, and of what their asking their Lord not to take them thereby is not permissible. As for the forgetting mentioned in this verse, it is only abandonment upon deliberateness thereby — like His saying Almighty: {They forgot Allah, so He forgot them}, meaning: They left Allah, so He left them. He said: What is meant by His saying Almighty, relating: {Or we erred}, when error is understood undtaken thereby — as He said: {And there is no sin upon you in what you erred therein, but what your hearts intended}? Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and help, is that the error in the verse he recited to us, in which is no sin, is opposite what they intend — as He said Almighty: {But what your hearts intended}. While the error in the verse we recited to him is the error one who does it does upon his being erring in his choosing it, intending it and acting upon it. From that is said: I erred in such — with hamzah — i.e. I did such as a sin. So that is of what its doer is taken by, punished for — or pardoned for him, if of what pardon for its like is permissible. So by Allah's praise it is clear they, may Allah be pleased with them, asked their Lord Almighty in a place of asking; and that He Almighty forgave them in two things He Almighty had taking them thereby and punishing them thereupon — and He is praised for His bounty therein upon them, and His mercy to them. And Him we ask for guidance."
"1868","268 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his saying: {Allah pardoned for me, for my community, what their souls discourse with, so long as no tongue utters it nor hand does it}. 1631 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yasa' Wahb ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah; and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah — then they joined and said: from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Allah pardoned for my community what their souls discourse with, so long as no tongue utters it nor hand does it}."
"1869","1632 - And 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Faddalah narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Indeed Allah pardoned for my community what their souls discourse with, so long as no hand does nor tongue utters it}."
"1870","1633 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Indeed Allah Almighty pardoned for my community what they whisper and what their souls discourse with, so long as they do not do nor speak it}."
"1871","1634 - And Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Shayban, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: {Indeed Allah Almighty pardoned for my community what their souls discourse with, so long as they do not speak it nor act upon it}."
"1872","1635 - And Ahmad narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam narrated to me, they said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and 'Abd al-Rahman said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — said: {Indeed Allah Almighty pardoned for my community everything their souls discourse with, so long as they do not speak nor act}."
"1873","1636 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Indeed Allah Almighty pardoned for me, for my community, what their souls discourse with, so long as they do not act upon it nor speak it}. Abu Ja'far said: I heard Ibn Abi Dawud say: We know for al-A'mash from al-A'raj no hadith but this hadith, and none narrates it from him but Jarir. Abu Ja'far said: And what all these who narrated this hadith to us narrated it upon is: What their souls discourse with — with accusative — so that was upon the meaning of their souls' discoursing therewith. While people of language oppose them therein, mentioning that it is: What their souls discourse — with nominative — and that their souls discourse therewith without their choosing it nor fetching it from them. They said: And of what points to that is Allah Almighty's saying: {And We created man, and know what his soul whispers to him with, and We are nearer to him than the jugular vein}. They said: And of what points to that, they mentioned:"
"1874","1637 - What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn 'Utham narrated to us, he said: Su'ayr ibn al-Khums narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullah, he said: {A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I discourse with myself of a thing; falling from heaven is dearer to me than speaking it. So he said: That is pure faith — or he said: clear faith}."
"1875","1638 - And they mentioned what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Dharr, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, from Ibn 'Abbas, {that the Prophet's companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us discourses with himself of a thing; being charred is dearer to him than speaking it. So he said: Praise be to Allah who empowered only whispering from you}."
"1876","Shu'bah said: And Sulayman narrated to us with this chain, saying in his hadith: Praise be to Allah who returned his matter to whispering."
"1877","1639 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur and Sulayman, from Dharr, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: {O Messenger of Allah, we used to discourse with ourselves of a thing: being charred is dearer to us than speaking it. So one said: Praise be to Allah who empowered only whispering from you. And the other said: Praise be to Allah who returned his matter to whispering}."
"1878","1640 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Dharr, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: {A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I discourse with myself of a thing, and being charred is dearer to me than speaking it. So he said: Allah is greatest; praise be to Allah who returned his matter to whispering}. They said: And this hadith, even if said therein: One of us discourses with himself — and it is of what Ibn Mas'ud mentioned from him: that is clear faith or pure faith, or for your guarding saying that with your tongues, so you are taken thereby — so your guarding that and barring yourselves from it was faith. And what Ibn 'Abbas mentioned from him, and it is: Praise be to Allah who empowered only whispering over you, or: Praise be to Allah who returned his matter to the whispering you are not taken by — rather rewarded for your guarding releasing it. They said: And this hadith, even if said therein: One of us is discoursed by his soul, or: We discourse with ourselves — then the Messenger of Allah's answer (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them therein is relied upon and intended thereby, and it is: {Praise be to Allah who empowered only whispering from you} — or: Praise be to Allah who returned his matter to whispering — so that returns to their souls' whispering them with what it whispers them. Abu Ja'far said: We considered this hadith ourselves — does it bear contrary to what people of language said therein, of what agrees what those we took it from narrated to us, of what returns to: What their souls discourse with — with accusative — or not? We found therein mention of Allah Almighty's pardoning to His prophet in his community what He pardoned them thereof. And pardoning is only from what, had He not pardoned it, they would have been punished therefor. And that is of what we understood: it is not of the excused thoughts, but only of the things fetched by worrying therewith. So its aspect in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon what one intends of sins to do them — so Allah pardoned to His prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for them, that; so He did not take them thereby nor punish them therefor. And of that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"1879","1641 - Of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zinad narrated to us, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah Almighty said: When My servant intends a good deed and does not do it, write it a good deed; if he does it, write it ten; and when My servant intends an evil deed and does not do it, do not write it; if he does it, write it with its like; and if he leaves it, write it a good deed. Abu Ja'far said: I heard Yunus say: Then I read this hadith to Sufyan, after he narrated it to us, and he added to me in the good deed: {So write it to seven hundred multiples}, and added to me in the evil deed: {If he leaves it from fear of Me}. So thereby what people of language claimed against the narrators in this hadith, of what we mentioned with them, is negated; and the hadith returns to: What their souls discourse with — with accusative — as they conveyed it to us, not with nominative. And Allah Almighty we ask for guidance."
"1880","269 - Chapter explaining the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the wrestler — who is he of men. 1642 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn 'Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {What do you count the wrestler among you? He said: We said: One whom men do not throw down. He said: Not that; but one who controls himself at anger}."
"1881","1643 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The strong is not by wrestling; the strong is only one who controls himself at anger}."
"1882","1644 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he informed him that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {The strong is not by wrestling}. They said: Who is the strong, O Messenger of Allah? He said: One who controls himself at anger}."
"1883","1645 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The strong is not one who overcomes people, but the strong is one who overcomes himself}. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that the wrestler deserving this name is one who controls himself at anger, so throws it thereby from what its desire calls it to. That is not in our view, and Allah knows best, expulsion of the strong over his companion till he throws him from being a wrestler — since one who controls himself and throws it from what it wants of its desire is above that. So he deserved to be the wrestler, even if others of those we mentioned are wrestlers also. And the like of this is the Prophet's saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {The needy is not the roaming one whom a date or two dates, a morsel or two morsels, turn back. They said: Who is the needy, O Messenger of Allah? He said: One who does not ask people, nor is known so charity be given him}. And we shall mention that later in this book of ours in its place thereof, if Allah wills. And his saying: {The needy is not the roaming one} was not expulsion from him of one who asks upon need from being needy, but he is not in the highest ranks of neediness. Likewise his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith we narrated, that the wrestler whom men do not throw is not the wrestler — since among the two wrestlers is one above him, and he is one who controls himself at its anger and throws it from its desire to what is worthier of it thereof. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant thereby, and Him we ask for guidance."
"1884","270 - Chapter of proof from Allah's Book, then from the Messenger of Allah's practice (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), against one who dislikes a man asking Allah Almighty to give him charity of a thing he mentions. Abu Ja'far said: People disliked a man saying in his supplication: O Allah, give me charity of such. And they narrated that from Abu Wa'il Shaqiq ibn Salamah. As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us; and as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, they said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu Wa'il, that he disliked a man saying: O Allah, give me charity of Paradise, saying: Only one hoping reward gives charity. Of proof against them, for others besides them who permitted that, from Allah Almighty's Book, is what Allah Almighty related therein from His prophet Zakariyya in his supplicating Him: {Grant me from Yours good offspring}. And what was of Allah's answering him therein, from His saying: {So We answered him and granted him Yahya}. And the like of that His saying Almighty in the story of His prophet Ayyub (peace be upon him): {And We granted him his family and the like of them with them}. And since granting from Allah to whom He wills of His servants is permitted, supplicating Him thereby is permitted; and granting from the sons of Adam may be for seeking reward thereupon — so charity from Him Almighty, for which reward from Him is not proper for the sons of Adam, is more permitted. And in that is what widens for people to call upon Him Almighty thereby. As for from the Messenger of Allah's practice (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"1885","1646 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Ammar narrating, from 'Abdullah ibn Babah, from Ya'la ibn Munabbih, he said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: Allah Almighty only said: {There is no sin upon you to shorten of prayer, if you fear those who disbelieved may persecute you}. So people are safe. So 'Umar said: I wondered at what you wondered at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: {Charity Allah gave you charity of, so accept His charity}."
"1886","1647 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Ammar, from Abd Allah ibn Babayh, from Yala ibn Munayyah, who said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Allah's lightening upon His servants a charity from Him upon them, and in that is proof of what we mentioned, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for success."
"1887","Chapter 271 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: There were among the nations before you inspired men (muhaddathun); if there is any among my ummah, it is Umar ibn al-Khattab. 1648 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me. And Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan. And Harun ibn Kamil ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: There used to be among the nations before you inspired men (muhaddathun); if there is any among my ummah, it is Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"1888","1649 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ayyub narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"1889","1650 - And Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: my uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There were among those before you of the nations men who were inspired (yuhaddathun); if there is any among my ummah, it is Umar ibn al-Khattab. Ibrahim ibn Sad said: and they are those who are inspired (yulhamun)."
"1890","1651 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except for what is in it of the saying of Ibrahim ibn Sad: they are those who are inspired."
"1891","1652 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, from his father Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of Abu Dawud. Abu Jafar said: So Ibrahim ibn Sad and Muhammad ibn Ajlan differed over Sad ibn Ibrahim as to who this hadith goes back to after Abu Salamah, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - from Aishah and from Abu Hurayrah - as we mentioned of their disagreement from him in that. We examined this hadith to determine what is meant by it, Allah willing. The meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) muhaddathun is: inspired ones (mulhamun), and likewise yuhaddathun means: they are inspired until their tongues speak wisdom, as the tongue of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to speak of it. Of that is what we mentioned from him in the hadith of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ila from his wives, when he said to them: You shall desist from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or Allah the Mighty and Majestic shall replace for him wives better than you, as Allah mentioned in the verse He sent down about that, and Allah sent down after that upon His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) His saying: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you - the verse - in agreement with what he had said to them before that."
"1892","And what was reported from Anas ibn Malik from him of his saying: My Lord agreed with me in three, or: I agreed with my Lord in three. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: My Lord agreed with me in three - or: I agreed with my Lord in three. He said: I said, O Messenger of Allah, if only you took from the Station of Ibrahim a place of prayer, so Allah sent down: And take from the Station of Ibrahim a place of prayer. And I said: the righteous and the wicked enter upon you, so if only you veiled the Mothers of the Believers, so Allah sent down the verse of the veil. And something of the reproach of the Mothers of the Believers reached me, so I went around them saying: You shall stop from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or Allah shall replace for him wives better than you. I ended at one of the Mothers of the Believers, and she said: O Umar, is there not in the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what admonishes his wives so that you admonish them? So Allah sent down: Perhaps his Lord, if he divorces you, will replace for him wives better than you."
"1893","And it was reported from Abd Allah ibn Abbas in confirmation of our interpretation of the first hadith we mentioned in this chapter what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr - he is Ibn Dinar - from Ibn Abbas, that he used to read it: And We did not send before you any messenger nor prophet nor inspired one (muhaddath). Abu Jafar said: So the muhaddath in this is of the kind whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. An objector said: Is it permitted to say of these inspired ones that Allah sent them, as Ibn Abbas read the verse accordingly in this hadith of his? Our answer to him, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that the message mentioned in this verse intends thereby the prophets and messengers, blessings of Allah upon them, not the inspired ones mentioned with them. He said: How can that be when they are mentioned with them by what is at the beginning of the verse - which is the message? Our answer to him in that, according to what the people of Arabic held therein, is that they were joined with them by writing in the verse, as if intended: And We did not send before you any messenger nor prophet, nor did We inspire of an inspired one, except that when he wished, Satan cast into his wish. And they used to cite in that a verse of poetry: and the sword - of what is worn suspended, and the spear is not so, it is only carried - and the figure of speech (kinayah) was used in that, so it became as if he said: one wearing a sword suspended, and carrying a spear. And Allah knows best the reality of that, and Him we ask for success."
"1894","Chapter 272 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Which of you is the wealth of his heir more beloved to him than his own wealth. 1653 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, he said: Masud ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim - he is al-Taymi - from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1895","1654 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim - meaning al-Taymi - narrated to me, from al-Harith, who said: Abd Allah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of you is the wealth of his heir more beloved to him than his own wealth? They said: O Messenger of Allah, there is none of us but his own wealth is more beloved to him. He said: For his wealth is what he sent forth, and the wealth of his heir is what he left behind."
"1896","1655 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, who said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of you is his wealth more beloved to him than the wealth of his heir? They said: O Messenger of Allah, there is none of us but his wealth is more beloved to him than the wealth of his heir. He said: Know what you say. They said: We know nothing but that, O Messenger of Allah. He said: There is none of you but the wealth of his heir is more beloved to him. They said: How, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The wealth of one of you is only what he sent forth, and the wealth of his heir is what he left behind. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is that what a man leaves behind of his wealth and does not send forth for Allah the Mighty and Majestic in what would be reward for him with Him and nearness to Him is not of his wealth - not that it is not his wealth, as the wealth of others is not his wealth - but in our view, and Allah knows best, it is not of his wealth which is the highest of his wealths in benefit to him, since what he sent forth of his wealth benefits him in his Hereafter, and what he did not send forth does not benefit him therein. So it is permitted thereby to say of it that it is not of his wealth, and permitted thereby to ascribe to the one who obtains it after his death, in good, to the best of his wealths for him - which is what obtains for him reward with his Lord and nearness to Him - and what his heir may send forth, so it is for him nearness with Allah the Mighty and Majestic, so it is his wealth which is the highest ranks of his wealths in benefits for him in his return. And of what enters into this meaning also is what was reported from Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1897","1656 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah, from his father, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was reciting: Rivalry has distracted you. So he said: The son of Adam says: my wealth, my wealth, and what is yours of your wealth except what you gave in charity and sent forth, or ate and consumed, or wore and wore out."
"1898","1657 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from his father, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1899","1658 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, from Hammam, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from his father, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So that is upon that what returns of his wealth to other than him after his death is not wealth for him, since there is no benefit for him in it then, as there is no benefit for him in the wealth of another. And we seek refuge with Allah from that, and Him we ask for success."
"1900","Chapter - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was of his saying while Abu Hurayrah was present: Which of you spread his garment, then took from this hadith of mine, for he will not forget anything he heard - and that Abu Hurayrah did that, and did not forget after that anything he heard. 1659 - Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: Ibn al-Musayyib said: Abu Hurayrah said: They say: this Abu Hurayrah has narrated much, and Allah is the appointed one. And they say: what is with the Muhajirun and the Ansar that they do not narrate like his hadiths? I shall inform you about that: my brothers among the Ansar were occupied by work on their lands, and my brothers among the Muhajirun were occupied by their trading in the markets, and I used to stay with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the fill of my stomach, so I witnessed when they were absent and memorized when they forgot. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Which of you spread his garment, so he takes from this hadith of mine then gathers it to his chest, for he will not forget anything he heard. So I spread a cloak over me until he finished his hadith, then I gathered them to my chest, and I did not forget after that day anything he narrated to me. And were it not for two verses Allah sent down in His Book, I would never have narrated anything: Those who conceal what We sent down of the clear proofs and the guidance - to the end of the two verses. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is of the speech of Abu Hurayrah: I did not forget after that day anything he narrated to me - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). An objector said: We found him narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something, then forgetting it after that, and he mentioned."
"1901","1660 - What Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Salamah narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion (adwa). And he narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No sick one should bring to a healthy one. Abu Salamah said: Abu Hurayrah used to narrate both from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Abu Hurayrah fell silent after that from his saying There is no contagion, and kept to No sick one should bring to a healthy one. Al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab - cousin of Abu Hurayrah - said: I used to hear you, O Abu Hurayrah, narrating with this hadith another hadith you have fallen silent about; you say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion. Abu Hurayrah refused that, and said: No sick one should bring to a healthy one. Al-Harith did not see him after that until Abu Hurayrah became angry and spoke in Abyssinian, and said to al-Harith: Do you know what I said? He said: No. Abu Hurayrah said: I said: I refuse (abaytu). Abu Salamah said: By my life, Abu Hurayrah used to narrate to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said There is no contagion, so we do not know whether Abu Hurayrah forgot, or one of the two sayings abrogated the other."
"1902","1661 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi al-Bahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Sinan ibn Abi Sinan al-Duali informed me, that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion. A bedouin stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, what of camels in the sands like gazelles, then a mangy camel comes to them and all get mange? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Then who infected the first? Abu Salamah said: And I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: No sick one should bring to a healthy one. Al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab al-Dawsi said to him: You used to narrate to us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion. Abu Hurayrah denied that. Al-Harith said: Indeed. So he and Abu Hurayrah disputed until their matter intensified, then he mentioned the rest of the first hadith. Abu Jafar said: Our answer in that is that this hadith mentioned - Abu Hurayrah's forgetting of it in this hadith of al-Zuhri - may be of what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before there was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the matter in the hadith of Ibn al-Musayyib from him. This is the most fitting construction of both hadiths together, so there is in nothing of them contradiction or disagreement, nor breach of the promise of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor contradiction in his saying. This objector said: It was also reported from Abu Hurayrah his forgetting of another thing close to his hearing it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned."
"1903","1662 - What Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, from Khazim ibn Khuzaymah of Taym al-Ribab, from Mujahid al-Makki, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were guarding the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his campaigns one night. Abu Jafar said: And there fell, as I think, from Salih: so I came. Then he mentioned the rest which will come connected: one night to the place in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be lying down, and I did not find the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his bed, so I knew prayer had made him get up. I looked right and left, and behold the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at the tree praying. I hastened toward him, and there was a man whom the like of what brought me out had brought out. I and he stood behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), praying with his prayer as long as we wished, until in the midst of his prayer he prostrated a prostration so I thought he had been taken in it. We hastened to him and sat before him, I and my companion. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked us and we asked him. Then he said: Did you find anything amiss in my prayer tonight? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, you prostrated in the midst of your prayer a prostration so we thought you had been taken in it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given in it five which no prophet before me was given: I was sent to all people, red and black, while the prophet before me was sent to his household or village folk; I was aided against my enemy with terror a month before me and a month behind me; spoils and fifths were made lawful for me, not lawful for any prophet before me - they were taken and placed, then a white fire from heaven descended and burned them; the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, I pray wherever prayer overtakes me; and I was given then a supplication, so I stored it as intercession for my ummah on the Day of Resurrection. Mujahid said: Abu Hurayrah said, and my companion said to me - and he was better than me: You forgot the best of it - the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): and I hope it will reach of my ummah whoever does not associate anything with Allah. Abu Hurayrah mentioned his companion was Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him. In this hadith is Abu Dharr informing Abu Hurayrah of his forgetting what he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) close to hearing it. Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is this may have been from Abu Hurayrah before there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is in the hadith of Ibn al-Musayyib. Then we examined Abu Hurayrah's hadith in this story: did other than Said ibn al-Musayyib narrate it, contradicting or agreeing? Did al-Araj contradict or agree?"
"1904","1663 - So we found al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, that Abu Hurayrah said: They say: Abu Hurayrah narrates much, and Allah is the appointed one. They say: what is with the Muhajirun that they do not narrate like his hadith, and the Ansar that they do not narrate like his hadiths? I inform you: my brothers among the Muhajirun were occupied by trading in the markets, my brothers among the Ansar by work on their properties, and I was a poor man staying with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the fill of my stomach; I attended when they were absent and retained when they forgot. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: If one of you spreads his garment until I finish this speech of mine, then gathers it to his chest, he will never forget anything of my speech. Abu Hurayrah said: So I spread a namirah, with no garment on me but it, until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his speech, then I gathered it to my chest. By the One who sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the truth, I did not forget of that speech a word to this day. By Allah, were it not for two verses Allah sent down in His Book, I would never narrate anything: Those who conceal what We sent down of the clear proofs and the guidance. So we came thereby upon al-Araj's contradiction of Said ibn al-Musayyib from Abu Hurayrah in this hadith, and upon Said's narration of it upon unrestricted negation of forgetting from Abu Hurayrah what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after what was, and upon al-Araj's narration that that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only in the speech in that gathering, not before or after. Allah knows best the reality. A people argued for confirming al-Araj against Said ibn al-Musayyib in what they differed in from Abu Hurayrah, of what others narrated from him."
"1905","1664 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: - meaning Abd al-Rahman ibn Salman - informed me, from Uqayl, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim, that he heard from Abu Hurayrah."
"1906","1665 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim and Mujahid, that they heard Abu Hurayrah say: No one was more retentive of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than me, except Abd Allah ibn Amr; for I retained with my heart, and he retained with his heart and wrote with his hand: he sought permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, so he permitted him."
"1907","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from his brother, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: None of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrates more from him than me, except Abd Allah ibn Amr; for he used to write and I did not write. They said: So it was reasonable that what Abu Hurayrah was distinguished with of what he took from the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only his memorization, not else, and what Abd Allah ibn Amr was distinguished with is his memorization and writing. The exertion of Abd Allah ibn Amr was memorization with heart and writing with hand, while Abu Hurayrah's was taking with heart without writing. What Abd Allah ibn Amr exerted was harder than what Abu Hurayrah exerted. So Abu Hurayrah - had he forgotten nothing he heard - ought to have been more prolific and retentive than Abd Allah ibn Amr. When the matter was contrary, and Abd Allah ibn Amr was the more prolific, judgment was due for al-Araj against Ibn al-Musayyib in what they differed in from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. What was with Abu Hurayrah from which forgetting was negated is what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that single occasion, not before or after. And Allah we ask for success."
"1908","Chapter 274 - Explanation of the difficulty of the proof against one who disliked saying: O Allah, free me from the Fire - from the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: A people disliked a man saying: O Allah, free me from the Fire, saying freeing is only ascribed to one for whom reward is hoped, reported from Abu Wail. As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, who said: Abu Wail disliked a man saying: O Allah, free me from the Fire, saying only one who hopes for reward frees. They said: Allah is exalted above that. Others opposed them and saw no harm in it. Of the proof for them was the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we already reported: Whoever frees a slave, Allah frees with every limb of it a limb of him from the Fire. In that is the ascription of freeing from the Fire to Allah, and in his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting that is release for Muslims to supplicate Him with it. And Allah we ask for success."
"1909","Chapter 275 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, or from Abu Dharr, of what we know he only took from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the two intended by Allah's saying: These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord - to: and they are guided to the path of the Praiseworthy. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Concerning us this verse was sent down, concerning the duelists of Badr: These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord, but those who disbelieved, garments of fire will be cut for them."
"1910","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: Hamzah and Ali and Ubaydah ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with them, duelled with Utbah ibn Rabiah and Shaybah ibn Rabiah and al-Walid ibn Utbah, so there was sent down concerning them: These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord."
"1911","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: I heard Abu Dharr swearing by Allah an oath: this verse was sent down concerning six of Quraysh: Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ubaydah ibn al-Harith, Utbah ibn Rabiah, Shaybah ibn Rabiah and al-Walid ibn Utbah: These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord, but those who disbelieved, garments of fire will be cut for them - the verse, and the other verse: Indeed Allah will admit those who believed and did righteous deeds into gardens beneath which rivers flow."
"1912","And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym ibn Bashir narrated to us, he said: Abu Hashim informed us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, who said: I heard Abu Dharr swearing by Allah that this verse: These are two disputants who disputed concerning their Lord - was sent down concerning those who came forth on Badr, the three and the three: Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn al-Muttalib, and Utbah, Shaybah ibn Rabiah and al-Walid ibn Utbah."
"1913","And Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn Abbad, the like, except he did not mention Abu Dharr. Abu Jafar said: We examined these two verses, and found Allah's saying: they disputed - in the plural above two, as Arabs say: the two armies met and some killed others. We found the disbelievers named: Shaybah and Utbah sons of Rabiah, and al-Walid ibn Utbah. We found the believers named: Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn al-Muttalib, upon them peace. What Allah threatened the disbelievers with will befall them. What He promised the believers will certainly be, for Allah does not break the appointment; abrogation does not attach to it, for abrogation only attaches to laws, making unlawful lawful and vice versa, not to what He informed He will do as reward for a deed. These are the states of the two parties in the Hereafter. Then we found Him follow His promise to the believers with: And they are guided to the good of speech and guided to the path of the Praiseworthy - information of their states in this world, may Allah be pleased with them, praiseworthy states with no blame. We found His saying And they are guided, per linguists, meaning: they are made firm, like Guide us to the straight path - make us firm upon it. Whoever's states in this world are these, and in the Hereafter those mentioned, is of the highest stations in both. And with Allah is success."
"1914","Chapter 276 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the merit of dutifulness to the mother over the father. 1666 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid al-Sakuni narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Shubrumah narrated to us, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are most deserving of my good companionship? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your mother - three times. He said: Then whom? He said: Then your father."
"1915","1667 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I said: O Prophet of Allah, whom shall I honor? He said: Your mother. I said, then whom? He said: Then your mother. I said, then whom? He said: Then your mother - three times - then your father, the nearest then the nearest."
"1916","1668 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us. And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"1917","1669 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi and al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Jizi al-Kufi narrated to us, they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Ali ibn Urfutah, from Khidash Abu Salamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I enjoin a man with his mother, I enjoin a man with his mother, I enjoin a man with his mother - three times - I enjoin a man with his father, I enjoin a man with his client who is next to him, even if there is from him upon him harm. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is what proves the mother has upon her child three times the dutifulness of the father. An objector said: There was reported from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this."
"1918","1670 - He mentioned what Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn al-Qaqa narrated to us, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Who of people is most deserving of good companionship from me? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your father. Sufyan said: So they hold the mother has two-thirds of dutifulness."
"1919","I heard al-Saqati say: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: And likewise al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: For the mother two-thirds of dutifulness, and for the father one-third."
"1920","1671 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn al-Qaqa narrated to us, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, who is most deserving of good companionship from me? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your mother. He said: Then whom? He said: Your father. He said: So they hold the mother has two-thirds of dutifulness, and the father one-third. It was said to Sufyan: The mother two-thirds in the hadith? He said: I heard it from Ibn Shubrumah narrating it from Umarah before I saw him, then I asked Umarah and he brought it. Abu Jafar said: Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is Ibn Uyaynah may have lost what Shuja memorized, for Ibn Uyaynah narrated from memory while Shuja narrated from his book - though Ibn Uyaynah added Umarah ibn al-Qaqa between Ibn Shubrumah and Abu Zurah. The first for us, with this disagreement from Abu Hurayrah on the mother's dutifulness, is what Muawiyah ibn Haydah, grandfather of Bahz ibn Hakim, and Khidash Abu Salamah agreed with him upon from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not what contradicted them. So the mother's due upon her child of dutifulness and companionship is three times the father's. And Allah we ask for success. Abu Jafar said: Then we examined Abu Zurah's hadith at the chapter's start, and whether anyone agreed with Shuja against Ibn Uyaynah."
"1921","1672 - So we found Abu Ayyub Ubayd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Imran al-Tabarani, known as Ibn Khalaf, narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Nasr al-Matbakhi narrated to us, he said: Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, from Umarah ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are most deserving of good companionship? He said: Your mother. I said, then whom? He said: Your mother. I said, then whom, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Your mother. I said, then whom, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Your father. Abu Jafar said: This Hibban agreed with Shuja, and Hibban is sound in hadith. Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to me, he said: Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, he said: I said to Yahya ibn Main: Hibban ought to be the more trustworthy of the two - him and Mindal. He said: How close they are. Then we found Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Kufi al-Bajali narrated this hadith from Abu Zurah, agreeing with Shuja and contradicting Ibn Uyaynah."
"1922","1673 - As Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Bajali narrated to us, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What do you command me? He said: Honor your mother. Then he returned and said: Honor your mother. Then he returned and said: Honor your mother. Then he returned the fourth time and said: Honor your father. Then we looked into Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Bajali's standing with the imams. Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Main say: Waki and Abu Nuaym narrate from Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Bajali, and there is no harm in him. So Abu Hurayrah's hadith with Ibn Uyaynah and Shuja's disagreement returned to Shuja's version being first, by corroboration of those we mentioned. And Allah we ask for success."
"1923","Chapter 277 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he vomited and broke his fast. 1674 - Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Hisham - meaning al-Dastuwai - narrated to us, from Yahya - meaning Ibn Abi Kathir - from a man, from Yaish ibn al-Walid ibn Hisham, from Madan, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke his fast. He said: So I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus - so I mentioned that to him - and he said: He spoke the truth, and I poured his ablution water. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is Hisham's silence from naming the man whom Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated it from, and he is Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awzai."
"1924","1675 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith al-Tannuri narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awzai, from Yaish ibn al-Walid, from his father, from Madan ibn Abi Talhah, from Abu al-Darda, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke his fast. He said: So I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus, and he said: He spoke the truth; I poured his ablution water."
"1925","1676 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awzai, from Yaish ibn al-Walid ibn Hisham, from Madan ibn Talhah, from Abu al-Darda, then he mentioned the like. I heard Ibn Abi Dawud say: Abu Mamar said thus: Abd al-Warith said Abd Allah ibn Amr, and the correct is Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr. Abu Jafar said: Ibn Abi Dawud did not mention the father of Yaish ibn al-Walid, and said Madan ibn Talhah; thus the Iraqis say in this man's lineage, while the Syrians say Madan ibn Abi Talhah, and they know him better, for he is of them and Yamuri, and he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1926","1677 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Judi narrated to us, from Balj - a man of Mahrah - from Abu Shaybah al-Mahri, who said: I said to Thawban: Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomit and break his fast."
"1927","1678 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us. And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us. They all said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a drink, and one of us said: Did you not wake fasting, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Indeed, but I vomited."
"1928","1679 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, he said: Abu Marzuq narrated to me, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, then he mentioned the like. An objector said: This is contrary to all scholars' hadith, for none disagree one overcome by vomiting has not broken fast. Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is these reports do not intend what he imagined, for the speech is Arabic speech with figures for the addressees' understanding. The meaning of vomited and broke fast is: he vomited and became weak, so broke fast - weakness expressed by figure, like the verse of oath expiations: That is the expiation of your oaths when you swear - meaning when you swear and violate, for none disagree one who swore and did not violate has no expiation; expiation is due by violation, not swearing. Likewise Fadalah's hadith: but I vomited - but I vomited and became weak. What Abu Hurayrah narrated from the Messenger of Allah clarifying vomiting's ruling in fasting proves it."
"1929","1680 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is overcome by vomiting while fasting has no making up upon him, and whoever deliberately vomits, let him make up. So all we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter agreed, by Allah's praise, and did not differ. And with Allah is success."
"1930","Chapter 278 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the threat against intercession in the hudud of Allah the Mighty and Majestic. 1681 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a woman stole in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ordered her cut. Usamah ibn Zayd spoke to him concerning her, so the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color, and he said: Do you intercede in a hadd of Allah's hudud? Usamah said: Seek forgiveness for me, O Messenger of Allah. In the evening the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah as He deserves, then said: To proceed, those before you were destroyed only because when the noble among them stole they left him, and when the weak stole they established the hadd upon him. By the One in whose hand is my soul, had Fatimah daughter of Muhammad stolen, I would cut her hand. Then he ordered that woman who stole, and her hand was cut."
"1931","1682 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Quraysh were concerned by the Makhzumiyyah who stole, and said: Who will speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning her? They said: Who dares but Usamah? Then he mentioned the prior hadith's meaning. An objector said: You reported from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, that he interceded for a thief - contrary to this hadith. Al-Zubayr did that only after knowing its permissibility, which cannot be by opinion but by instruction (tawqif), and that is only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1932","And he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed us, from his brother Abd Allah ibn Urwah, from al-Furafisah, that al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by a thief who had stolen, and said: Leave him, pardon him. They said: Do you command this, O Abu Abd Allah, while Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Al-Zubayr said: Hudud are pardoned so long as not raised to the ruler; when raised to the ruler, may Allah not pardon him if he pardons him."
"1933","And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abd Allah ibn Urwah, from Furafisah al-Hanafi, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, that they passed by him with a thief, and he said: Release him. They said: Do you command that? He said: Yes, so long as not raised to the imam; when raised to the imam, may Allah not pardon him if he pardons him. Abu Jafar said: Al-Zubayr clarified the place of the threatened intercession in the first hadith - intercession for what reached the imam - while intercession before reaching the imam is otherwise, with no threat. Such a statement admits no opinion; it is only by instruction from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah we ask for success. We shall mention later his saying to Safwan ibn Umayyah about the thief who stole his khamisah: Why not before you brought him to me? Allah willing."
"1934","Chapter 278 - Explanation of the difficulty of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. 1683 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: my uncle Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed me, he said: I heard Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan delivering a sermon say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. I am only a distributor, and Allah gives. This ummah will ever stand upon Allah's command; opposers will not harm them until Allah's command comes while dominant over people."
"1935","1684 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Yazid ibn Ziyad - Abu Jafar said: of Banu Qurayzah - from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, who said: Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan said upon the minbar: O people, none prevents what Allah gave, none gives what He withheld, and no fortune avails against Him. Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. Then he said: I heard these words from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon these timbers."
"1936","1685 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim al-Ansari, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, who said: Muawiyah said in his pilgrimage: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon these timbers: O Allah, none prevents what You gave, none gives what You withheld. Whomever Allah intends good, He gives him understanding in religion."
"1937","1686 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Jarad - a man of Tamim - from Raja ibn Haywah, from Muawiyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. Abu Jafar said: Al-Bukhari mentioned this Jarad, saying: he is Jarad ibn Mujalid; Shubah and Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated from him."
"1938","1687 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, saying: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us - Yazid adding Hibban ibn Hilal, Ibrahim instead Yahya ibn Hammad - they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Mabad al-Juhani, from Muawiyah, that he hardly narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), yet hardly omitted these words on Fridays, narrating the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. This wealth is sweet and green; whoever takes it rightfully, Allah blesses him in it. Beware excessive praise, for it is slaughter. Abu Jafar said: Al-Bukhari mentioned this Mabad, saying: he first spoke of qadar in Basrah; some say Mabad ibn Abd Allah ibn Uwaymir, some Mabad ibn Khalid. Al-Bukhari said: this shows he is not of Al Sabrah at al-Marwah who accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1939","1688 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Rashid ibn Abi Sakanah narrated to him that he heard Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan upon the minbar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion."
"1940","1689 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Baghdadi al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Atiyyah, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Muawiyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion."
"1941","1690 - And Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ibn Ajlan, from Yazid ibn Ziyad, then he mentioned the like of Yunus's hadith from Malik in this chapter, in chain and text."
"1942","1691 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion. I am only a distributor, and Allah gives. Abu Jafar said: We already mentioned in this book on the intended fiqh in his saying: Many a carrier of fiqh has no fiqh, and many a carrier conveys it to one more understanding - sufficing us from repeating here, since of like kind. Of what we mentioned: fiqh is understanding (fahm). We found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirming that."
"1943","1692 - Which is what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Abbad ibn Salim narrated to him, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He makes him understand (yufahhimuhu). Abu Jafar said: So we understood yufaqqihuhu means yufahhimuhu, as reported in this chapter. Yet we mentioned in the chapter on his saying: Many a carrier of fiqh - that not every understood thing is at the level of fiqh of religion's matter, which is a degree above every other understanding, as mentioned there. And Allah we ask for success."
"1944","Chapter 280 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from Imran ibn Husayn on the prayer the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him when he had a fistula (nasur), and on the sitting prayer - its measure of the standing, and the lying (reclining) prayer - its measure of the sitting. 1693 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Husayn al-Muallim, from Ibn Buraydah, from Imran ibn Husayn, who said: I had a fistula, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prayer, and he said: Pray standing; if unable, sitting; if unable, on your side."
"1945","1694 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Muallim narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, who said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man's prayer sitting, and he said: Whoever prays standing is best; whoever prays sitting has half the reward of the standing; whoever prays lying has half the reward of the sitting. Abu Jafar said: A people held Imran's hadith confused due to Ibrahim ibn Tahman and Isa ibn Yunus differing from Husayn al-Muallim. Not so with us; they are two different hadiths: Ibrahim's is the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer on how to pray, and Isa's is his information of the voluntary sitting prayer's measure against the standing - in our view for one who prays voluntary sitting while able to stand, so has half what standing gives; not for one sitting unable to stand - his sitting prayer in reward is as standing, for he aimed at standing but fell short, deserving standing's reward; if able yet sat by choice, he left standing and earns only sitting's reward. Then on his saying Whoever prays lying has half the sitting's reward: we found a sitting one able to bow and prostrate in sitting may not pray lying on his side. So intended is not one who prays lying while able to sit with bowing and prostration; rather one sitting who can only gesture for them may pray on his side gesturing. So the lying one earning half the sitting's is one able to sit gesturing yet prayed lying gesturing by choice, deserving half the sitting's reward. And Allah we ask for success."
"1946","Chapter 281 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the thigh - is it awrah or not. 1695 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, from Ata ibn Yasar, Sulayman ibn Yasar and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was reclining in his house, uncovering his thighs. Abu Bakr sought permission and he permitted him in that state, then Umar sought permission and he permitted him likewise, and he conversed. Then Uthman sought permission, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat up and straightened his clothes - Muhammad said: I do not say that in one day - so he entered and conversed. When he left, Aishah said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr entered and you showed no concern, then Umar entered likewise, then Uthman entered and you sat up and straightened your clothes? He said: Shall I not be shy of one of whom the angels are shy? Abu Jafar said: In this is proof the thigh is not awrah. Likewise reported."
"1947","1696 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Muslim, keeper of al-Maqsurah, narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered a garden of the Ansar with a well in it; he sat at its head, dangled his legs, part of his thigh uncovered, and ordered me to sit at the gate. Soon Abu Bakr came; I informed him, and he said: Permit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. He entered, praised Allah, and did as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. Then Umar came likewise, then Ali came - permit him and glad tidings - and did as his companions. Then Uthman came; he said: Permit him and glad tidings of Paradise. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he covered his thigh. They said: Why, O Messenger of Allah, did you cover your thigh when Uthman came? He said: I am shy of one of whom the angels are shy. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith too is like the prior. Yet reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the thigh is awrah."
"1948","1697 - As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thigh is awrah."
"1949","1698 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Yahya, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, saw a man's thigh, and said: A man's thigh is of his awrah."
"1950","1699 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah informed me, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Jahsh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Mamar in the mosque courtyard uncovering part of his thigh, and said to him: Cover your thigh, O Mamar; the two thighs are awrah."
"1951","1700 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-Ala, from Abu Kathir, freedman of Muhammad ibn Jahsh, from Muhammad ibn Jahsh, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1952","1701 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim ibn Jarhad, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man's thigh is of his awrah - or said: of the awrah."
"1953","1702 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hasan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abd Allah ibn Jarhad al-Aslami, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1954","1703 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Abu al-Nadr, from Zurah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jarhad, from his father, from Jarhad - of the people of al-Suffah - that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with me while my thigh was uncovered, and said: Cover yourself; do you not know the thigh is awrah?"
"1955","1704 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sad narrated to us, from Misar, he said: Abu al-Zinad narrated to us, from his uncle Zurah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jarhad, from his grandfather Jarhad, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me with a cloak, my thigh uncovered, and said: Cover your thigh; the thigh is awrah. Abu Jafar said: In these reports the thigh is awrah. With disagreement from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whether the thigh is awrah or not, we sought the first by sound reasoning. We found a woman's thigh is of her awrah: no mahram relative nor other besides her husband may look at it, as her private part or belly, unlike her chest, head or shin which mahrams may see and only strangers may not. So her thigh is awrah like her private part and belly, not like head, shin or chest. If so for the woman, so for the man: his thigh is awrah, unlike the rest of his body. Then we looked at the knees - like the thigh or the shin?"
"1956","1705 - So we found Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb and Fahd ibn Sulayman both narrated to us, saying: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Ali informed me, that al-Husayn ibn Ali informed him, that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought permission upon Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he permitted him; he was drinking, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began blaming him for what he did to Ali's two she-camels. Hamzah was drunk, eyes red. Hamzah looked at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), raised his gaze, looked to his knee, raised it, looked to his navel, raised it, looked to his face, then said: Are you but slaves of my father? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew he was drunk, so stepped back on his heels and left, and we left with him."
"1957","1706 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"1958","1707 - And we found Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Anbasah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, then he mentioned the like with its chain. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is proof the knee's ruling is like the shin's, not the thigh's."
"1959","1708 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah narrated to us, that he heard Amr ibn al-Sharid narrate from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed a man of Thaqif, hurrying after him until seizing his garment, and said: Raise your izar. The man uncovered his knees and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am knock-kneed and my knees knock. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All Allah's creation is good. We never saw the man but his izar to mid-shin until he died. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is like the prior."
"1960","1709 - And we found Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah, from Aidh Allah Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Abu Bakr came holding his garment's edge, uncovering his knees. He said: As for your companion, he has contended. He greeted and said: Between me and Ibn al-Khattab there was; I hastened against him then regretted, asked him to forgive me and he refused, withdrawing to his house. He said: May Allah forgive you, Abu Bakr - twice. Then Umar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, Allah sent me to you and you said: you lied, while Abu Bakr said: you spoke truth, consoling me with himself and his wealth. Will you leave me my companion? - twice. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is like the prior. And we found reported from Abu Musa al-Ashari speech mixed with threat against opposers, which cannot be by opinion, for threat is not in what is said by opinion where others may differ - contradicting this meaning."
"1961","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hakim al-Athram, from Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, who said: I heard Abu Musa al-Ashari say: Let me not know one who looked of a slave girl but above her navel and below her knees; let me not know one who did that but I punished him. Abu Jafar said: So by what we mentioned this hadith may oppose the prior contrary reports. Then we returned to sound reasoning. We found thigh and shin two joined limbs, one mounted on the other, joining as the disc - two bones, one in the thigh, one in the shin - that disc is the knee. What of it is in the thigh takes the thigh's ruling as awrah; what in the shin takes the shin's, not awrah. But its detail from the shin bone and each measure cannot be distinguished, appearing as one thing, so first is to rule it awrah, not otherwise. As for the navel: in Ali's hadith is proof it is not awrah, likewise in Abu Mahdhurah's."
"1962","1710 - Which Ali ibn Mabad and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, saying: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah informed me, that Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz informed him, from Abu Mahdhurah in the adhan hadith, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand on Abu Mahdhurah's forelock, then passed it over his face, then between his breasts, then upon his liver, then the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached Abu Mahdhurah's navel."
"1963","1711 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah also narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, then he mentioned the like with its chain. So that proves the navel is not awrah. That in the navel is proof it is first over what Abu Musa said. Three Companions also opposed Abu Musa: al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, Abd Allah ibn Umar and Abu Hurayrah."
"1964","1712 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Umayr ibn Ishaq, who said: I was with al-Hasan ibn Ali, and Abu Hurayrah met him and said: Come near me to kiss of you where I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss of you. So he raised his garment and he kissed his navel."
"1965","And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Qudamah ibn Musa, from his father, who said: Abd Allah ibn Umar used to come to us in the mosque; he came while I had wrapped a youth's izar, and he hooked his finger in my izar until lowering it below the navel. So this was first with us over Abu Musa's contrary report, for the navel resembles the chest more than the awrah. And Allah we ask for success."
"1966","Chapter 282 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was from him upon Uthman's entry after Abu Bakr and Umar's entry before, and his changing his state upon Uthman's entry which he did not change for them, may Allah be pleased with them. 1713 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Yahya ibn Said - meaning ibn al-As - from his father, from Aishah, that Abu Bakr sought permission upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while wearing the Mother of the Believers' cloak; he permitted him and he fulfilled his need, then Umar sought permission in that state and fulfilled his need, then left. Then Uthman sought permission, so he sat upright and said to Aishah: Gather your clothes upon you. When he left, Aishah said: Why did you not stir for Abu Bakr and Umar as you stirred for Uthman? He said: Uthman is a very shy man; had I permitted him in that state I feared he would not convey his need."
"1967","1714 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us in another sitting, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Yahya ibn Said, from his father, from Aishah, the like of it."
"1968","1715 - And Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Said ibn al-As informed me, that Said ibn al-As informed him, that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"1969","1716 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Yahya ibn Said ibn al-As, that Said ibn al-As informed him, that Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Uthman narrated to him, that Abu Bakr sought permission upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. An objector said: You reported this hadith in the first chapter, mentioning therein the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying on Uthman: Shall I not be shy of one of whom the angels are shy - and between that and this chapter's is evident disagreement. And he mentioned."
"1970","1717 - What Ali ibn al-Husayn Abu Ubayd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi al-Jurjani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Yahya ibn Said, not mentioning his father, from Aishah, who said: Abu Bakr sought permission upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him in one cloak; he permitted him and he fulfilled his need with him with me in the cloak, then left. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission; he permitted him and he fulfilled his need in that state, then left. Then Uthman sought permission, so he fixed his clothes and sat, and he fulfilled his need, then left. Aishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr sought permission and fulfilled his need in that state, then Umar likewise, then Uthman sought permission and it is as if you were guarded. He said: Uthman is a shy man; had I permitted him in that state I would have thought he would not convey his need to me. Al-Zuhri said: Not as the liars say: Shall I not be shy of a man of whom the angels are shy. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is al-Zuhri - narrator of the first hadith we mentioned in the prior chapter, Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah - ascribing to lying in his narration of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Shall I not be shy of one of whom the angels are shy. So how is one al-Zuhri calls liar adduced, with al-Zuhri's eminence? Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is al-Zuhri is of the eminence mentioned, and we do not think he uttered such a saying about Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, for Muhammad's eminence, meeting Companions he met, and his approved place in taking from whom he took. Of them: Ismail ibn Jafar and Malik ibn Anas narrated from him."
"1971","What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Huwaytib, that Zaynab daughter of Abu Salamah died while Tariq was governor of Madinah; her bier was brought after dawn prayer and placed at al-Baqi. Tariq prayed dawn early. Ibn Abi Harmalah said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Umar say to her family: Either pray over your bier now, or leave it until the sun rises. Of them is Ibn Uyaynah."
"1972","1718 - As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, who said: My brother al-Fadl informed me that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say the talbiyah until stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah. Abu Jafar said: What we hold, Allah knows best, of al-Zuhri's uttering this saying about this hadith's narrator is he did not mean Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, for Muhammad's eminence, soundness and imamate with scholars who narrated from him and adduced his narration. Rather he meant an unknown man whom Ibn Jurayj narrated this hadith from, kunya Abu Khalid."
"1973","1719 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Khalid narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Said al-Madini, he said: Hafsah daughter of Umar narrated to me, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day had placed his garment between his thighs; Abu Bakr came and sought permission, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him in his state, then Umar likewise, then people of his Companions while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his state. Then Uthman came and sought permission, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his garment and wrapped himself. They conversed then left. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr, Umar, Ali and people of your Companions came while in your state, but when Uthman came you wrapped your garment. He said: Shall I not be shy of one of whom the angels are shy? He said: I heard my father and others narrate similar. Abu Jafar said: Al-Zuhri's speech you addressed us with is in our view aimed at this Abu Khalid or another, and at Abd Allah ibn Abi Said and likes - not at Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah and likes, Allah willing. What we say is to authenticate both hadiths, making them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on two different days or occasions, saying in each one of the two mentioned sayings. In that gather both merits for Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: the angels' shyness of him and his own shyness. And with Allah is success."
"1974","Chapter - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to people when he ordered them to leave pollinating palms, so they did and it became poor (shis), what he said to them then. 1720 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was walking with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he passed people atop palms, and said: What are these doing? I said: They pollinate, placing the male in the female. He said: I do not think that avails anything. So they left it and it became poor. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed, and said: If it benefits them, let them do it. I only thought a thought; do not hold me for thought. But when I narrate to you something from Allah, take it, for I shall never lie upon Allah."
"1975","1721 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, from Musa ibn Talhah, from his father, and he mentioned the like, except he did not say: do not hold me for thought, and said instead: thought errs and hits."
"1976","1722 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, and Hisham ibn Urwah from his father, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed people atop palms and said: What are these doing? They said: They pollinate palms. He said: Had they left it, it would be sound. So they left it and it became poor. He said: What is of your worldly affair, you know your worldly affair better; what is of your religious affair, to me."
"1977","1723 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Mujalid ibn Said narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw people pollinating, and said: What is with people? They said: They pollinate, O Messenger of Allah. He said: No pollination - or: I see no pollination as anything. So they left pollination and people's dates came poor. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with it? I am not a farmer nor palm owner; pollinate. An objector said: In what you reported is severe confusion: in Talhah's hadith he said: I do not think that avails; in Aishah and Anas's, he said: Had they left it, it would be sound; in Jabir's: No pollination, or I see no pollination as anything. What is the وجه? Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is what was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that may be that females other than humans do not take from males - prevailing upon hearts - and that was not from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) information from revelation, but a non-decisive saying in which people are equal, then differ, distinguishing knowledgeable from others. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not practice it nor was from a land whose people did, for his land was Makkah, not a palm land then; palms were elsewhere, in Madinah he came to, whose people practiced palms unlike Makkans. Speech in the matter he spoke of was wide for him to say, upon what he deemed impossible and upon his thought. So he said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Talhah reported to some he saw practicing pollination, then what Aishah and Anas reported to other folk, and what is in Jabir's hadith to others. Each reporter truthfully reported what he heard him say, and all his sayings they reported are as he said. And with Allah is success."
"1978","Chapter 284 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the pledge of the emigrant and the bedouin's pledge - what each owes in his pledge. 1724 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Maruf ibn Suwayd, from Abu Ushshanah, from Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: News of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrival at Madinah reached me while with sheep of mine; I left them then came to him and said: I came to pledge you. He said: A bedouin pledge do you want, or an emigration pledge? I said: An emigration pledge. So I pledged him and stayed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: Whoever here is of Mad is to stand. Men stood and I stood with them. He said to me: Sit - twice or thrice. I said: O Messenger of Allah, are we not of Mad? He said: No. I said: Of whom are we? He said: Of Qudaah ibn Malik ibn Himyar. Abu Jafar said: What is in this hadith of Uqbah's saying: So I pledged him and stayed - i.e., in the abode of emigration - proves the emigrant's pledge obliges residence in the emigration abode with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to dispose in what he disposes him in of Islam's affairs, while the bedouin pledge is otherwise, not obliging residence with him. Proven by."
"1979","1725 - What al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii informed us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi informed us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, he said: Abu Qilabah al-Jarmi said: Malik ibn al-Huwayrith Abu Sulayman narrated to us, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among people, youths close in age; we stayed with him twenty nights. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was gentle, merciful. When he thought we desired our families, he asked about whom we left behind; we informed him, and said: Return to your families, stay among them, teach and command them - mentioning things I remember or not - and pray as you saw me pray. When prayer comes, one of you calls adhan and the eldest leads you. Abu Jafar said: Those pledging emigration had to reside in the emigration abode in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) life and after his death, until he in his life, then his successors, may Allah be pleased with them, after him, disposed them in fighting remaining disbelievers and guarding conquered lands. Returning to their bedouin abode was unlawful, for thereby they apostatize from emigration to bedouin life; whoever returns thus is cursed upon the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue."
"1980","1726 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from al-Harith ibn Abd Allah, that Ibn Masud said: The eater of riba, its feeder, its writer and its witness when they know it, the tattooer and tattooed for beauty, the withholder of charity, and the apostate bedouin after his emigration - are cursed upon Muhammad's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tongue to the Day of Resurrection."
"1981","1727 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, then he mentioned the like with its chain, except he said: and its two witnesses when they know it."
"1982","1728 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us - Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash - then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"1983","1729 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Masud informed us, he said: Khalid - meaning ibn al-Harith - narrated to us, from Shubah, from Sulayman, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Murrah, then he mentioned the like with its chain. And enters into this also what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the bedouin who pledged him, then when fevered in Madinah asked him to release him from his pledge."
"1984","1730 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that a bedouin pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Islam; the bedouin was struck by fever in Madinah, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, release me from my pledge. He refused. Then he came and said: Release me; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused. So the bedouin left, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Madinah is like the bellows: it expels its dross and its good becomes pure. Abu Jafar said: It is upon Islam - i.e., the Islam by whose pledge he becomes an emigrant obliged to stay with him, as emigrants must, for him to dispose him. In what we mentioned is the distinction between the emigrant's and bedouin's pledge. And Allah we ask for success."
"1985","Chapter 285 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in permitting Aslam to go out to valleys and ravines after their pledge to him before upon emigration. 1731 - Fahd ibn Sulayman and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, saying: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Harmalah - Abd al-Rahman - he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Husayn narrated to me, that he heard Abd Allah ibn Jarhad - thus Fahd said; Ali said: he heard Umar ibn Abd Allah ibn Jarhad - then both agreed, saying: he says: I heard a man say to Jabir ibn Abd Allah: Who remains with you of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Anas ibn Malik and Salamah ibn al-Akwa remain. A man said: As for Salamah, he apostatized from his emigration. Jabir said: Do not say that; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Go out, O Aslam. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear apostatizing from our emigration. He said: Go out; you are emigrants wherever you are."
"1986","1732 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Harmalah, from Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa, that his father narrated to him, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa came to Madinah and Buraydah ibn Hasib met him and said: You apostatized from your emigration, O Salamah. He said: Allah forbid; I am with the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Go out, O Aslam, seek the winds and dwell in the ravines. They said: We fear that harms our emigration. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are emigrants wherever you are."
"1987","1733 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Mashar al-Barra narrated to us - Abu Jafar said: Abu Mashar Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Barra, Barra al-Ud - he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Iyas ibn Salamah, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Salamah ibn al-Akwa came to Madinah and Buraydah met him and said: O Salamah, your emigration apostatized. He said: Allah forbid; I am with the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission. He said: Go out, O Aslam, dwell in the ravines. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we fear that harms our emigration. He said: You are emigrants wherever you are. An objector said: In what you reported is Aslam's exit from residence in the emigration abode to the bedouin abode - contrary to what you reported obliging in the prior chapter. Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is what we reported in the prior chapter of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursing the apostate bedouin after emigration is in our view - Allah knows best - upon one who apostatizes so as to exit the emigration obliging obedience to bedouin life with no obedience. Aslam were not so; rather contrary, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified of them in what Aishah reported."
"1988","1734 - As al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Abd Allah ibn Niyar, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: Umm Sunbulah al-Aslamiyyah came with a skin of milk gifting it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she placed it with me with her cup. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said: Welcome, O Umm Sunbulah. She said: By my father and mother, I gifted you this skin. He said: May Allah bless you; pour for me in this cup. She poured for him; when he took it I said: You said: I accept no gift from a bedouin. He said: The bedouins of Aslam, O Aishah - they are not bedouins, but our desert folk and we their town folk; when we call them they answer, and when they call us we answer. Then he drank."
"1989","1735 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, then he mentioned the like."
"1990","1736 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, then he mentioned the like with its chain. Abu Jafar said: In al-Rabi's hadith is something we missed mentioning not in others: they are not bedouins - ending his hadith thus. Abu Jafar said: In Aishah's hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) information that Aslam, though gone out, would answer when called to what he wants, as had they not gone out. When so, they are as had they not gone out. In that is proof the blameworthy going out is that whose people do not answer when called; going out otherwise is like town residence. Allah mentioned bedouins in His Book in one place blaming them: they are strongest in disbelief and hypocrisy and most apt not to know Allah's bounds upon His Messenger, and elsewhere describing them with faith: And of the bedouins are those who believe in Allah and the Last Day and take what they spend as nearness with Allah and the Messenger's prayers. The blamed bedouins are those absent from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so as not to know Allah's rulings and obligations upon his tongue. Whoever is otherwise is whom He praised. The Aslamis, may Allah be pleased with them, entered into that, as not parting from him. And Allah we ask for success."
"1991","Chapter 286 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on naming Rabah, Aflah, Yasar, Yasir, Ala, Nafi and Barakah - his dislike and what proves permissibility. 1737 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to forbid naming Ala, Barakah, Aflah and the like, then he fell silent thereafter and said nothing."
"1992","1738 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I live to next year, I shall forbid naming Nafi, Yasar and Barakah. He said: I do not know whether he said: Rafi or not."
"1993","1739 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Abu Sufyan narrated to us, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: If I live, I shall forbid my ummah, Allah willing, that any name Barakah, Nafi and Aflah. I do not know whether he said: Rafi - it is said: Is Barakah here? So it is said: No. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died without forbidding that. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is his saying: If I live to next year I shall forbid these names - proving naming them is not unlawful, for had it been unlawful he would have forbidden at once, not delayed, Allah knows best. In some is he fell silent and did not forbid until he died - proving no prohibition attached, so permissibility stands. Then we looked: did other than Jabir report a prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"1994","1740 - So we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I heard Hilal ibn Yasaf, from al-Rabi ibn Umaylah al-Fazari, from Samurah ibn Jundub, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not name your boy Rabah, Aflah or Yasir - or said: Yasar; it is said: Then so-and-so? So it is said: No."
"1995","1741 - And we found Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Rabi ibn Umaylah, from Samurah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like."
"1996","1742 - And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Mansur, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"1997","1743 - And we found Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Umarah ibn Umayr al-Taymi, from Rabi ibn Umaylah, from Samurah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like."
"1998","1744 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do not name your slave Aflah, Rabah or Yasar. Abu Jafar said: In some of these reports: you say: Is he there? So there is none, so he says: No. In that is proof the prohibition of these names was only fear of bad omen (tiyarah) by them, as forbidden to bring a sick one to a healthy one lest he be afflicted and it be said: it afflicted him because he brought. We mentioned that before. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade tiyarah."
"1999","1745 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Hisham - meaning al-Dastuwai - from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir - meaning from al-Hadrami - that Said ibn al-Musayyib said: I asked Sad about tiyarah and he rebuked me, saying: Who narrated to you? I disliked telling him. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no contagion and no tiyarah."
"2000","1746 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, then he mentioned the like with its chain. He said: That was his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibition of tiyarah, and Muslims must lift it from themselves by his prohibition. Then there came from him on tiyarah beyond this hadith of Sad."
"2001","1747 - Which is what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Isa ibn Asim al-Asadi, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tiyarah is shirk. None of us - but Allah removes it by trust (tawakkul)."
"2002","1748 - And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani and Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah, from Isa, a man of Banu Asad, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So that proves the lifting of tiyarah, Muslims' practicing it, and the duty to abandon heeding it. Of what proves what we mentioned."
"2003","1749 - What Bakkar and Yazid narrated to us, saying: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Simak Abu Zumayl, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abbas narrated to me, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) secluded from his wives, he sat in his loft; I came, and behold Rabah, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), at its threshold. I said: O Rabah, seek permission for me upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. In this is proof of what we mentioned. Of what enters herein also is that among the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, and his governors was al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami, his governor over Bahrayn, remaining upon that name until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while upon it, and remaining upon it until he died, may Allah be pleased with him. In that is proof. And reported from him, upon him peace."
"2004","1750 - What al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, that Zaynab daughter of Abu Salamah asked him: What did you name your daughter? He said: I named her Barrah. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade this name. I was named Barrah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not praise yourselves; Allah knows best the righteous among you. They said: What shall we name her? He said: Name her Zaynab. Abu Jafar said: This in our view, Allah knows best, is before the prohibition of tiyarah. The ruling on names thereby returned to using them all, unless a prohibition later than tiyarah, for they are markers distinguishing the indicated from others of its kind. And Allah we ask for success."
"2005","Chapter 287 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what befalls in prayer - tasbih, clapping and throat-clearing. 1751 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Mughirah al-Dabbi, from al-Harith al-Ukli, from Abd Allah ibn Nujayy, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I had two entries upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when I entered while he was praying, he cleared his throat."
"2006","1752 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad ibn Shaddad al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, then he mentioned the like with its chain. Abu Jafar said: In what we reported is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting throat-clearing for the praying one at things befalling him in prayer. Then we considered this hadith: were its mentioned narrators contradicted or not?"
"2007","1753 - So we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil Fudayl ibn al-Husayn al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn al-Qaqa narrated to us, from al-Harith al-Ukli, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abd Allah ibn Nujayy, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: I had an hour before dawn entering upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if he was in prayer he glorified (sabbaha), and that was his permission to me. Abu Jafar said: Thereby we found its narrators with the first meaning of throat-clearing were contradicted, and in place of the mentioned throat-clearing is the tasbih in the second hadith - first with us, for the reports the generality of scholars narrate on what befalls a man in prayer to use therein is tasbih, while women use clapping."
"2008","1754 - Of that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him say: Glory be to Allah. Clapping is for women, tasbih for men."
"2009","1755 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him glorify; when he glorifies, he is turned to. Clapping is for women."
"2010","1756 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him glorify; for clapping is for women. Abu Jafar said: What is commanded in these reports is tasbih from men - sound, accepted reports all scholars upon - except Malik equated men and women, making what all use therein tasbih, not clapping."
"2011","1757 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik was asked: Does a woman clap in prayer? He said: No. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is befallen by something in his prayer, let him glorify. Except Abu Hanifah used to say: Whoever glorifies in his prayer initiating does not spoil his prayer, but if glorifying in answer spoils it. Muhammad ibn al-Hasan followed him, while Abu Yusuf opposed them, saying: Prayer is valid in all that."
"2012","As Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah with what we mentioned from him. And from Ali, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf with what we mentioned from him. And from Ali, from Muhammad with what we mentioned from him. The matter with us in all that is following what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, abandoning departure from it, women using therein clapping not tasbih, men tasbih not clapping, with no difference between tasbih initiating or answering; for we saw speech not spoken in prayer - its ruling: it cuts it whether initiating or answering. Since tasbih does not cut it initiating, it does not cut it answering. Abu Hurayrah reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the distinction between women and men as mentioned in Ibn Uyaynah's hadith from Abu Hazim."
"2013","1758 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Tasbih is for men and clapping for women."
"2014","1759 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Abu Jafar said: That confirmed what Ibn Uyaynah reported from Abu Hazim of distinguishing men and women in what they use in this befalling in their prayers. And Allah we ask for success."
"2015","Chapter 288 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying on the Day of Ghadir Khumm to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Whomsoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla. 1760 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali, from his father, from Ali, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attended the tree at Khumm, coming out holding Ali's hand, and said: O people, do you not testify Allah is your Lord? They said: Indeed. He said: Do you not testify Allah and His Messenger are worthier of you than yourselves, and Allah and His Messenger your two mawlas? They said: Indeed. He said: Whomsoever I am his mawla, this is his mawla - or said: Ali is his mawla - Ibn Marzuq doubted - I have left among you what if you hold to you shall never stray: Allah's Book, its rope in your hands, and my household. And Kathir ibn Zayd is Madinan, freedman of Aslam; Hammad ibn Zayd, Waki and Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated from him."
"2016","1761 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Amir al-Bajali narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Sabii narrated to us, from Amr Dhu Murr, who said: I heard Ali adjuring people in al-Rahbah: Whoever heard the Messenger of Allah speak on Ghadir Khumm day but stands. A dozen-odd men stood and testified they heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Ghadir Khumm day say: O Allah, whomsoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla. O Allah, befriend who befriends him, oppose who opposes him, love who loves him, hate who hates him, aid who aids him, help who helps him, forsake who forsakes him."
"2017","1762 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb al-Nasai narrated to us, he said: Harun - meaning al-Hammal - narrated to us, he said: Musab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered people in al-Rahbah and said: I adjure by Allah every man who heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say on Ghadir Khumm day what he heard. People stood and testified the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on Ghadir Khumm day: Do you not know I am worthier of the believers than themselves? - standing - then he took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Whomsoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla. O Allah, befriend who befriends him and oppose who opposes him. Abu al-Tufayl said: I left with something in myself, met Zayd ibn Arqam and told him. He said: What do you deny? I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: An objector rejected this hadith, saying it is impossible, claiming Ali, upon him peace, was not with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his departure to Hajj from Madinah whose route passed Ghadir Khumm - for Ghadir Khumm is at al-Juhfah - mentioning."
"2018","1763 - What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, who said: We entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah and he mentioned his hadith on the Prophet's Hajj, upon him peace, saying: Ali came from Yemen with the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificial camels, then he mentioned the rest."
"2019","1764 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata narrated to me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari among people with me, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib came from his collection; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: With what did you enter ihram, O Ali? He said: With what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram. He said: So sacrifice and stay inviolate as you are. Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is Ali as mentioned was not with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his departure to Hajj from Madinah whose passing was at Ghadir Khumm, but he was with him in his coming from Makkah to Madinah on whose route was Ghadir Khumm. So what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him there may have been in his return from Hajj. It would be impossible as mentioned only had the hadith said he said it heading to Makkah. We found, by Allah's praise, a sound-chained hadith informing that saying to Ali at Ghadir Khumm was in his return to Madinah from Hajj, not his departure to it."
"2020","1765 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman - meaning al-Amash - he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Zayd ibn Arqam, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from the Farewell Hajj and stopped at Ghadir Khumm, he ordered the large trees cleared, then said: It is as if I was called and answered. I have left among you the two weighty things, one greater than the other: Allah's Book and my progeny, my household. See how you succeed me in them, for they shall never separate until they come to me at the Basin. Then he said: Allah is my Mawla and I the wali of every believer. Then he took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Whomsoever I am his wali, this is his wali. O Allah, befriend who befriends him and oppose who opposes him. I said to Zayd: Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: None under the trees but saw it with his eyes and heard it with his ears. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is sound-chained, none impugning any narrator, that the saying to Ali at Ghadir Khumm was in his return from Hajj to Madinah, not departure. This objector said: This hadith was reported from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas in this story, that the saying was at Ghadir Khumm in his departure from Madinah to Hajj, not return."
"2021","1766 - He mentioned what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya, meaning ibn Abi Umar, narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Jafar ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Muhajir ibn Mismar, he said: Aishah daughter of Sad informed me, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Makkah's road heading to it; when he reached Ghadir Khumm he halted people, returned those ahead and those behind caught up. When people gathered, he said: O people, did I convey? They said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness - thrice - then said: O people, who is your wali? They said: Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - thrice - then he took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, raised him and said: Whomsoever Allah and His Messenger are his wali, this is his wali. O Allah, befriend who befriends him and oppose who opposes him. Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is this hadith was only narrated as mentioned by Yaqub ibn Jafar ibn Abi Kathir, not well-known for knowledge nor among its firm transmitters. Another narrated it from Muhajir ibn Mismar - Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai - without this phrase Yaqub mentioned."
"2022","1767 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Musafir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai narrated to us, from Muhajir ibn Mismar, freedman of Amir ibn Sad, that Aishah informed him, that Sad ibn Abi Waqqas said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of al-Juhfah order the palm trees cleared beneath them; at departure the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out, took Ali's hand, addressed people, praised Allah, then said: To proceed, O people, I am your wali. They said: You spoke truth, O Messenger of Allah. Then he took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, raised it and said: This is my wali and the performer on my behalf. May Allah befriend who befriends him and oppose who opposes him."
"2023","1768 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Uthman al-Basri Abu al-Jawza narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Athmah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Yaqub narrated to us, from Muhajir ibn Mismar, from Aishah daughter of Sad, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed people, praised Allah, then said: Do you not know I am worthier of you than yourselves? They said: Yes, you spoke truth, O Messenger of Allah. Then he took Ali's hand, may Allah be pleased with him, raised it and said: Whomsoever I am his wali, this is his wali. Allah befriends who befriends him and opposes who opposes him. Abu Jafar said: Ibn Abi Fudayk and Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Athmah both narrated this hadith from Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai from Muhajir ibn Mismar free of Yaqub ibn Jafar's addition you adduced. What Abu Awanah narrated from Sulayman al-Amash from Habib ibn Abi Thabit from Abu al-Tufayl from Zayd ibn Arqam would have sufficed us from engaging Yaqub ibn Jafar's narration, for his like does not oppose the firm, eminent, learned transmitters we mentioned. But we undertook it as added proof against you. Malik ibn Anas saw Aishah daughter of Sad and entered upon her."
"2024","Yunus narrated: Ibn Wahb and Ashhab both informed us from Malik, he said: Aishah daughter of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to me that her father, may Allah be pleased with him, had a basin from which he and his household performed ablution - in Ashhab's hadith: perhaps performing with their leftover. I heard Yunus when narrating this hadith say: See Malik's precision and his choice of whom to take knowledge from: he entered upon this woman and did not see her precise in what she narrated, so he took from her nothing but what he knew she had precise, not going beyond her; he did not take from her else what others took. Then he mentioned to us therewith from an unnamed one from Malik this speech verbatim, may Allah have mercy on him. This objector said: Al-Hakam ibn Utaybah narrated from this Aishah - proof of her eminence in knowledge, else al-Hakam would take nothing from her. Said to him: That from al-Hakam was only mentioned by Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, whose narration, as scholars of transmission know, is well-known."
"2025","1769 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Ismail ibn Sulayman al-Mujalidi narrated to us, he said: al-Muttalib - ibn Ziyad - narrated to us, from Layth, from al-Hakam, from Aishah daughter of Sad, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali in the Tabuk campaign: You are to me in the place of Harun to Musa, except there is no prophet after me. And he said: As if the correct is al-Hakam did not take this hadith from Aishah daughter of Sad, but from Musab ibn Sad. Likewise narrated the firm, trusted, precise proof - Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj."
"2026","1770 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar - meaning Ghundar - narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Musab ibn Sad, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left Ali behind in the Tabuk campaign, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, do you leave me over women and children? He said: Are you not pleased to be to me at Harun's station to Musa, except there is no prophet after me? So was nullified, by Allah's praise, what Layth narrated therein from al-Hakam, and confirmed what Shubah narrated. An objector said: What is the meaning of: Whomsoever I am his mawla, Ali is his mawla? Said to him: Mawla here is wali, as Allah said: The believing men and women, some are awliya of others. That is clarified in what we reported: whoever was a wali of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is likewise of Ali; likewise his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, some awliya of others. And Allah we ask for success."
"2027","Chapter 289 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, when he pointed to the moon: Seek refuge with Allah from the evil of this; for it is the darkening (ghasiq) when it sets. 1771 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This moon, O Aishah - seek refuge with Allah from the evil of this. Do you know what this is? This is the ghasiq when it sets (waqaba)."
"2028","1772 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi and Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, saying: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, then he mentioned the like with its chain."
"2029","1773 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman and al-Mundhir, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its meaning."
"2030","1774 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman - meaning Ibn Abi Dhib - from al-Harith, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Abu Jafar said: We know no outlet for this hadith but this, nor know any who narrated it from Ibn Abi Dhib mentioning al-Mundhir with al-Harith in its chain but Abu Amir al-Aqadi. This al-Mundhir is al-Mundhir ibn Abi al-Mundhir; we know none narrated from him but Ibn Abi Dhib. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to determine its intent, Allah willing, since some deemed it grave, saying: What evil is in the moon, a creation of Allah obedient to Him? - mentioning Allah's saying: Do you not see Allah prostrates to Him whoever in heavens and earth, the sun and moon - to: and many of people - informing of obedient creation, then saying: and many deserved punishment - the disobedient. So what evil in the moon to seek refuge from? Our answer, by Allah's guidance, is the moon is Allah's obedient creation with no evil of its own; intended therein is other than imagined. Allah made night and day two signs, clarifying: We made night and day two signs, effacing night's sign and making day's seeing. Night's sign is the moon, day's the sun. The moon, for the effacing Allah effaced in it, is at the darkness not with day. People of sins unable to show them by day for fear of penalties show them at night in safety. Allah has creation - the devils - spreading at night not day, as reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"2031","1775 - As Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When night falls, hold back your children until an hour of the night has passed, then let them go; for the devils spread out at that time. And close your doors and mention the name of Allah, Mighty and Majestic; for the devils do not open what is locked. And tie your waterskins and mention the name of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and cover your vessels and mention the name of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, even if by laying a stick across it. He said: And Amr informed me from Jabir with something like this, but did not mention: mention the name of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"2032","1776 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to me from his father. And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: al-Rabi said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam said: my father and Shuayb informed us, from al-Layth, then they all agreed, saying: from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Cover the vessel, tie the waterskin, close the door, and extinguish the lamp; for Satan does not untie a waterskin, nor open a door, nor uncover a vessel. And if one of you cannot find anything but to lay a stick across his vessel and mention the name of Allah over it, let him do so; for the little mouse sets fire to the house of its people."
"2033","1777 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Close the door, tie the waterskin, turn over the vessel or cover the vessel, and extinguish the lamp; for Satan does not open what is locked, nor untie a knot, nor uncover a vessel, and the little mouse sets fire to the house of people. This, with what we mentioned of the sons of Adam and of the devils occurring at night in the darkness caused by the blotting out in the moon, unlike the daylight of the day, is why the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, to seek refuge from the evil of the moon which is the cause of the night, meaning thereby the things that happen at night which the moon is a cause of, and he did not mean the moon itself. That from him is like the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Ask the town where we were, and the caravan in which we came - not meaning the town itself nor the caravan itself, but meaning the people of the town and the people of the caravan. So likewise his saying to Aisha about the moon, Seek refuge with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from the evil of this, does not mean the moon itself, but means what happens in the darkness which the moon causes, from the sons of Adam and from the devils who are enemies to Aisha and to others besides her of the sons of Adam. And like that is what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2034","1778 - As Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysara narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, from Kab, he said: I bear witness - by the One who parted the sea for Musa, peace be upon him - that I heard Suhayb say: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a town he intended to stay in, he said: O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, and Lord of the winds and what they scatter, and Lord of the lands and what they carry, and Lord of the devils and what they lead astray, I ask You for the good of this town and the good of its people, and I seek refuge with You from its evil and the evil of its people and the evil of what is in it. Abu Jafar said: The town itself has no good in it nor evil in it; rather good and evil come from other than it, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attributed them to it because they are in it. And such is the speech of the Arabs. So likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attributed to the moon of what Aisha mentioned is of this meaning. And we ask Allah for success."
"2035","290 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his prohibition of killing the frog. 1779 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Said ibn Khalid, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman, he said: A physician mentioned a medicine in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned a frog used in the medicine, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade killing it."
"2036","1780 - And al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to find in it what needs the like of it, God willing, and we found the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against killing the frog. In that is what indicates the difference between its ruling and the ruling of fish; for there is no harm in killing fish, and since the frog is forbidden to kill, it is unlike fish. And in that is what indicates that what is in the sea besides fish is disliked to eat, unlike fish whose eating is lawful. And if one says: he forbade killing the frog only because it glorifies Allah, it is said to him: fish also glorify Allah. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: There is nothing except that it glorifies His praise, but you do not understand their glorification. And that did not prevent killing it for eating and benefiting from it. So that proves the frog was forbidden to kill for something else, namely because it is not eaten, and whatever is not eaten, killing it is idle, and idle action in that is unlawful. And we ask Allah for success."
"2037","291 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about private conversation, both prohibition and permission. 1781 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr al-Asadi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Rubayh ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Said al-Khudri narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: We used to take turns attending the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he would have a need, or send us for some matter, and those waiting of the people of the turns became many. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were discussing the Dajjal, and said: What is this private conversation? Did I not forbid you from private conversation? He said: We said, O Messenger of Allah, we were discussing the False Messiah out of fear of him. He said: Something else is more fearful for you than that: hidden polytheism, that a man acts for the sake of another man. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is the report of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about private conversation, informing them of his prior prohibition to them of it. And that, in our view - and Allah knows best - is not about all private conversation, but about private conversation of what private conversation has been forbidden for, as Allah said: O you who believe, when you converse privately, do not converse in sin and aggression and disobedience to the Messenger, but converse in righteousness and piety, and fear Allah to whom you shall be gathered. So the private conversation forbidden in that hadith is the private conversation forbidden in this verse. And Allah knows best. Then we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about private conversation:"
"2038","1782 - Of what Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When there are three, two should not converse privately apart from one."
"2039","1783 - And of what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Umar, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Two should not whisper apart from the third."
"2040","1784 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When there are three, two should not converse privately apart from one."
"2041","1785 - And of what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same. So in what we reported is the prohibition for three, against two of them conversing apart from the third. It may be that is a prohibition because of the bad manners in it from the two conversing apart from their companion. Then we found from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about that:"
"2042","1786 - Of what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us. And of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, both saying: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When you are three, two should not converse privately apart from their companion. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what if we are four? He said: It will not harm him. So in that is what indicates that four in that are unlike three; for when two converse apart from one, they deprive him of his share from them, but when they are four and two of them converse, the remaining two can converse together, so they are in that like their two companions in their conversing."
"2043","1787 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, he said: I and Abdillah ibn Umar were at the house of Khalid ibn Uqba in the market, and a man came wanting to converse with him privately, and there was no one with Ibn Umar except me and the man wanting to converse with him. So Abdillah ibn Umar called another man until we were four, and said to me and to the man he called: Step back, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Two should not converse privately apart from one. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith, from the action of Ibn Umar, is what agrees with what we mentioned of the hadith of Abu Salih from him. This is what we found in this chapter, from Abdillah ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And it has been reported from Abdillah ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning, the like of what Ibn Umar reported from him, with an addition of the reason for which the prohibition was made."
"2044","1788 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you are three, two should not converse privately apart from their companion; for that grieves him."
"2045","1789 - And as Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, from Abu Wail, from Abdillah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same."
"2046","1790 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir narrated to me, from Asim ibn Bahdala, from Abu Wail or Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ibn Masud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same."
"2047","1791 - And as Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you are three, two should not converse privately apart from one. Abu Jafar said: Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, thus informed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the meaning for which he forbade two conversing apart from one, and it does not contradict what we mentioned before it. And this hadith has been reported from Ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with an addition to this meaning."
"2048","1792 - As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdillah ibn Masud said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, when three are on a journey, that two converse privately apart from one, until they mix with people; because that grieves him. Abu Jafar said: He thus informed that he meant thereby private conversation on a journey in which the third fears for himself in that conversation, since there is no helper for him if from that conversation comes a cause needing help in it. And in that is what indicates the lifting of the prohibition when that is absent, though the better in it is to leave that act, so that the hadith of Ibn Masud and the hadith of Ibn Umar are both acted upon in what they came about. And if one says: this hadith with mention of the journey was reported only in the hadith of Salih which you mentioned, it is said to him: what do you object to in it, given the soundness of its channel, and it has been reported by another route from the words of Ibn Masud of what we know he did not say from his own opinion, since the like of it is not said by opinion, but he said it because he took it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2049","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah, he said: When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you over you, and two should not converse privately apart from their companion. And this has been reported from Ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with wording other than this wording."
"2050","1793 - As Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Mansur, from Shaqiq, from Abdillah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us, when we were three, that two converse privately apart from their companion, until they mix with people, because that grieves him."
"2051","1794 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ubayda ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, from Abdillah ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. So in that is what indicates that the prohibition of this meaning mentioned in this chapter is only in a place where there is no helper. And in that is what agrees with what is in the hadith of Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman which we reported, containing the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his prohibition of what he forbade therein when they are on a journey. And we ask Allah for success."
"2052","292 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what he did to those who raided his milch camels and apostatized from Islam: was that a punishment from him for their waging war, as punishes warriors for that, whether apostates or not, or for their apostasy along with the deeds they did. 1795 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Shabbuwayh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, from his father, from Yazid al-Nahwi, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and spread corruption in the land is that they be killed or crucified, up to His saying: Forgiving, Merciful. This verse was revealed about the polytheists; whoever of them repents before you overpower them, there is no way against him. And this verse does not protect a Muslim man from the prescribed punishment if he kills or spreads corruption in the land or wages war against Allah and His Messenger, then joins the disbelievers before he is overpowered; that does not prevent the prescribed punishment he incurred from being carried out on him."
"2053","1796 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya informed me, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, he said: Yazid al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about His saying, Mighty and Majestic: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, the verse, he said: This verse was revealed about the polytheists; whoever of them repents before he is overpowered, there is no way against him. And this verse is not for a Muslim man who kills and spreads corruption in the land and wages war against Allah and His Messenger, then joins the disbelievers before he is overpowered; that does not prevent the prescribed punishment he incurred from being carried out on him."
"2054","1797 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karima informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahim narrated to me, he said: Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa narrated to me, from Talha ibn Musarrif, from Yahya ibn Said, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Bedouins from Urayna came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and became Muslims, but Medina did not suit them, until their colors turned yellow and their bellies swelled. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent them to some milch camels of his and ordered them to drink from their milk and urine until they recovered. Then they killed their herdsmen and drove off the camels. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent in pursuit of them, and they were brought, and he cut off their hands and feet and branded their eyes. The Commander of the Believers Abd al-Malik said to Anas while he was narrating this hadith to him: Was it for disbelief or for sin? He said: For disbelief. So in the first of these two hadiths, the ruling mentioned therein is about polytheists when they do these deeds, not about others who hold to Islam. And in the second of them is what indicates that the punishment in that, in the view of Anas ibn Malik, was because those deeds were with apostasy, not with Islam. And since they differed in that, we sought the right view in it, and we found that Allah said in His Book: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and spread corruption in the land is that they be killed or crucified, or their hands and feet cut off from opposite sides, or they be exiled from the land; that is disgrace for them in this world, the verse. So what Allah mentioned in this verse states that the punishments mentioned therein are a recompense for whoever commits those things which those punishments punish, and those things may come from one who claims Islam and from others. And waging war is enmity toward Allah, Mighty and Majestic, by deeds He does not approve."
"2055","1798 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: And Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Ayyash ibn Abbas, that is al-Qitbani, narrated to me, from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, went out to the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was Muadh ibn Jabal weeping at the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: What makes you weep, O Muadh? He said: Something I heard from the occupant of this grave makes me weep. He said: What is it? He said: I heard him say: A little showing off is polytheism, and whoever shows enmity to the allies of Allah has openly warred against Allah. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves the righteous, the hidden, the pious, who when absent are not missed, and when present are not called upon nor brought near; their hearts are lamps of guidance; they come out of every dusty darkness."
"2056","1799 - And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, then he mentioned the same, but did not mention Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman in its chain. Abu Jafar said: Thus it is necessary to apply what is in this verse to whoever commits this waging of war and the spreading mentioned therein until the Day of Resurrection, whether of the people of the faith remaining upon Islam, or of the people of the faith leaving Islam for its opposite, or of the people of the covenant remaining upon their covenant, or of the people of the covenant leaving their covenant by breaking the pact upon them therein. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that a hadith necessitating what we said."
"2057","1800 - And it is what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awaqi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aisha, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful to kill a Muslim who testifies that there is no god but Allah except for one of three: an adulterer after his marriage, or a man who kills and is killed for it, or a man who goes out warring against Allah and His Messenger, so he is killed or crucified or exiled from the land. Then one said: Muhammad ibn Sinan has been contradicted in this hadith from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, for it was reported from him otherwise."
"2058","1801 - As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad, that is al-Duri, informed us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The blood of a Muslim is not lawful except for one of three traits: a married adulterer who is stoned, or a man who kills deliberately and is killed, or a man who leaves Islam and wars against Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he is killed or crucified or exiled from the land. Abu Jafar said: Our answer to him was: His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or a man who leaves Islam, after his saying: The blood of a Muslim is not lawful except for one of three traits, affirms Islam for its people, then the mention of these occurrences from them proves he meant one who has a share in Islam when he does these deeds. And his saying, leaves Islam, may mean he leaves the body of the people of Islam to rise against them with his sword, so that agrees with what Muhammad ibn Sinan reported of this hadith from Ibrahim ibn Tahman. Were it not for that, there would be no meaning to mentioning Islam at its start; for if these deeds were from other than the people of Islam, they would deserve this punishment in the view of all the people of knowledge. But mentioning Islam necessitates that the doers of these three deeds are of the people of Islam, departing from the character of its people to those blameworthy deeds, and we seek refuge with Allah from them. Then one said: You have argued with this hadith of Ibrahim ibn Tahman, and in it is the choice of the ruler in these things, whichever he sees fit to carry out on the warriors, and you do not say that, though more than one of the people of knowledge before you held choice in this punishment? Then he mentioned."
"2059","Of what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isam, from al-Hasan, about His saying, or... or..., he said: The ruler has the choice: if he wishes he kills, and if he wishes he crucifies, and if he wishes he cuts."
"2060","And of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ubayda, from Ibrahim and Abu Hurra, from al-Hasan, and Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, and al-Hajjaj, from Ata, and Layth, from Ata and Mujahid, that they used to say: The ruler has the choice in that; whichever of that he wishes, he does."
"2061","And of what Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Awn al-Ziyadi narrated to us, from Hammad, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz."
"2062","And of what Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, from Hammad, from Qatada, he said: The ruler has the choice."
"2063","And of what Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Damra narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, he said: The ruler has the choice: if he wishes he kills, and if he wishes he kills and crucifies, and if he wishes he cuts, and if he wishes he exiles."
"2064","And of what Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi Abu al-Awwam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Qatada, from Said, he said: When the ruler seizes the warrior, he judges in him as he wishes. He said: All these reports, from these Successors, are about the choice of the ruler. And Malik ibn Anas used to hold this. So to whose saying did you oppose that? It was said to him: To the saying of Abdillah ibn Abbas."
"2065","As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiya al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Atiyya al-Awfi, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When a man goes out warring and frightens the road and takes wealth, his hand and foot are cut off from opposite sides. And if he takes wealth and kills, his hand and foot are cut off from opposite sides and he is crucified. And if he kills and does not take wealth, he is killed. And if he frightens the road and does not take wealth, he is exiled. And to this saying Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and Abu Yusuf used to go."
"2066","As for Abu Hanifa, he used to say: When he takes wealth and kills, the ruler has the choice: if he wishes he cuts his hand and foot from opposite sides then kills him, and if he wishes he kills him and does not cut his hand and foot from opposite sides. Thus Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. As for what you related from Malik, you have erred about him in it; for Malik used to apply choice, as I mentioned, so long as he did not kill or long remain in warring. But when that happened, his ruling was to kill him. So his saying thereby returns to part of the saying of the others who make the verse by ranks, not by choice. Then this speaker said: Why did you not allow the ruler to kill for warring when its people did not commit killing, by the outward of the verse? I said: Because of what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which repels that."
"2067","1802 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fadl Arim narrated to us, and as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Abu Umama ibn Sahl, he said: I was with Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, in the house while he was besieged. He entered one day for a need then came out and said: Why do they kill me? For I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The blood of a Muslim is not lawful except for one of three: a man who disbelieves after his faith, or commits adultery after his marriage, or kills a soul without a soul. By Allah, I never committed adultery in ignorance nor in Islam ever, nor did I wish to exchange my religion since Allah, Mighty and Majestic, guided me. So why do they kill me?"
"2068","And as al-Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Amir ibn Rabia, that they were with Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, in the house. When he heard that they intended to kill him, he said: I know of nothing that makes killing the believer lawful except disbelief after faith, or adultery after marriage, or killing a soul without a soul."
"2069","1803 - And as Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Yaqub informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Isa, that is Ibn al-Tabba, narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Umama ibn Sahl and Abdillah ibn Amir ibn Rabia narrated to us, saying: We were with Uthman while he was besieged. He entered one day then came out with changed color and said: They are threatening me with killing, but why do they kill me? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The blood of a Muslim is not lawful except for one of three: a man who disbelieves after his Islam, or commits adultery after his marriage, or kills a soul without a soul. By Allah, I never committed adultery in ignorance nor in Islam, nor did I wish to have a substitute for my religion since Allah, Mighty and Majestic, guided me, nor did I kill a soul. So for what do they kill me?"
"2070","1804 - And of what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abdillah ibn Murra, from Masruq, from Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: By the One besides whom there is no god, the blood of one who testifies that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah is not lawful except for one of three: the one who abandons Islam and leaves the community, and the married adulterer, and a soul for a soul."
"2071","1805 - And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yaqub al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, then he mentioned the same with its chain, with an addition."
"2072","He said: Sufyan said: Ibrahim narrated it to me, he said: al-Aswad narrated to me, from Aisha, with that."
"2073","1806 - And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, he said: Zaida ibn Qudama narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Amash narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with both chains in it together."
"2074","1807 - And as Ali ibn Shayba and Abu Umayya both narrated to us, saying: Ubaydillah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Shayban al-Nahwi narrated to us, from al-Amash, then he mentioned the same with both chains in it together."
"2075","1808 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Ghalib, he said: al-Ashtar entered upon Aisha, and she said: You intended to kill my nephew. He said: He was keen to kill me, and I was keen to kill him. She said: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then she mentioned something like the first hadith."
"2076","1809 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Ghalib, he said: Ammar ibn Yasir and al-Ashtar entered upon Aisha at Basra, and she said: As for you, O Ammar, you have known what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned something like it. So in what we reported is the denial by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the lawfulness of the blood of one who testifies that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, except for one of the three mentioned in this hadith. Thus it is established that the blood of one who departs from the Muslims by his departing is not lawful until there is killing in that. And in what we mentioned is agreement with what we reported from Ibn Abbas. And we ask Allah for success."
"2077","293 - Explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the manner of punishing the people of the milch camels. 1810 - Abu Umayya Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Qabisa ibn Uqba narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas: The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger, he said: They were a people from Ukl; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut off their hands and feet and branded their eyes."
"2078","1811 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah, from Abdillah ibn Umar or Ibn Amr - the doubt is from Amr - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning the hadith of the Uranis, he said: And about them the verse of warring was revealed."
"2079","1812 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awzai, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Qilaba al-Jarmi narrated to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, he said: People from Ukl came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Medina did not suit them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to go to the charity camels and drink from their urine and milk. So they went to them, killed their herdsmen, and drove off the camels. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent in pursuit of them, and they were brought, and he cut off their hands and feet, then did not cauterize them."
"2080","1813 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Eight men from Ukl came and Medina made them ill. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent them to a herd of his, and they drank from its milk. When they recovered, they apostatized from Islam, killed, and stole the camels. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent after them, cut off their hands and feet, gouged their eyes, and left them until they died."
"2081","1814 - Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: People from Urayna came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Medina and it did not suit them. He said: If you went out to some herd of ours and drank from its milk. He said: And Qatada mentioned that he remembered from him: its urine."
"2082","1815 - Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Maslama ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Qatada and Thabit and Humayd, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, and he said: from its milk and its urine. Abu Jafar said: We mentioned these two hadiths, though there is no mention in them of what the punishment was, for a meaning we needed to mention them for; we shall bring it in the chapter following this chapter, God willing."
"2083","1816 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Kufi al-Dhuhli narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Abi Uthman narrated to me, he said: Abu Raja, the freedman of Abu Qilaba, narrated to me, from Abu Qilaba, he said: Anas himself narrated to me that a group from Ukl, eight men, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pledged to him upon Islam. But the land did not suit them and their bodies sickened. So they complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Will you not go out with our herdsman among his camels and take from their urine and milk? So they recovered, then killed the herdsman and drove off the cattle. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent after them, and they were caught and brought. Their hands and feet were cut off, their eyes were gouged, then they were cast in the sun until they died."
"2084","1817 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil and Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, that people from Urayna came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Medina and it did not suit them. He said to them: If you wish to go out to the charity camels and drink from their milk and urine. So they did and recovered, then turned on the herdsmen and killed them, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"2085","1818 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Qurra, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A group from an Arab tribe came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), became Muslims and pledged to him. Then illness struck - that is pleurisy - and they said: O Messenger of Allah, this ailment has struck; if you would permit us to go out to the camels and stay among them. He said: Yes, go out and stay among them. So they went out, killed one of the two herdsmen, and took the camels. He said: The other came wounded and said: They killed my companion and took the camels. And with him were youths of the Ansar, nearly twenty. So he sent to them and dispatched with them a tracker, who tracked their traces. They were brought, and he cut off their hands and feet and branded their eyes."
"2086","1819 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Damra ibn Rabia narrated to us, from Ibn Shawdhab, from al-Hasan, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf summoned Anas ibn Malik and said to him: What is the severest punishment with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) punished? So he narrated to him of those whose hands and feet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut, whose eyes he gouged without cauterizing them, whom he cast at al-Harra, without feeding them nor giving them drink until they died. Abu Jafar said: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did to them was killing; for them the killing mentioned in the verse revealed about them, which we already recited in this chapter. Some people argued thereby for what Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say about warriors: when they take wealth and kill, the ruler has the choice: if he wishes he cuts their hands and feet from opposite sides, as is done to them if they took wealth and did not kill, and if he wishes he kills them as punishment for the killing from them. Abu Yusuf differed with him in that, saying: He has no way to cutting their hands and feet; rather his way with them is killing them, nothing else. And this saying, in our view, is more deserving than what Abu Hanifa said in this meaning; for what belongs to the ruler in the prescribed punishments is to carry them out, not to leave them. And since, in his view in this meaning, he may leave cutting hands and feet and suffice with the killing he must carry out on them, we understood thereby that what he may leave is not of the prescribed punishments; rather what he must carry out of them, he may not go beyond to other than it. And of our proof against one who argued for Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, with what we mentioned, against his opponent, is that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did to those people, which he did to them, was before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forbade him mutilation of one whose killing was lawful to him. So at that time he might kill one whose killing was lawful to him by cutting hands and feet without cauterizing them, and withholding from their people - lawful for him in those people - food and drink until they died by that. So doing that to them was killing them thereby, not because it was a prescribed punishment on them for their hands and feet. Do you not see that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gouged their eyes intending thereby to kill them, not anything else of a prescribed punishment on them below their lives upon their limbs. Then he was prevented from the like of that by his prohibition (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of mutilation."
"2087","1820 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Mansur, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to address us, commanding us to give charity and forbidding us mutilation."
"2088","1821 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, he said: Samura ibn Jundub narrated to us, he said: Seldom did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) address us in a sermon without commanding us in it to give charity and forbidding us in it mutilation."
"2089","1822 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Samura said: Seldom did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand among us addressing without commanding us to give charity and forbidding us mutilation. Abu Jafar said: That was an abrogation of mutilation. And the killing obligated by the like of what those people did became lawful to apply by the verse revealed about them, with the mutilation applied in that abrogated from it. And some people reported a hadith containing, from the words of Anas ibn Malik, an extra letter beyond all in these hadiths we reported in this chapter, and it is:"
"2090","1823 - Of what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Sahl narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ghaylan - trustworthy, reliable - narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gouged the eyes of those only because they gouged the eyes of the herdsmen. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is from the saying of Anas what we mentioned of him therein. And this hadith, in our view, is rejected; for in what we already reported in this chapter is that one of the two herdsmen of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who was among those camels, when he came to him, said: They killed my companion. And in that is what negates that he was gouged in the eye. And there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge about what is carried out on one from whom comes the like of what came from those people: that it is a prescribed punishment of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for the warring that occurred, not a right of those warred upon by it; and that those warred upon, if their guardians pardoned what was done to their companions, their pardon is void. And in that is what proves that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do to those people what he did as retaliation for what they did, but he did it to them for what warring obligated upon them, not for other than it. And there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that we know about warriors: if they cut ears and hands and feet until they leave nothing of ear nor hand nor foot to one they warred upon, the like of that is not done to them, but they are confined to what is in the verse Allah revealed about warring, which we already recited in this chapter. And in what we mentioned of that is what proves the corruption of this hadith we reported. And success is with Allah."
"2091","294 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the milch camels for whose takers his punishment was what it was: were they of the charity camels, or were they his own. 1824 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih informed me. And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, about those who stole the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent them out to his milch camels. They killed its herdsman and drove them to the land of polytheism. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, make thirsty whoever made the family of Muhammad thirsty this night. Then he sent in pursuit of them, and they were seized. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut off their hands and feet and gouged their eyes. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what indicates that the milch camels in which that deed was done were his own, not of charity; for charity was forbidden to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and to all of Banu Hashim. And in his family, for whom he supplicated to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to make thirsty whoever made them thirsty, is its clarification. So in that is what indicates that the camels were his, not of charity. And if one says: Is it allowed for the rulers after him to carry out punishments in the like of this on one who does it to their wealth, as they carry them out on one who does it to the wealth of others? Our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, is that for the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning is unlike the rulers after him; and that he may carry out the like of this on one who does it to his wealth as he carries it out on one who does it to the wealth of others; for what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do, he did by the command of Allah. So the Judge thereby over one to whom He does it is Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the one carrying it out by His command is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So to him belongs to do that by evidences and confessions together. As for others besides him of the rulers after him, it is otherwise in evidences; and they may not hear evidence to carry out a punishment on one who does to their wealth what necessitates that punishment; for it is not right for them to judge with those wealths for themselves against one in whose hand it is who claims it for himself besides them. But they may judge in that by confession against violators of that in their wealth who confess what they violated of that, and the obligatoriness of the punishment upon him therein, and their ownership of those wealths besides him. And like that is what Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, did about the bald man in whose case occurred in the house of Asma, his wife, what occurred. As:"
"2092","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Urwa ibn al-Zubayr informed him that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that a man born in captivity, bald, of the people of Mecca, used to serve Abu Bakr in his caliphate. He treated him kindly until Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent a collector of charity, and sent him with him, commending him to him. He stayed nearly a month, then came urging his camel on, cut by the collector. When Abu Bakr saw him, he said: Woe to you, what is with you? He said: O Abu Bakr, he found that I betrayed an obligatory share, so he cut my hand for it. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: May Allah destroy this one who cut your hand for an obligatory share you betrayed; by Allah, I see him betraying more than thirty obligatory shares. By the One in whose hand is my soul, if you are truthful, I shall retaliate you against him. So he stayed with Abu Bakr in the standing he had, praying at night. Abu Bakr would wake from his bed, and when he heard his recitation, his eyes overflowed, and he said: May Allah destroy the one who cut the hand of this one. She said: While we were upon that, Asma bint Umays was visited at night and her house was robbed. When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed the dawn prayer, he stood among the people and said: The quarter was visited last night and robbed; so disperse seeking your goods. She said: That amputee sought permission to us while I was sitting in the women's quarters, and said: O Abu Bakr, were you robbed last night? He said: Yes. He said: He raised his sound hand and his maimed hand and said: O Allah, expose the thief of Abu Bakr. She said: By Allah, the day had not risen before the stolen goods were taken from his house. Abu Bakr was brought to him, and he said to him: Woe to you, by Allah, you are not learned in Allah; take him away and cut him off. Abu Jafar said: Then one said: In this hadith is Abu Bakr cutting him without a confession from him of theft; so that proves that was from him by evidence he heard. And this is unlike what you held. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, is: What is in the hadith of the stolen thing being found in his house proves that he also confessed to stealing it, though one who reported the hadith did not transmit that to us. And we found that textually stated mentioned in a hadith not below that hadith. And it is what:"
"2093","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, that a man of the people of Yemen, amputated in hand and foot, came and stayed with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him. He complained to him that the governor of Yemen wronged him. He used to pray at night, and Abu Bakr would say: Your night is not the night of a thief. Then they missed jewelry of Asma bint Umays, wife of Abu Bakr. The man went about with them, saying: O Allah, against one who entered this righteous household by night. They found the jewelry with a goldsmith who claimed the amputee brought it to him. So the amputee confessed to it, or it was testified against him for it. So he ordered him, and his left hand was cut. And Abu Bakr said: By Allah, his supplication against himself is weightier with me than his theft. Then this speaker said: In this hadith is doubt about what he was cut for, of confession or testimony against him. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah and His aid, is: That doubt was only from some reporter of the hadith, and there is no verification in it that was by evidence testifying against him. So seeking the truth in that, what it is, is required."
"2094","So we found Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, saying: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim, that a man stayed with Abu Bakr, cut in hand and foot. He said: Who cut you? He said: The governor of Yemen. Abu Bakr said: If I overpower him... He began praying at night, and Abu Bakr said: Your night is not the night of a thief. They missed jewelry of Asma. He said: He began supplicating against whoever took it, and said: a righteous household. He said: They found it with a goldsmith; he pointed to him, so he confessed. Abu Bakr wanted to cut his foot, but they refused him, saying: You know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prescribed the hand after the foot. So he cut his hand. Abu Bakr said: His deception with Allah is weightier upon me than his theft. So we understood thereby that the truth was by the proof established against that thief of what was established against him: his confession, not evidence testifying against him thereby. And we thereby knew that the doubt in the first hadith was from below Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, and that it was from Malik, and that what al-Thawri had in that was preserving the truth therein, so he is more deserving of it than others. And in what we mentioned of that is what necessitates that for a ruler other than the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is carrying out punishments on violators of violated sanctities in his wealth who confess that, as he carries them out on one who violates them in the wealth of others. Then this speaker said: In this hadith the stolen jewelry therein is Asma's, not Abu Bakr's; so there is nothing in that to repel that Abu Bakr in that carried out the punishment by the testifying evidence with him of deserving that. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, is: That thing, though it was not Abu Bakr's, may Allah be pleased with him, was his wife's. And a man may not testify in the wealth of his wife for her, as he may not testify in his own wealth for himself."
"2095","And the proof of that is the saying of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, to Abdillah ibn Amr ibn al-Hadrami when he brought his slave to him and said: He stole something he mentioned for my wife. Umar said to him: No cutting upon him; your slave stole your wealth. Yunus narrated it to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from Abdillah ibn Amr ibn al-Hadrami. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, informed that the thief from the wealth of his wife, of one for whom there would be no cutting if he stole that from his wealth, since he was owned by him - there is no cutting upon him in it when he steals from the wealth of his wife. So in that is what proves that what the ruler may not do with a violator of sanctities in his wealth, he may not do the like of it with violators of sanctities from the wealth of his wife. And we ask Allah for success."
"2096","295 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: I was commanded to a town that consumes towns. 1825 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I heard Abu al-Hubab Said ibn Yasar say: I heard Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to a town that consumes towns. They say: Yathrib, and it is Medina; it expels people as the bellows expel the dross of iron."
"2097","1826 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: my uncle Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas informed me, that Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari narrated to them that Abu al-Hubab Said ibn Yasar narrated to him that he heard Abu Hurayra say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to a town that consumes towns. It is said of it: Yathrib, and it is Medina; it expels its dross as the bellows expel dross. Except that Malik said: Abu Hurayra said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I was commanded to a town, meaning I was commanded to emigrate to a town. And we found his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), consumes towns, meaning its people consume towns, as He, Mighty and Majestic, said: And Allah struck the example of a town that was secure and at rest, its provision coming to it abundantly from every place, but it denied the favors of Allah, so Allah made it taste the garment of hunger and fear for what they used to do, meaning: Allah struck the example of a town whose people were secure and at rest, and the mention of the town in this is a metonymy for its people, and its people are meant by what is mentioned therein, not it. And the proof of that is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: for what they used to do - and a town has no doing; and His saying: but it denied the favors of Allah - and a town has no denial; and His saying, Mighty and Majestic: so Allah made it taste the garment of hunger and fear - and a town tastes nothing of that; and His saying, Majestic and Mighty: And there came to them a messenger from among them - so that proves that what is meant by the town is its people, not the town itself. And likewise His saying in this hadith, consumes towns, is by this meaning. And the like of what the people of knowledge said in that is what Malik said in the interpretation of His saying: it expels people; that is, it expels its evil people as the bellows expel the dross of iron. So his saying: it expels people proves he meant: it expels its evil people, not all its people. And the like of that is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina: It is Tayba; it expels dross as fire expels the dross of silver. So likewise, his saying in this hadith: consumes towns is of this meaning. And we ask Allah for success."
"2098","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb said to us: I heard Malik say in the interpretation of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), consumes towns, he said: It conquers towns. So this agrees with what we mentioned in that of the interpretation upon which we interpreted the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith. And we ask Allah for success."
"2099","296 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the reason for which was revealed: Think not that those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they did not do. 1827 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Rafi ibn Khadij, that he and Zayd ibn Thabit were with Marwan ibn al-Hakam while he was governor of Medina. Marwan said to Rafi: About what was this verse revealed: Think not that those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they did not do, the verse. Rafi said: It was revealed about some hypocrites who, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a journey, stayed behind from him. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions came, they excused themselves and said: Nothing kept us from you but illness and occupation, and we wished we were with you. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed this verse about them. It is as if Marwan denied that and said: What is this? So Rafi was distressed by that and said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know what I say? Zayd said: Yes. When they left Marwan, Zayd said to him, joking with him: Will you not praise me as I bore witness for you? Rafi said: And where is this from this - should I praise you for bearing witness to the truth? Zayd said: Yes; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has praised for truth its people."
"2100","1828 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam informed me, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that men of the hypocrites in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to battle, stayed behind from him and rejoiced in their staying behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, they excused themselves to him and swore, and loved to be praised for what they did not do. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: Think not that those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they did not do, think them not safe from punishment."
"2101","1829 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka narrated to me that Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed him that Marwan said: Go, O Rafi, to Ibn Abbas and say: If every one of us who rejoices in what he did and loves to be praised for what he did not do is punished, we shall all be punished. Ibn Abbas said: What have you to do with this verse? This verse was revealed only about the People of the Book. Then Ibn Abbas recited: And when Allah took the covenant of those given the Book: you shall make it clear to people, the verse. Then Ibn Abbas recited: Think not that those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they did not do. Ibn Abbas said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked them about something, and they concealed it from him and informed him of other than it. So they left having shown him that they informed him of what he asked them about, and sought praise thereby from him, and rejoiced in what they did of concealing from him what he asked them about."
"2102","1830 - As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Abi Muhammad, freedman of Zayd ibn Thabit, that he narrated to him from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Finhas - who was of the scholars and rabbis of the Jews - Fear Allah and become Muslim; for by Allah, you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a messenger from Allah; he came to you with truth from Him; you find him written with you in the Torah and the Gospel. Finhas said: O Abu Bakr, by Allah, we have no need of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and He is in need of us. We do not implore Him as He implores us, and we are rich without Him. Had He been rich without us, He would not have asked us for our wealth as a loan, as your companion claims; he forbids you usury and gives it to us. Had He been rich without us, He would not have given us usury. So Abu Bakr became angry and struck the face of Finhas. Finhas informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: What carried you to what you did? He informed him, but Finhas denied that, saying: I did not say that. So Allah revealed: Allah has heard the saying of those who said: Allah is poor and we are rich, the verse, up to His saying, Mighty and Majestic: the punishment of the Blaze. And He revealed about Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and what reached him of that anger: And you shall hear from those given the Book before you and from those who associate partners much harm; but if you are patient and fearful, that is of resolute matters. And He said about what Finhas said, from their saying: Allah is poor, that they said it in concealing what they knew and in their disobedience, and that they love to be praised for what they did not do. So thereby we know that the verse: Think not that those who rejoice in what they have done, was revealed about the People of the Book, not about the hypocrites. And the two hadiths that came about the hypocrites are both from Zayd ibn Aslam, and they are interrupted, not connected: one from Rafi ibn Khadij, and Rafi did not witness its revelation, nor did Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and the other from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said, and Abu Said did not witness that either. So both hadiths are of what does not establish proof. And what Ibn Abbas narrated of its revelation about the People of the Book is connected and sounder. So it is more deserving to be held. And we ask Allah for success."
"2103","297 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Render the trust to one who entrusted you, and do not betray one who betrayed you. 1831 - Ahmad ibn Asram al-Muzani, then al-Maqili, Abu al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, he said: Sharik and Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Render the trust to one who entrusted you, and do not betray one who betrayed you."
"2104","1832 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad, that is al-Duri, narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn Ghannam narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, and he mentioned another, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith is what prevents one who has a debt on a man, and is entrusted by him with the like of it, or gains power over the like of it without his entrusting him, from taking it as settlement of his debt on him. Then one said to us: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned:"
"2105","1833 - Of what Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiya al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Hind, mother of Muawiya, said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Abu Sufyan is a miserly man, and he does not give me except that I take from his wealth secretly. He said: Take what suffices you and your children according to what is customary."
"2106","1834 - And of what Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, then he mentioned the same."
"2107","1835 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to me, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, he narrated to him that Hind bint Utba, mother of Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Abu Sufyan is a miserly, severe man, and he does not give me and my child except what I take from him without his knowledge. Is there anything upon me in that? He said: Take what suffices you and your children according to what is customary."
"2108","1836 - And of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman informed us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Urwa ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me that Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Hind bint Utba ibn Rabia came and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, there was no tent-dwellers on the face of the earth more beloved to me to be humbled than your tent-dwellers; then there came to be no tent-dwellers on the face of the earth more beloved to me to be honored than your tent-dwellers. Then she said: Abu Sufyan is a withholding man; is there any restriction upon me to feed from what is his our dependents? He said: There is no restriction upon you to feed them according to what is customary."
"2109","1837 - And of what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with its chain the same, except that he said: Is there any restriction upon me to spend on his dependents without his permission?"
"2110","1838 - And of what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Hind came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a withholding man; is there any sin upon me to spend on his dependents from his wealth without his permission? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sin upon you to spend on them according to what is customary. He said: So in this is the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Hind to take from the wealth of her husband Abu Sufyan without his permission what is due to her upon him of maintenance by the right of marriage between him and her, and to spend on his dependents from his wealth without his permission what is due upon him to them of maintenance according to what is customary. And this is contrary to what is in the first hadith. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah and His aid, is: What is in these hadiths does not contradict what is in the first hadith; for what is in the first hadith is only: Render the trust to one who entrusted you, and do not betray one who betrayed you. And what is in the other hadiths is the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allowing Hind to spend from her husband's wealth upon herself what he must spend upon her, and to convey to his dependents from it what he must spend upon them from his wealth. And whoever takes what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him to take is not a betrayer. So we understood thereby that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in each of the two reports we mentioned is other than what he meant in the other of them, and that whoever takes what he ordered him to take takes a right permitted to him, not a betrayal. And we ask Allah for success."
"2111","1839 - Of what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Shabi, from al-Miqdam Abu Karima al-Shami, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The night of the guest is a right upon every Muslim. If he wakes at his courtyard, it is a debt for him upon him; if he wishes he claims it, and if he wishes he leaves it. So in this hadith is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the right of the guest a debt for the hosted upon the one with whom he stayed."
"2112","1840 - And of what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth and Ibn Lahia informed me. And of what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us. Then they all agreed, saying: from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, you send us, and we stay with people who do not order for us the right of the guest. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you stay with people and they do not order for you the right of the guest, take it from their wealth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made in the first hadith the right of the guest a debt, and made in the second hadith, for one to whom it is due, taking it from the wealth of one upon whom it is due. So that agrees with what we validated of the first two meanings with which we began mentioning in this chapter. And we ask Allah for success."
"2113","298 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to like good omens. 1841 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurra ibn Abi Khalifa al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salama al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no contagion and no evil omen, but good omen pleases me. It was said: What is the omen? He said: The good word."
"2114","1842 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utba, that Abu Hurayra said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no evil omen, and the best of it is the good omen. It was said: What is the omen, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The righteous word one of you hears."
"2115","1843 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utba informed me, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2116","1844 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Maslama ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq Abu Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu Burayda, he said: Aisha was asked: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say about decree? She said: He used to say: Everything is by decree, and good omen used to please him."
"2117","1845 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq, from Yusuf ibn Abi Burda, from Abu Burda, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Birds run by decree, and good omen used to please him."
"2118","1846 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Amr al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utba, that Abu Hurayra said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say... then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Umayya from Yahya ibn Salih."
"2119","1847 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karima informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahim narrated to me, he said: Zayd, that is Ibn Abi Unaysa, narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Said, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no evil omen; the best of it is the good omen, the best of it is the good omen. Then one said: You reported to us earlier in this book of yours from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no evil omen, or that he said: Omen is polytheism. And in that is what proves that omen has no meaning. And when it has no meaning, being only of heard things and what resembles them of what people dislike, and when it is so, the loved of it is likewise: it only runs by the decree of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in it, not by other than it. And when it is so, the heard of it, disliked or loved, has no meaning. So whence is it allowed for you, with that, to attribute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that good omen used to please him, in which there is no benefit, nor harm in its opposite? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, is: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of what we reported from him, that good omen used to please him, was for other than what was imagined. That is because good speech is not taken as an evil omen by its hearers, as they take evil speech as an omen. So it pleased the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that there is no evil omen with it. And when its hearers count it as glad tidings from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to them, they praise Him for it. So this is of what pleases, not of what harms. And we ask Allah for success."
"2120","1848 - Of what Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out for a need, it used to please him to hear: O guided one, O successful one. So in that is: when one going out to a need hears it, he praises Allah for it and hopes thereby to reach his need by the favor of Allah upon him and His granting it to him."
"2121","1849 - And like that is what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abda ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a land called Ghadira and named it Khadira. And that from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was, in our view, from a dislike he removed upon its first name, that one staying it, with its name with him Ghadira, would take it as an evil omen. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed its name to Khadira, in which there is no evil omen. So it is clear, praise be to Allah, that there is no contradiction in anything we mentioned. And we ask Allah for success."
"2122","299 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his command for fever to be cooled with water: does he mean all waters, or does he mean a specific of them. 1850 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is of the blast of Hell; cool it with water."
"2123","1851 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, but he did not mention Aisha in it."
"2124","1852 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Ibrahim said: I heard nothing from Hisham except this hadith."
"2125","1853 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2126","1854 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwa."
"2127","1855 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from Fatima bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, that when a woman with fever was brought to her, she would call for her, take water and pour it between her and her breast pocket, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order us to cool it with water."
"2128","1856 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"2129","1857 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samura, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is a portion of the Fire; so cool it from you with water. And when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had fever, he would call for a waterskin of water and pour it over his head."
"2130","1858 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad al-Umari informed me, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is only of the blast of Hell; so cool it with water."
"2131","1859 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: So extinguish it with water."
"2132","1860 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Aisha narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, he said: Ibn Aisha said: Thus I noted it: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you has fever, let him sprinkle cold water upon himself at dawn three times."
"2133","1861 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Abaya ibn Rifaa, from his grandfather Rafi ibn Khadij: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Fever is a flare of Hell, or of its fire; so cool it with water. So the outward of what is in these hadiths is upon all waters. So we considered that to find the truth of the matter in it."
"2134","1862 - So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn al-Ward narrated to us, saying: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Jamra informed us, he said: I used to push the crowd from Ibn Abbas, and I was held back from him for days. He said to me: What held you back? I said: Fever. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fever is of the blast of Hell; so cool it with Zamzam water. He said: So we understood thereby that the water the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in the first hadiths is Zamzam water, not other waters. And what Abu Dharr reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirmed that with us."
"2135","1863 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us. And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us. Then both agreed: Abu Dawud said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, and Yazid said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira informed us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdillah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about Zamzam water: It is satisfying food and a cure for illness. So we understood thereby that his intent (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what we mentioned was toward Zamzam water, for the cure in it. And we ask Allah for success."
"2136","300 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from Abu Talha about his eating hail while fasting, and some raising that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his approving that of him. 1864 - Musa ibn al-Hasan al-Baghdadi, known as al-Saqalli, narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The sky rained hail, and Abu Talha said to us: Hand me some of this hail. He began eating while fasting, and that was in Ramadan. I said: Do you eat hail while fasting? He said: It is only hail descending from the sky; we purify our bellies with it, and it is neither food nor drink. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that. He said: Take it from your uncle. Then one said: How is it allowed for you to accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the Quran contradicts it; for Allah said: And eat and drink until the white thread becomes clear to you from the black thread of dawn, then complete the fast until night. So in that is what proves that there is no eating in fasting nor drinking. And in this hadith is that Abu Talha used to eat hail while fasting in Ramadan, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Anas to take it from his uncle, meaning Abu Talha. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah and His aid, is: We did not accept this hadith, since its raising to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is by Ali ibn Zayd, who is not of the firm in narration. And one more firm than him reported it from Anas without raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2137","Of what Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Nuh ibn Qays narrated to us, from his brother, from Qatada, from Anas, that Abu Talha used to eat hail while fasting, saying: It is neither food nor drink."
"2138","Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad, that is Ibn Salama, narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: Abu Talha used to eat hail while fasting. When asked about that, he said: A blessing upon a blessing in voluntary fasting. He said: So both agreed, by what we mentioned, that this hadith is not raised to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And it may be that Abu Talha used to do that before the revelation of this verse to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then when it was revealed, he went to what is in it and left what he was upon contradicting it. Then this speaker said: Is it allowed that this deed of Abu Talha was in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that of him was hidden from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah and His aid, is: That is of what may be: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may not have known of his deed to teach him what is obligatory upon him in it. And the like of this was in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Ansari mentioned to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, arguing with it against him about what they were upon of water. Umar uncovered from him about that: Did you mention it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he approved you upon it? He said: No. So Umar did not see that as a proof."
"2139","As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Idris informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq."
"2140","And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Mamar ibn Abi Huyayya, from Ubayd ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi, from his father, he said: I was sitting to the right of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man came to him and said: Zayd ibn Thabit gives people fatwa about washing from major impurity by his opinion. Umar said: Bring him to me quickly. Zayd came, and Umar said: Has your matter reached that you give people fatwa about washing from major impurity in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by your opinion? Zayd said: By Allah, O Commander of the Believers, I did not give fatwa by my opinion, but I heard something from my uncles and said it. He said: From which of your uncles? He said: From Ubayy ibn Kab and Abu Ayyub and Rifaa ibn Rafi. He turned to me, Umar, and said: What does this youth say? I said: We used to do it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then not wash. He said: Did you ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that? He said: No. Then Umar said at the end of the hadith: If I am told of one doing it then not washing, I shall punish him severely. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that this, in what Rifaa informed, was done in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then its doers did not wash, and that Umar did not see that as a proof, nor act by it, but rather lifted it and ordered us to act by its opposite; since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not known it from its doers to approve them upon it. So like that is what Abu Talha did in his hadith which we reported from him, of the hadith of Qatada and Thabit."
"2141","301 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: You have a treasure in Paradise, and you are its two-horned one; so do not follow the glance with the glance; for you have only the first, and the last is not for you. 1865 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari narrated to us, saying: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us. And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, all saying: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Salama ibn Abi al-Tufayl, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Ali, you have a treasure in Paradise, and you are its two-horned one; so do not follow the glance with the glance; for you have only the first, and the last is not for you. So people differed about what is meant by his saying: and you are its two-horned one. Some went to that he meant: you are its two-horned one, meaning: its two ends; since his mention of that followed his mention of Paradise. And some went to that he meant: you are the two-horned one of this community, suppressing the community, like His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And if Allah were to take people for what they earned, He would not leave upon its back a creature, and in another place: He would not leave upon it a creature, meaning the earth, though He did not mention it before that; and like His saying, Mighty and Majestic: until it disappeared behind the veil, meaning the sun, suppressing it; then like the saying of people: there is none in it - meaning the town or the city - more learned than so-and-so."
"2142","By what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Dawud al-Khuraybi narrated to us, from Bassam al-Sayrafi, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood upon the pulpit and said: Ask me before you cannot ask me; and you shall not ask after me one like me. Ibn al-Kawwa stood to him and said: What was Dhu al-Qarnayn? Was he a king or a prophet? He said: He was neither a prophet nor a king, but he was a righteous servant; he loved Allah and Allah loved him, and he was sincere to Allah and Allah was sincere to him. He was struck on his right horn and died, then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, resurrected him, then he was struck on his left horn and died. And among you is one like him. And of those who held this saying was Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam; Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated that to me from him. And Ali and Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me that they heard Ubaydillah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, meaning Ibn Aisha, and he was asked about this hadith: you are its two-horned one, and he said: He meant: you are its ram and its horseman. Then one said: In the hadith of Ali which you reported: and among you is one like him - so what is meant by that of what Dhu al-Qarnayn was made an example in? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His aid, is: Meant thereby is that he is like Dhu al-Qarnayn in his calling to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and in his standing with truth, calling and standing until the Day of Resurrection, as Dhu al-Qarnayn was in what he called to and what he stood with, calling with it and standing until the Day of Resurrection. And things may be likened to things for their likeness to them in a meaning, though they do not resemble them in other than it, like the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Allah who created seven heavens and of the earth the like of them - not that they are like them in that they are heavens, but that they are like them in number. And like His saying, Mighty and Majestic: O you who believe, be helpers of Allah as Jesus son of Mary said to the disciples: who are my helpers to Allah - the disciples were not prophets like Jesus, but they were like him in calling to Allah. So likewise, what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, meant by his saying: and among you is one like him, is in calling to Allah and in standing with truth until the Day of Resurrection. And we ask Allah for success."
"2143","1866 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Abu Rabia al-Iyadi, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do not follow the glance with the glance; the first is for you and the last is against you."
"2144","1867 - And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Rabia al-Iyadi, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, raising it, the same, but he did not mention Ali in its chain. And like that also is the hadith of Jarir ibn Abdillah al-Bajali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning."
"2145","1868 - And as Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Amr ibn Said, from Abu Zura ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Jarir ibn Abdillah, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the sudden glance, and he said: Turn away your sight."
"2146","1869 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Zuray, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"2147","1870 - And as Abu al-Awwam Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid and Abu Shihab al-Hannat narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"2148","1871 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayya narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, then he mentioned with its chain the same. So these reports have come about the glance we mentioned therein initially, and about the glance after it, with some confirming others. And Allah knows best what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in that, and Him we ask for success."
"2149","302 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Your oath is upon what your companion confirms you in. 1872 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your oath is upon what your companion confirms you in. Abu Jafar said: We do not know this hadith reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a better channel than this channel. As for what was reported from him from a channel below this channel:"
"2150","1873 - Of what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Hisham al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali ibn Muqaddam narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Said ibn Abi Said, he said: I heard my grandfather Abu Said al-Maqburi narrate from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your oath is upon what your companion confirms you in. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith to find what is meant by it, God willing. The best that occurred in it is that the oath meant therein - and Allah knows best - may be the obligatory oath in a claim which one may deny and repel from himself and swear upon it. Of that is a man who owns a thing, and a man turns over upon it in his sleep and destroys it without the sleeper knowing that, with the owner of that thing witnessing that from him in his thing. So the owner of the thing is at liberty to claim what is due in that against that sleeper, and the sleeper is at liberty to repel that from himself; for he does not know its obligatoriness upon him, and at liberty to swear upon what is claimed against him of that, since he did not know it from himself. And it is of the right of one who claims that against him to make him swear upon it, since it is due to him in truth. And the claimed-against is at liberty to swear upon that, since he does not know its obligatoriness upon him. Yet the obligation upon him in that is that his oath in outward be as it is in inward, without equivocation from him therein. And that is unlike what is claimed against him of what he knows in truth that he is wronged in what is claimed against him of it, in which he is at liberty to equivocate his oath upon that to repel thereby what its claimant intends of him of what he wrongs him in of that, as Suwayd did in the hadith we shall mention."
"2151","1874 - As Imran ibn Musa al-Tai Abu al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Israil ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from his grandmother, from her father Suwayd ibn Hanzala, he said: We went out intending the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with us was Wail ibn Hujr. An enemy of his seized him, and people scrupled to swear, but I swore that he is my brother, so he released him. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, saying: They scrupled to swear, so I swore: he is my brother, and he released him. He said: You spoke truthfully; the Muslim is the brother of the Muslim. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that Suwayd's oath to the enemy of Wail ibn Hujr that he is his brother was to release him from him, and that was from the enemy of Wail wrongdoing to Wail. So Suwayd was at liberty to swear upon what repels from Wail what his enemy intended of him, until that was the cause of his rescue from his hand, and until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised Suwayd for it. So the validation of the hadith of Abu Hurayra and the hadith of Suwayd is what we carried each of them upon and interpreted therein, until each of them came out from the other without contradiction. And we ask Allah for success."
"2152","303 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his selling a free man when he found no wealth for him to settle that debt from. 1875 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith al-Tannuri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, he said: I met a man at Alexandria called Surraq. I said to him: What is this name? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me it. I entered Medina and informed them that wealth would come to me, so they traded with me. I consumed their wealth. They came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: He is Surraq. So he sold me for four camels. His creditors said to him: What will you do with him? He said: Free him. They said: We are not less desirous of the Hereafter than you. So they freed me. Abu Jafar said: Muslim ibn Khalid reported this hadith and inserted in its chain, between Zayd ibn Aslam and Surraq, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani."
"2153","1876 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, he said: I was in Egypt, and a man said to me: Shall I guide you to a man of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He pointed to a man, so I came to him and said: Who are you, may Allah have mercy on you? He said: I am Surraq. I said: Glory be to Allah, you should not be named with this name while you are a man of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named me Surraq, and I shall never leave that. I said: Why did he name you Surraq? He said: I met a man of the desert people with two camels of his selling them, so I bought them from him, saying: Come with me until I give you. I entered my house, then went out from behind me, spent the price of the two camels on my need, and hid until I thought the Bedouin had left. I went out, and the Bedouin was staying. He seized me and brought me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I informed him of the news. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What carried you to what you did? I said: I spent their price on my need, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then settle it. I said: I have nothing. He said: You are Surraq. Go, O Bedouin, sell him until you take your right. People began bidding for me by him, and he would turn to them saying: What do you want? They would say: We want to buy him from you. He said: By Allah, none of you needs him more than I. Go, for I have freed you. Abu Jafar said: Then one said: What you reported of this hadith is either established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or it is not established. If it is established, then in it is the selling of a free man for a debt upon him, and that is contrary to what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of stern threat against one who sells a free man."
"2154","1877 - As Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Ubaydillah al-Ansari al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani narrated to us, from Abu Umama al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day to his companions: Shall I not narrate to you about al-Khidr? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: While he was one day walking in the market of the Children of Israel, a mukatab man saw him and said: Give charity to me, may Allah bless you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah; what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, wills of a matter shall be. I have nothing to give you. The poor man said: I ask you by the face of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to give charity to me; I saw the mark of good in your face, and I hope for blessing with you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah; I have nothing to give you except that you take me and sell me. The poor man said: Can this be right? He said: Yes, truly I tell you; you asked me by a great matter. I shall not disappoint you by the face of my Lord. So sell me. He brought him to the market and sold him for four hundred dirhams. He stayed with the buyer a time, who did not employ him in anything. Al-Khidr said: You bought me only seeking my good, so commend me to work. He said: I dislike to burden you; you are an old man. He said: It does not burden me. He said: Then stand and carry these stones - and none would carry them in a day but six men. The man went out to relieve himself, then returned, and he had carried the stones in his hour. He said to him: You did well and beautifully, and bore what I did not see you bear. Then travel came upon the man, and he said: I count you trustworthy, so take my place among my family with a good deputyship. He said: Commend me to work. He said: I dislike to burden you. He refused but to commend him. He said: Then plaster this house, and do not mix mud over the water until I return. The man went out on his travel, and al-Khidr plastered and mixed and built well. When his master came, he thanked him, freed him, and said: I ask you by the face of Allah: who are you? He said: I ask you by the face of Allah not to ask me; I am a man whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made to stand with His right when He asked. Abu Jafar said: So in what we mentioned of this hadith, from al-Khidr selling himself to that poor man for what he asked him by the face of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, of charity, making himself thereby his price, is proof that Surraq selling himself is not of what contradicts the threat reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). For that threat, in our view - and Allah knows best - is about one who sells a free man wrongfully, enslaving him for his own benefit. As for one who sells himself, or one whom another sells by his permission for his need, that is not included in the threat. For the threat is only upon one who sells free men and consumes their price. And of that is what Medina judges use as proof for one who owes debts and has no wealth: that the judge may hire him out until he settles his debt from his hire. And that is a meaning we mention in the chapter after this chapter, God willing."
"2155","1878 - As Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayya, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three, I am their adversary on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever I am adversary of, I overcome him: a man who gives by Me then betrays, and a man who sells a free man and consumes his price, and a man who hires a worker and takes his full due from him without giving him his wage. Abu Jafar said: In that is the prohibition of the prices of free men in all ways. And in what we mentioned is the establishment of proof for us in our leaving what we reported at the start of this chapter, of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we reported therein, to what Allah abrogated in His Book of what He revealed therein of what we recited upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we reported. And we ask Allah for success."
"2156","304 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports people differed about, of the insolvent debtor upon him: is he hired out in that until he settles his debt from his hire, or not? And was anything reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that or not? Abu Jafar said: We know of none of the people of knowledge who held hiring out the indebted who has nothing until he settles his debt from his hire, except Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri; for he used to hold that. And I know only that I took that from his saying, from Harun ibn Kamil, from Abdillah ibn Salih, from al-Layth ibn Sad, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what repels that and contradicts it. 1879 - As Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. And as al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: as for al-Rabi, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, and as for Muhammad ibn Abdillah, he said: my father and Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed us. And as Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Bajali narrated to us, all saying: al-Layth narrated to us. And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me. Then Amr and al-Layth agreed, saying: from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Iyad ibn Abdillah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man was afflicted in fruits he bought, and his debt became much. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give charity to him. So charity was given to him, but that did not reach settling his debt. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take what you find; you have only that. So in what we reported of this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his saying to the creditors of the debtor mentioned therein, after people gave charity to him with what they gave him to settle his debt: Take what you find, and you have only that. And in that is what repels that they may hire him out to take their debts from his hire. And we ask Allah, Exalted, for success."
"2157","305 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about racing with what does not count. 1880 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyayna, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, she said: I raced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and outran him. When I carried flesh, I raced him and he outran me, and said: This is for that."
"2158","1881 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salama, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the last Badr expedition, until when we were at al-Uthayl near al-Safra, I turned aside for a need, away from the road. While I was so, a rider prodded, and it was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I finished my need, then came. He said: Come, I race you. She said: I threw my cloak behind my back, then put its end in my waistband, then drew a line with my foot, then said: Come, we stand upon this line. He looked at my face as if amazed. We stood upon that line. She said: I said: Go. He said: Go you. We set out, and he outran me, ahead of me. He said: This is for the day of Dhi al-Majaz. I remembered what the day of Dhi al-Majaz was: he came while I was a girl, my father following me; something was in my hand, and he asked me for it, but I refused. He went to take it, so I fled, and he went after me. I outran him and entered the house. So in this hadith is the permission of racing on feet. And it has been reported from Salama ibn al-Akwa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in this meaning."
"2159","1882 - Of what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to me, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salama, from his father, he said: We came with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from al-Hudaybiya, and he mounted me behind him returning to Medina upon his camel al-Adba. When between us and Medina was a push, and among us was a man of the Ansar unbeaten in running, he said: Is there a racer to Medina - he said it repeatedly while I was silent. I said: You honor no generous nor fear a noble. He said: No, unless it be the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to race him. He said: If you wish, do. I said: Away to you. He set out running, and I leapt from the camel running, tying upon me one or two honors. He asked me what I tied? He said: I spared myself. Then I went until I caught him, striking between his shoulders, saying: I outran you, by Allah. He looked at me and laughed. And thereby Muhammad ibn al-Hasan used to say. Some people went to other than that, that there is no racing except in hoof or sole, and they argued in that."
"2160","1883 - By what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Abbad ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no prize except in hoof or sole."
"2161","1884 - And by what Abd al-Malik al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu al-Hakam al-Laythi, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2162","1885 - And by what Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abu Zura narrated to us, he said: Haywa narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Salih, freedman of the Junda'is, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No prize is lawful except upon sole or hoof."
"2163","1886 - And by what Muhammad also narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Layth. And by what Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Abdillah, freedman of the Junda'is, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2164","1887 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Hakam al-Laythi narrated to me, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same. And others went to other than that also, saying: There is no prize except in arrowhead or hoof or sole. And they argued in that."
"2165","1888 - By what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Nafi ibn Abi Nafi, he informed him, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no prize except in arrowhead or hoof or sole."
"2166","1889 - And of what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Maslama narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Nafi ibn Abi Nafi, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"2167","1890 - And of what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the same."
"2168","1891 - And by what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. And by what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the same. 1892"
"2169","1892 - And by what Ahmad ibn Amr al-Makki al-Khallal narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Nafi ibn Abi Nafi, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Jafar said: In these are three sayings said in this chapter. The people of the second saying and the people of the third saying argued, with what is in their reports they argued with for their saying, from the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negating prize except by what he permitted in their reports we mentioned in the section in which we mentioned their two sayings. And the people of the first saying argued against the people of these two sayings with the two hadiths of Aisha. Of the proof of the people of these two sayings against them was that in their reports they reported, of their saying, is what necessitates negating racing on feet. Of the proof of the people of the first saying against them was that that would only be so if we knew that what is in the reports they reported, of negating racing on feet, was after what Aisha reported in that. And it may be that what Aisha reported in that was after what is in their reports, so that would join with what is in their reports, making prize only upon feet, and upon hoof, and upon sole, and by arrowhead. And since we knew from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the permission of racing on feet, we should not repel it, nor remove it from its cause, since we do not know that he repelled it nor removed it from them. So thereby the use of what the people of the first saying said in this chapter is necessary, since no proof stands against them necessitating repelling what they said therein. And we ask Allah for success."
"2170","306 - Chapter explaining the problematic reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: There is no jolting nor turning aside. 1893 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Qazaa, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no jolting nor turning aside."
"2171","1894 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr Abu Umayr narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"2172","1895 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from Thabit, from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Jafar said: This is a practice the Basrans alone transmitted; we know of no people of the lands of the Muslims besides them reporting it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from an accepted channel, nor do we know anyone besides them reporting it from any channel, though impugned therein, other than the people of Medina."
"2173","1896 - For Imran ibn Musa al-Tai narrated to us, saying: Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, from Kathir ibn Abdillah al-Muzani, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no jolting nor turning aside. Abu Jafar said: There is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that what is meant thereby is the prohibition of these two meanings mentioned in these reports, in racing with what racing is allowed with."
"2174","And there has been reported in that from Malik and from al-Layth ibn Sad. Of what Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas was asked: Did you hear that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no jolting nor turning aside? And what is its interpretation? He said: That did not reach me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And its interpretation is that one jolts behind the horse when it turns away, stirring behind it something to urge it on, so it outruns. That is the jolting. And the turning aside is that another horse is led beside the horse raced with, until when near the goal, its rider shifts to the led horse."
"2175","And of what Yunus mentioned, from Ibn Wahb, he said: al-Layth said in the interpretation of There is no jolting: that one jolts behind the horse in racing. And the turning aside is that one be beside him calling out to him for racing. And we know of no saying in that other than these two sayings we mentioned, in these two reports. As for the jolting, Malik and al-Layth agreed upon what is meant by it; each of them said therein, in these two reports, what we mentioned from him therein. And obligatory in that is to use both interpretations together, so their user encompasses knowledge that he did not enter into what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him. And we ask Allah, Exalted, for success."
"2176","Chapter 307: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding entering a horse between two horses in a race when it is one whose outrunning is feared. 1897 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever enters a horse between two horses while it is not feared that it will be outrun, there is no harm; and whoever enters a horse between two horses while it is feared that it will be outrun, that is gambling."
"2177","1898 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam and Marwan ibn Muawiyah al-Fazari and Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: The intended meaning in this hadith, and Allah knows best, is that two men race with two horses and enter between them an intruding third horse and place a prize between them; that intruder the Arabs call muhallil. The first two place two stakes and the muhallil places nothing, then they release the three horses: if one of the first two outruns, he takes his companion's stake along with his own stake as lawful for him; if the muhallil outruns while neither of the first two outruns, he takes both stakes together as lawful for him; and if he is outrun, nothing is due from him to the first two. And we considered the meaning of his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): if it is not feared that it will outrun, there is no harm in it, and if it is feared that it will outrun, there is no good in it. We found the people of knowledge unanimously agree that what is meant is the slow horse which is feared to be outrun. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and more than one explaining this explanation, and likewise Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Abbas interpreted to us, from Musa ibn Nasr, from Hisham ibn Ubayd Allah, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan in his narrations which he interpreted to us from him. And he informed us that he heard it from Musa, and that Musa narrated to them that it was from Hisham, from Muhammad with these meanings, and that he did not relate to them any disagreement between him and any of his companions about it. Abu Jafar said: The intruding horse is placed in the legal status of the two racers themselves without an intruder between them with a stake they place between them: if the horse on his side outruns, it is secured for him with nothing due to the outrun one; and if the horse not his outruns, he takes that stake as lawful and good; and if the wager between them was that if he outruns he pays something named to his companion, that is gambling and not lawful. He applied this same meaning to the intruding muhallil between them: if he outruns both wagering parties, both stakes are lawful for him, and if he is outrun, nothing is due from him to his two companions or to either of them. Abu Jafar said: And there has been reported about wagering from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one single hadith; we do not know any other reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about wagering."
"2178","1899 - And it is what Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit narrated to me, he said: Abu Labid narrated to us, he said: The horses were released in the time of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf while al-Hakam ibn Ayyub was governor over Basra; when we returned from the wager we said: If only we turned to Anas ibn Malik and asked him whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wager on horses? He said: So Anas was asked about that, and he said: Yes, by Allah, he certainly wagered on a horse of his called Sabhah, and it outran the people, so he rejoiced at that and was pleased with it. Abu Jafar said: This is also from the hadith of the Basrans, even though Said ibn Zayd is not strong in his narration according to the people of isnad. As for racing without mention of a wager in it, authentic reports have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2179","1900 - Among them is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him. And what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Malik, from Nafi, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raced between the horses that had been made lean from al-Hafya, and their finish was Thaniyyat al-Wada, and he raced between the horses that had not been made lean from al-Thaniyyah to the mosque of Banu Zurayq, and Abd Allah ibn Umar was among those who raced them."
"2180","1901 - And among them is what al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raced between horses; he sent what had been made lean of them from al-Hafya to Thaniyyat al-Wada, and what had not been made lean from Thaniyyat al-Wada to the mosque of Banu Zurayq."
"2181","1902 - And among them is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a she-camel called al-Adba which was not outrun; then a Bedouin came on a young camel of his and raced her, and it outran her, and that weighed heavily on the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a right upon Allah that He does not raise anything in this world except that He lowers it."
"2182","1903 - And among them is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a she-camel called al-Adba which was never outrun; then a Bedouin came on a young camel of his and outran her, and that grieved the Muslims. When he saw what was in their faces, they said: O Messenger of Allah, al-Adba has been outrun. He said: It is indeed a right upon Allah, mighty and majestic, that He does not raise anything of this world except that He lowers it. And from Allah we ask success."
"2183","Chapter 308: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy. 1904 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, from Abu Usamah, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, fearing that the enemy would get hold of it."
"2184","1905 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn al-Furat narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, he said: Yahya ibn Said said: Nafi informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, fearing that the enemy would get hold of it."
"2185","1906 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the like of it."
"2186","1907 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us. And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"2187","1908 - And what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Abu Salih narrated to us, they said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. He said: So in this hadith was the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to travel with the Quran to the land of the enemy, and joined to his forbidding that was fear that the enemy would get hold of it. So it was possible that this was from the speech of Ibn Umar, or from the speech of Nafi his freed slave, not from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we investigated that to arrive at the truth of the matter, by the success granted by Allah, mighty and majestic."
"2188","1909 - So we found that al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not travel with the Quran to the land of the enemy, for I fear that the enemy will get hold of it. Abu Jafar said: And Ayyub in our view, and Allah knows best, is not the Ayyub from whom Shubah narrated this hadith from Nafi; rather he is Ayyub ibn Musa al-Umawi, while the one from whom Shubah narrated is Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani."
"2189","1910 - And we found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah and Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not travel with the Quran to the land of the enemy, for I fear that the enemy will get hold of it. And one who supposed supposed that between Abu Ishaq al-Fazari and Ismail ibn Umayyah in this hadith was Sufyan al-Thawri, but it is not as he supposed, since we found it in other than the narration of Muawiyah, from Abu Ishaq, just as in the narration of Muawiyah, from Abu Ishaq."
"2190","1911 - As Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: al-Musayyib ibn Wadih narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah and Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. And we accepted al-Musayyib in this hadith, even though the people of knowledge of isnad criticize him, so that it be verified that there is no intruder between Abu Ishaq and Ismail in this chain. And what is in these hadiths of Ayyub ibn Musa and Ismail ibn Umayyah and Layth ibn Abi Sulaym established with us that the fear expressed in these hadiths for the Quran, that the enemy would get hold of it so that travelling with it to their land was forbidden because of it, is from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not from anyone else among the narrators of these hadiths."
"2191","Chapter 309: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about withdrawal (azl) and that it is hidden infanticide, and about what was reported from him denying whoever said that. 1912 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Judhamah narrated to me, she said: Withdrawal was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: That is hidden infanticide."
"2192","1913 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zura al-Hajari narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad that he heard Urwah narrating from Aishah, from Judhamah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"2193","1914 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2194","1915 - And what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And in it he said Judamah with a dal. Then a questioner said: What is in these reports which you narrated, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deemed withdrawal as he deemed it in them, yet you have narrated from him what contradicts that?"
"2195","1916 - So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Rifah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a slave girl and I practice withdrawal with her, and I dislike that she become pregnant, while I desire what men desire; and the Jews say it is the lesser infanticide. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Jews have lied; if Allah, mighty and majestic, willed to create him, you could not avert it."
"2196","1917 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Jazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Muti ibn Rifah, from Abu Said, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"2197","1918 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ayyash ibn Uqbah al-Hadrami informed me, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the Jews say withdrawal is the lesser infanticide, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Jews have lied. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you ejaculated, it would be only by decree."
"2198","1919 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: I kept a slave girl of mine at the market of Banu Qaynuqa; a Jew passed by me and said: What is this slave girl? I said: A slave girl of mine. He said: Did you have intercourse with her? I said: Yes. He said: Perhaps in her belly is a lamb of yours. I said: I used to practice withdrawal with her. He said: That is the lesser infanticide. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The Jews have lied, the Jews have lied. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: It is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what we mentioned from him in the first section of this chapter because he followed the Jews in their law for what Allah had not yet abrogated in his law, since they were People of the Book following the one who brought their Book, and since Allah, mighty and majestic, had revealed to him: Those are they whom Allah guided, meaning those of His prophets before him, so follow their guidance. Rather he reached that from what he found in the Torah and in the other Books of Allah revealed to His prophets before him, blessings of Allah upon him and upon them. So it is possible that when he questioned them how it is in their Book, they told him it is the lesser infanticide but belied him, so he said what Judhamah narrates from him. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, informed him of their lying, and that the matter in truth is otherwise, just as when he asked them about the hadd of zina in their Book they told him it is flogging and disgrace with no stoning in it, and they brought the Torah, and one of them placed his hand over the verse of stoning until Abd Allah ibn Salam informed him that they had belied him, and he ordered that Jew to lift his hand from the verse of stoning, so he lifted it, and the proof stood against them that stoning is in their Book. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then stoned whoever of them committed zina among those brought to him seeking his judgment. Like that is what happened with them about withdrawal: when Allah, mighty and majestic, made clear to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) their lying in that, he made clear to his community (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) their lying in it. And He revealed to him in His Book what clarified in what infanticide applies, namely His statement, mighty and majestic: And We created man from an extract of clay, up to His statement: Then We produced him as another creation, so blessed is Allah, the best of creators. So He informed him thereby of the time at which the created from the sperm has life in it, so it is possible to bury it alive then and it be dead; but before that it is not alive, it is like the rest of things in which there is no life, so it is impossible that what is like that be buried alive. And there was from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, an address to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, on this meaning which we have mentioned."
"2199","As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Mamar ibn Abi Huyyah, he said: I heard Ubayd Allah ibn Rifah al-Ansari say: The companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discussed withdrawal before Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and differed about it. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You have differed while you are the people of Badr, the chosen; so how will it be with the people after you, when two men whispered together. So Umar said: What is this whispering? He said: The Jews claim it is the lesser infanticide. So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It is not infanticide until it passes through the seven stages: And We created man from an extract of clay, to the end of the verse. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, marvelled at his statement and said: May Allah reward you with good."
"2200","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, Mamar ibn Abi Huyyah narrated to me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, he said: The companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discussed withdrawal before Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of it exactly, except that he did not mention in it his statement: So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, marvelled at his statement and said: May Allah reward you with good. Abu Jafar said: So this from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, is a sound derivation on this meaning. And this speech has also been narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2201","As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu al-Waddak, that some people asked Ibn Abbas about withdrawal, and he mentioned the like of the speech of Ali in the first two hadiths exactly."
"2202","And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sharik narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abbas, that some people of Iraq came to him asking him about withdrawal, considering it infanticide. So he said to his slave girls: Tell them how I act; they seemed shy, so he said: I pour it into the basin, then pour water over it, then say to one of them: Look, lest you say if there is anything. Then he said: It is a sperm drop, then blood, then a clot, then a lump, then it becomes bone, then it is clothed with flesh, then it becomes what Allah wills until the soul is breathed into it. Then he recited this verse: Then We produced him as another creation, so blessed is Allah, the best of creators. Abu Jafar said: So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became aware of the lying of the Jews in what they had said about withdrawal and its impossibility, he belied them in it and informed the people that nothing will be, whether they practice withdrawal or not, except what Allah, mighty and majestic, decreed in it of a child coming from it or not coming from it. And in what we mentioned of this is sufficiency for what we needed this discussion for. And from Allah we ask success."
"2203","Chapter 310: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about exception in oaths with if Allah wills. 1920 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: We read to al-Shafii, from Sufyan, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears an oath and says: If Allah wills, he has made exception."
"2204","1921 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed me, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Thus he dictated it to us; then I heard him afterwards in study mentioning it from Sufyan himself. So I said to him: You only dictated it to us from Ibn Wahb, from Sufyan. So he said: And I heard it from Sufyan. So I said to him: It is not in your book from Sufyan. So he said: I know that; I had another book from Sufyan with this hadith in it, but it burned. So we understood thereby that Ayyub the narrator of this hadith is Ayyub ibn Musa."
"2205","1922 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When he swears then says: If Allah wills, he has the choice."
"2206","1923 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: He said: If Allah wills, he has made exception. Abu Jafar said: And this Ayyub is Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, and Allah knows best."
"2207","1924 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Kathir ibn Farqad that he narrated to him that Nafi narrated to them, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears an oath and says: If Allah wills, then he has respite. Then a questioner said: You narrated this hadith as you narrated it, while you say that the exception mentioned in it is what is joined to the oath, not what is cut off from it; what is your proof for what you said? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: This hadith revolves upon Abd Allah ibn Umar, and we have narrated from him from his own statement."
"2208","What Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Whoever swears an oath and says right after it: If Allah wills, then if he does not do what he swore upon, he has not broken his oath."
"2209","And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Musa known as Ibn Bint al-Suddi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: There is no breaking in an oath joined at its end with if Allah wills. So we deemed it impossible that Abd Allah ibn Umar, with his merit, piety and knowledge, would restrict what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made general to something specific, except by what obliges him to restrict it. Then this questioner said: There has been narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas what contradicts what you narrated from Ibn Umar about it."
"2210","And he mentioned what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from Yala ibn Muslim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas about the hadith of the Companions of the Cave: And remember your Lord when you forget, Ibn Abbas said: When you say something and do not say if Allah wills, then say when you remember: If Allah wills. So my answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: What he mentioned from Ibn Abbas does not contradict what we mentioned from Ibn Umar; because what we mentioned from Ibn Umar is about oaths, and what he mentioned from Ibn Abbas is about things a man says he will do in the future, which he must refer to the will of Allah, mighty and majestic, since he may die before that or be cut off from it by an interrupter. If he deliberately does not do that, he is not praiseworthy for leaving it; and if he forgetfully does not do it, he says it when he remembers, so it joins his first speech. And the proof has been established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) necessitating in oaths what Ibn Umar said about them, namely his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever swears an oath then sees another better than it, let him do what is better and expiate for his oath, or let him expiate for his oath and do what is better, according to what has been narrated in that which we shall mention later in its place in this book of ours, if Allah wills. So we understood thereby that his attaching things with if Allah wills to his preceding oath is not possible; for if he were able to do that, he would not need breaking and expiation, or expiation and breaking, but would say: If Allah wills, and return to the ruling as if he had said it joined to his oath. And in that is a clear proof for what Ibn Umar said about it. As for what is intended in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, among it is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the story of Sulayman ibn Dawud (peace be upon him)."
"2211","1925 - As al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz that he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reporting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Sulayman ibn Dawud (peace be upon him) said: I shall certainly go around tonight to a hundred women, or ninety-nine women, each of them bringing a horseman striving in the path of Allah. His companion said to him: Say: If Allah wills. But he did not say: If Allah wills. So none of them conceived except one woman, with half a man. By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, had he said: If Allah wills, they would all have striven in the path of Allah as horsemen."
"2212","1926 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud had sixty women, so he said: I shall go around them tonight, and every woman of them will conceive a boy, a horseman fighting in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic. So he went around them, and none of them conceived except one, who gave birth to half a human. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had he made exception, every woman of them would have conceived a boy, a horseman fighting in the path of Allah. Abu Jafar said: And the omission of Sulayman ibn Dawud (peace be upon him) to say: If Allah wills, after the prompting of the one who prompted him, may have been due to an interrupter that cut him off from that, or due to a shortcoming he heard in that from the one who prompted him. And Abu Hurayrah narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about exception in oaths as Ibn Umar narrated from him."
"2213","1927 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Nuh ibn Habib informed us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears an oath and says: If Allah wills, he has made exception. And its aspect in our view, and Allah knows best, is like the aspect you mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Umar. And from Allah we ask success."
"2214","Chapter 311: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about oaths joined one to another sealed with if Allah wills: does that make exception in all of them or exception only in the last oath of them? 1928 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maktum narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Misar, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, then he said things, then he said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, then he said: If Allah wills, then he said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, then he said: If Allah wills. Abu Jafar said: And Ibrahim ibn Maktum who narrated this hadith is Basran who moved to Baghdad and narrated there; he is with the people of hadith trustworthy and known."
"2215","1929 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, but he did not mention Ibn Abbas. So thus Misar narrated this hadith with the exception from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for every oath of the oaths mentioned in it. And Sharik ibn Abd Allah al-Nakhai narrated it otherwise."
"2216","1930 - As Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, by Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, then he said in the third: If Allah wills."
"2217","1931 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Prepare ghusl for me; so he prepared it, then he said: Turn your back to me; so he turned his back to him, and he bathed. Then he said: By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, by Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, by Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, if Allah wills. If this hadith in truth is as Misar narrated it, then its meaning is open and needs no uncovering; but if it is as Sharik narrated it, then it needs uncovering. So we looked at that and found that Allah, mighty and majestic, said to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And do not say of anything: I shall do that tomorrow, except if Allah wills. And it was counted among what the speaker of this speech may attain, and may be cut short before it; so he was commanded to say with this: If Allah wills, out of sincerity to Allah, mighty and majestic, and leaving intrusion upon His unseen, even if that speech is among what Allah, mighty and majestic, caused to flow upon his tongue. And what is like that, employing sincerity to Allah, mighty and majestic, in it is more deserving, as He, mighty and majestic, said: You shall surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, secure. That was among what must come to be, since Allah, mighty and majestic, had promised them it; yet He, mighty and majestic, said in it: If Allah wills. And in that is proof that people in what they say about future matters, among what they know must come to be and what may or may not be, are commanded to join them with the will of Allah, mighty and majestic, sincerely to Him, mighty and majestic, and submitting matters to Him; and likewise all matters. So those swearing about them, when they are about future matters, should join them with if Allah wills. If a questioner says: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept away from his wives without any statement from him in it: If Allah wills, until he was thereby one keeping away from them. It is said to him: That from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have been before Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed to him: And do not say of anything: I shall do that tomorrow, except if Allah wills. And from Allah, exalted, we ask success."
"2218","Chapter 312: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating the correct view in what the people of knowledge differed about regarding exception in oaths when mention of divorce is brought forward or delayed in them: are they equal or otherwise? Abu Jafar said: The people of knowledge equated these two meanings and did not differentiate between them except Shurayh the judge; for he used to differentiate between them, saying: When divorce is brought forward in them, it becomes binding and the respite does not benefit, as a man saying to his wife: You are divorced if you enter the house; he deemed her divorced now though she had not entered the house. And he differentiated between that and his statement: When you enter the house, you are divorced; about this he said as the rest of the people of knowledge say: She is not divorced until she enters the house. And what was narrated from him in that is what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, he said: Whoever begins with divorce has no respite."
"2219","And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, the like of it. He said: And Ibrahim said: And what does Shurayh know?"
"2220","And what Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from al-Shabi, from Shurayh, the like of it. And what Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us. And what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Sayyar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, he said: Shurayh has left in the breasts of the pious a doubt from it. Abu Jafar said: Then we sought the aspect in what they differed about from the Book of Allah, and we found that Allah said in His Book to His Prophet Lut: We shall save you and your family except your wife; she was of those who remained behind. So He, mighty and majestic, began with mentioning His promise to him of what He promised him, then excepted from it who is outside that. And like that from the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among what was narrated from him about the cause of the ladud administered to those in his presence when he fell unconscious in his illness in which he then was, when they gave him ladud, from his statement: No one remains in the house who witnessed my ladud except that he is given ladud, except that my oath did not cover my uncle al-Abbas."
"2221","1932 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar narrated to us, from Arqam, Abu Jafar said: and he is Ibn Shurahbil, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Abbas, he said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while some women were with him; they veiled themselves from me except Maymunah. They took Indian incense, crushed it, then gave him ladud with it. He said: No one remains in the house who witnessed my ladud except that he is given ladud, except that my oath did not cover my uncle al-Abbas. So some of them began giving ladud to others."
"2222","1933 - And as Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya, meaning al-Qattan, narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: We gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ladud in his illness, and he kept gesturing to us not to give me ladud. We said: The dislike of the sick for ladud. When he recovered he said: Did I not forbid you to give me ladud? We said: The dislike of the sick for ladud. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one of you remains except that he is given ladud while I look on, except al-Abbas, for he did not witness you."
"2223","1934 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said to him: O son of my sister, I have seen of the honoring of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his uncle al-Abbas a wondrous matter. Pleurisy used to seize him and press him severely; we used to say the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seized by such-and-such vein. Then one day pleurisy seized the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pressed him hard until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell unconscious; we feared for him, the people panicked and thought he had pleuritis. So we gave him ladud, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recovered and regained consciousness; he knew we had given him ladud and felt the ladud. He said: Did you think that Allah, mighty and majestic, inflicted it upon me? Allah, mighty and majestic, would never inflict it upon me. No one remains except that he is given ladud except my uncle. So I saw them giving them ladud man by man. She said: And whoever was in the house that day, mentioning their merit, they were all given ladud. Then the ladud reached the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we were given ladud, by Allah, woman by woman, until the ladud reached a woman of us, and she said: By Allah, I am fasting. They said: Evil is what you thought, that we would leave you while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has sworn. So they gave her ladud, by Allah, O son of my sister, though she was fasting."
"2224","1935 - And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us. And as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, then they agreed, each of them saying: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrated to me, from Asma the daughter of Umays, she said: The first time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained was in the house of Maymunah; his illness intensified until he fell unconscious. She said: His wives consulted about giving him ladud, so they gave him ladud. When he recovered he said: What is this, the act of women coming from there? And he gestured toward the land of Abyssinia, and Asma was among them. They said: We suspected you had pleuritis, O Messenger of Allah. He said: That is an illness Allah would never torment me with. No one remains in the house except that he is given ladud, except the uncle of the Messenger of Allah, meaning al-Abbas. She said: Maymunah was given ladud that day though she was fasting, due to the oath of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in these reports is the oath of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to give ladud to those in the house at first, then he excluded from them some of those in the house, namely al-Abbas, who had not attended their giving ladud to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when they gave it to him, or out of honoring him until he excluded him from that, due to his place with him; yet the oath had covered him while he was in the house, and he was excluded from it by the delayed exception. And in what we mentioned is proof of the corruption of what Shurayh said among what we mentioned from him. And from Allah we ask success."
"2225","Chapter 313: Explanation of the difficulty of ladud: what is it, and is it permissible for people to be treated with it for some ailment? 1936 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Umm Qays the daughter of Mihsan, sister of Ukashah, she said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of mine for whom I had treated his throat ailment; he said: Why do you choke your children with this pressing? Upon you is this Indian wood, for in it are seven cures, among them pleuritis: it is snuffed for throat ailment and given as ladud for pleuritis. So we sought to know what ladud is. We found Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us from Abu Ubayd, he said: al-Asmai said: Ladud is what a man is given to drink from one side of the mouth; and from it is the hadith that he, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was given ladud in his illness while unconscious. al-Asmai said: Ladud is taken from the two ladid of a valley, which are its two sides; and from it is said of a man: He turns this way and that when he looks to his two sides right and left. So we thereby knew what ladud is, and the permissibility of treatment with it for the ailment it treats, and that the forbidding of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that in what we narrated from him in the chapter before this chapter was because he was given ladud though it was not its treatment, and because they thought he had a specific ailment which in reality he did not have. If a questioner says: Was what he ordered to be done requital against those he ordered it done to for what they did to him? It is said to him: It may have been from him as punishment and discipline so they would not return to its like. And among what proves that is not requital is that he did not order that they be given ladud in the measure of medicine they gave him; for had it been requital, he would have ordered they be given ladud in the measure they gave him, not more than it. And from Allah we ask success."
"2226","Chapter 314: Explanation of the difficulty in what the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed about regarding his age at which he died, in what was reported from him that he said in his lifetime. 1937 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Nafi ibn Yazid, he said: Ibn Ghaziyyah, meaning Umarah, narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, that his mother Fatimah the daughter of al-Husayn narrated to him that Aishah used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah his daughter in his illness in which he died, among what he confided to her and she informed Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, of after his death. Aishah said: She informed me that he told her that there was no prophet after whom was a prophet except that he lived half the age of the one before him; and he told me that Isa (peace be upon him) lived a hundred and twenty years, and I see myself departing at sixty."
"2227","1938 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Kamil Abu al-Ala al-Tamimi narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Yahya ibn Jadah, from Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, did not send a prophet except that he lived half of what the one before him lived. So in these two hadiths is proof of the correctness of the statement of whoever among his companions said he died at sixty years. And we shall mention in this book what reached us of what was narrated from those of his companions from whom a statement among the statements was narrated about that, if Allah wills."
"2228","1939 - Among them is Abd Allah ibn Abbas; differing was narrated from him about that. Abu Jamrah Nasr ibn Imran al-Dubai narrated from him about it. What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah."
"2229","1940 - And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, then they agreed and said: From Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah thirteen years receiving revelation, and in Madinah ten, and he died at sixty-three years. And Ikrimah his freed slave narrated from him about that."
"2230","1941 - What Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hisham, meaning Ibn Hassan, narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent at forty years; he stayed in Makkah thirteen years receiving revelation, then he was commanded to emigrate, so he emigrated ten years, and he died at sixty-three. And Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated from him about that what indicates otherwise."
"2231","1942 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Aishah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah ten years receiving revelation, and in Madinah ten years."
"2232","1943 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Shayban al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He said: So in this is proof that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah after revelation came to him ten years, and in Madinah ten years. So this brought near to hearts that his death was at sixty years. And Ammar, freed slave of Banu Hashim, narrated from him about that."
"2233","1944 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Khadir ibn Muhammad ibn Shuja narrated to us, he said: Miskin ibn Bukayr al-Hadhdha narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ammar, freed slave of Banu Hashim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-five years. And Said ibn Jubayr narrated from him about that."
"2234","1945 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, he said: Said ibn Jubayr narrated to me, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: Allah revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ten in Madinah and ten in Makkah. He said: From whom did you hear this? He said: It reached me, or I heard people saying it. So Ibn Abbas said: Indeed Allah revealed to him in Makkah ten years and five years and more. And Amr ibn Dinar narrated from him other than that, which he may have taken from him by hearing or by report."
"2235","1946 - What Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah thirteen, and he died at sixty-three. And among them is Aishah; there was narrated from her about that."
"2236","1947 - What Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd both narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-three years."
"2237","1948 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-three years."
"2238","1949 - And what Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Musa al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and added, he said: And al-Zuhri informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Aishah, the like of it. And among them is Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan; there was narrated from him about that."
"2239","1950 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir ibn Sad, a man of Bajilah, from Jarir, that he heard Muawiyah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-three, and Abu Bakr died at sixty-three, and Umar at sixty-three, and I am today sixty-three."
"2240","1951 - And what Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Suri narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Shabi, from Jarir ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, the like of it, except that he did not mention in it: And I am today sixty-three. And Abu al-Ahwas narrated this hadith from Abu Ishaq, mentioning that the speech in it about the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is from the speech of Jarir, not from the speech of Muawiyah."
"2241","1952 - As al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I was sitting with Abd Allah ibn Utbah, and they mentioned the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Abd Allah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken at sixty-three years, and Abu Bakr died at sixty-three years, and Umar was killed at sixty-three years. A man of the people called Amir ibn Sad said to him: We were with Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, and they mentioned the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Jarir said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken at sixty-three years, and Umar was killed at sixty-three years. So in this also is the entry of Abd Allah ibn Utbah among those informing of the age of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his companions, because he saw him, so he entered thereby among his companions. And among them is Anas ibn Malik; there was narrated from him about that."
"2242","1953 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed us, from Rabiah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-three years, and there were not on his head and beard twenty white hairs."
"2243","1954 - And what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Anas, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2244","1955 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Rabiah, from Anas, the like of it. And among them is Daghfal ibn Hanzalah, about whom there is disagreement as to the clan he is from: some say it is Shayban, some say it is Dhuhl, and some say it is Sadus. And this Daghfal, we do not know that he accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), yet the people have entered his hadith in this chapter."
"2245","1956 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Daghfal ibn Hanzalah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died at sixty-five. When they differed in that this differing, what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that decides for whoever among them agreed with it against whoever among them opposed it in it. And in that it has been established that his age (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at which he died was sixty years. And with Allah is success."
"2246","Chapter 315: Explanation of the corruption of whoever held that a youth is one whose age is forty years down to below it after his puberty, by what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repelling what he said in that. 1957 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik."
"2247","1958 - And Ali ibn Mabad and Bakkar ibn Qutaybah both narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas."
"2248","1959 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I entered Paradise and there was a palace of gold. I said: Whose is this palace? They said: For a youth of Quraysh. So I thought it was I, and I said: Who is he? They said: Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"2249","1960 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2250","1961 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I entered Paradise and there I was before a palace of gold. I said: Whose is this palace? They said: For a young man of Quraysh. So I thought it was for me, and I said: Who is he? They said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. So O Abu Hafs, were it not for what I know of your jealousy, I would have entered it. So Umar said: From whom would I be jealous, O Messenger of Allah, for I was never jealous of you."
"2251","1962 - And al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur narrated to me, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I entered Paradise and saw a white palace with a slave girl in its courtyard. I said: Whose is this palace? It was said: For a youth of Quraysh. So I thought I was he, and I said: Who is he? They said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. So I wanted to enter it to look at it, but I remembered your jealousy, O Abu Hafs. He said: With my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, would I be jealous of you? So in what we narrated is proof of the corruption of the statement of whoever held what we mentioned in the title of this chapter. Then we looked after that at the reality of what is below youth, and youth, and what is above them both. We found Allah, mighty and majestic, said in His Book: He it is who created you from dust, then from a sperm drop, then from a clot, then He brings you forth as a child. So He informed us He brings them forth as a child. Then we found Him define the end of childhood in another verse, His statement: And when the children among you reach puberty, let them ask permission as those before them asked permission. So we understood thereby that what is before reaching puberty is childhood, and what is after puberty is its opposite. And we know of nothing as a third to childhood except youth. So we understood thereby that whoever has a wet dream is a youth, and remains so until Allah wills him to be. And we sought the period in which he is so, then exits it to its opposite. We found Allah said in the verse we began reciting in this chapter: Then that you reach your maturity, but He did not clarify to us therein what reaching maturity is. Then we found Him clarify that in another verse by His statement: Until when he reaches his maturity. And we needed to know whether by that he exits youth to other than it or not. So we found Him clarify that in another verse by His statement: Until when he reaches his maturity and reaches forty years. So we understood thereby that whoever reaches forty years has reached his maturity. And we needed to know whether by that he exits youth to other than it or not. So we found Allah, mighty and majestic, said in the one we began reciting right after His statement therein: Then that you reach your maturity, then that you become old men. So it was possible that what is after forty is exit from youth and entry into old age. We found Allah, mighty and majestic, said in it: He it is who created you from dust, then from a sperm drop. Between creation from dust and creation from sperm is a separator, for the one created from dust is Adam (peace be upon him), and those created from sperm are his sons, and between the two creations is of time what Allah wills it to be. Like that is His statement, mighty and majestic: Then that you reach your maturity, then that you become old men. It is possible that between their reaching maturity and their becoming old men is a period, Allah knows its measure, and it is a period of youth. So the age in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on the day he saw that vision was above forty and below the state in which they are old men. And Allah knows best the truth of the matter in that. And from Allah, exalted, we ask success."
"2252","Chapter 316: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating who the middle-aged are. 1963 - Ali ibn Zayd al-Faraidi and al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr and Umar: These two are the masters of the middle-aged of the people of Paradise among the first and the last, except the prophets and messengers."
"2253","1964 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Aban narrated to us, from Abu Janab, from al-Shabi, from Zayd ibn Yuthay, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came forward. He said: O Ali, these two are the masters of the middle-aged of the people of Paradise among the first and the last, except the prophets and messengers. Do not inform them, O Ali. So I did not narrate it until they died."
"2254","1965 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: My grandfather narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, from al-Harith, from Ali, and he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention his statement: So I did not narrate it until they died."
"2255","1966 - al-Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abis narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman Abu Muhammad al-Arzami, and Abu al-Jahhaf and Kathir the date-seller, all of them heard Atiyyah al-Awfi mentioning from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: These two are the masters of the middle-aged of the people of Paradise among the first and the last. Do not inform them, O Ali; meaning Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Abu Jafar said: And the ages of the middle-aged enter into the ages of youth, for it is said: A youth middle-aged, so he is made middle-aged while a youth; it is not said: An old man middle-aged. Rather he is an old man after he exits middle-agedness. And middle-agedness is the end of the period of youth. And from it they said: This crop has become middle-aged, meaning when it reaches the state in which its like is harvested. And from Allah we ask success."
"2256","Chapter 317: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement: al-Hasan and al-Husayn are the masters of the youth of the people of Paradise. 1967 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nuaym al-Bajali narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: al-Hasan and al-Husayn are the masters of the youth of the people of Paradise, except the two sons of the maternal aunt: Isa son of Maryam and Yahya son of Zakariyya. Abu Jafar said: Then a questioner said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you know that this statement was from him when al-Hasan and al-Husayn that day were two children, not two youths? Rather this statement informs that they are the masters of the youth of the people of Paradise though they were then not of the youth? So likewise our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah and His help, is: They were at the time in which this statement was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about them not two youths, as you mentioned; but in the meaning that they will be two youths, the masters of the youth of the people of Paradise. And it was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a sign among the signs of his prophethood, for he informed that they will be two youths in the future, and that is only from him by Allah, mighty and majestic, informing him that it will be. And they will be thereby as he said; and were it not for that statement, it would have been possible with him that they die before being two youths, or one of them die before that. When it was for him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to say that statement about them, therein was the reality of their reaching to be as he said. We understood that was only permitted for him because Allah, mighty and majestic, informed him that it will be in them. As for his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Except the two sons of the maternal aunt, Isa son of Maryam and Yahya, then because he excepted them that day from the youth of the people of Paradise by affirming youth for them, since they left the world while so. And from Allah we ask success."
"2257","Chapter 318: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement: Three are given their reward twice: a man who believed in his prophet, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached him and he believed in him; and a slave who fulfills the right of Allah and the right of his master; and a man who disciplined his slave girl well, then freed her and married her. 1968 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari and Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Salih al-Hamdani informed us, he said: I was with al-Shabi when a man of Khurasan came to him and said: O Abu Amr, those of Khurasan before us say when a man frees his slave girl then marries her, he is like one riding his sacrificial camel? al-Shabi said: Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa informed me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are given their reward twice: a man of the People of the Book who believed in his prophet, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached him and he believed in him and followed him, so for him are two rewards; and an owned slave who fulfills the right of Allah, exalted, and the right of his master upon him, so for him are two rewards; and a man who has a slave girl and nourished her well, then disciplined her and disciplined her well, then freed her and married her, so for him are two rewards. Then al-Shabi said to the Khurasani: Take this hadith without anything, for a man used to travel to Madinah for what is less than it."
"2258","1969 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah Musa ibn Masud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Said al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salih, from al-Shabi, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Any man who has a slave girl and disciplines her well and teaches her well, then frees her and marries her, for him are two rewards; and any owned slave who fulfills the right of Allah upon him and the right of his masters, for him are two rewards; and any man of the People of the Book who believed in his prophet then submitted and believed in Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for him are two rewards."
"2259","1970 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih al-Hamdani, he said: A man of Khurasan came to Amir, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Salih, and his hadith which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, from Said ibn Mansur, from Hushaym, except that he said in it: And any man of the People of the Book who believed in his prophet then believed in me, for him are two rewards."
"2260","1971 - Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim, meaning al-Dawraqi, narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to me, from Salih ibn Salih, from Amir, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from Abu Musa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: And the believer of the People of the Book, and he did not mention the speech of al-Shabi at its end."
"2261","1972 - al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih al-Hamdani Abu Hasan ibn Hayy, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yusuf, from Hajjaj, from Abu Awanah, exactly."
"2262","1973 - Ali ibn Said ibn Bashir al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Mamar ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three are given their reward twice: a man who believed in the first Book and the last Book; and a man who has a slave girl and disciplined her well, then freed her and married her; and an owned slave who excelled in worship of his Lord and was sincere to his master, or as he said."
"2263","1974 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih, from al-Shabi, from Abu Burdah, from his father, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Yusuf ibn Yazid, from Hajjaj, from Abu Awanah, from Salih."
"2264","1975 - And Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Khulayd al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And this for which we brought these reports is the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the three who are given their reward twice: And a man who believed in his prophet then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached him and he believed in him. For we understood thereby that what is intended of whoever entered the religion of the Prophet before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among those who believed in him is in the religion of the Prophet. And we understood thereby that the Prophet whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directly followed among the prophets of Allah, mighty and majestic, blessings of Allah upon them, is Isa (peace be upon him). So whoever was so deserves his reward twice, and whoever was not so deserves by his entry into the religion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only one reward, the reward of his entry into his religion. As for what he was upon before that of the religion of Musa (peace be upon him), he deserves nothing like that for it, because the religion of Isa (peace be upon him) had come upon the religion of Musa (peace be upon him), and he did not follow it, so he exited the religion of Musa (peace be upon him). Then he followed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while before following him he was upon other than what Allah, mighty and majestic, had bound him to be upon of the religion of Isa (peace be upon him). And we understood by what we mentioned that the one given his reward twice by his believing in his prophet then by his believing in the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the one who reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while upon what he was bound to of the religion of the Prophet before him, namely Isa (peace be upon him), until he entered from it into the religion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among what confirms what we mentioned is what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement in the hadith of Iyad ibn Himar."
"2265","1976 - What Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Ziyad, and Yazid the brother of Mutarrif, and two other men whose names Hammam forgot, narrated to me that Mutarrif narrated to them that Iyad ibn Himar narrated to him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say in his sermon: Allah, blessed and exalted, looked upon His servants and abhorred them, their non-Arabs and Arabs, except remnants of the People of the Book. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that into the abhorrence of Allah, mighty and majestic, those remnants of the People of the Book did not enter. And they in our view, and Allah knows best, are those who remained upon what Isa (peace be upon him) was sent with among those who did not alter it and did not introduce into it what is not from it, and remained upon what Allah bound him to until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that day this statement. And from Allah, exalted, we ask success."
"2266","Chapter 319: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among what he addressed to Caesar in his letter to him from his statement: Submit, Allah will give you your reward twice; and if you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin. 1977 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurrah ibn Abi Khalifah al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah informed me that Abd Allah ibn Abbas informed him, he said: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb ibn Umayyah informed me from his mouth to my mouth: That Heraclius called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and read it; in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Merciful, from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to Heraclius, chief of Rome: Peace upon whoever follows guidance. As for after, I call you with the call of Islam: Submit, you will be safe; and submit, Allah will give you your reward twice. If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin. And: O People of the Book, come to a word equal between us and you, to His statement: That we are Muslims. When he finished reading the letter, voices rose with him and much clamour; so he ordered us and we were expelled. I said to my companions: The matter of Ibn Abi Kabshah has become great; the king of Banu al-Asfar fears him. I remained certain of the matter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it will prevail until Allah, mighty and majestic, brought Islam upon me."
"2267","1978 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Harun al-Bardi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Abrash narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2268","1979 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and al-Layth ibn Abdah narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2269","1980 - Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So we needed to know who the Arisiyyun mentioned in these reports are. We found Abu Ubayd said in his book which he named the Book of Wealth, among what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to me narrating it to me from him, he said: They are the servants and dependents. Abu Jafar said: As if he means that upon him will be their sin for barring them from Islam by his ownership over them and his leadership over them, like what Allah, mighty and majestic, related from those who will say on the Day of Resurrection: Our Lord, we obeyed our chiefs and our great ones, so they led us astray from the path. And like the statement of the magicians of Pharaoh to Pharaoh when the proof stood against them for Musa (peace be upon him) from the miraculous sign he brought them from Allah which no sorcery like it comes: And what you compelled us to of sorcery, meaning: you employed us in it and made us proceed upon it. Abu Ubayd said in this narration: And thus say the companions of hadith, meaning what they say of al-Arisiyyin, while the correct is al-Arisin. Abu Jafar said: And this in our view is contrary to what Abu Ubayd said; for what the companions of hadith said among what he related from them is upon relating them to a chief of theirs called Aris. So it is said in its genitive and accusative: al-Arisiyyin, and in its nominative: al-Arisiyyun, as is said of a people attributed to a man called Yaqub: al-Yaqubiyyin in its accusative and genitive, and you say in its nominative: These are al-Yaqubiyyun. Like that in what we mentioned is al-Arisiyyin and al-Arisiyyun. And when you intend thereby the plural of numbers, not attribution to a man called Yaqub, you say in genitive and accusative: al-Yaqubin, and in nominative: al-Yaqubun. So it became clear, by the praise of Allah and His favour, that the companions of hadith did not err in what Abu Ubayd claimed error against them in, and that they said what bears what they said. And Allah, mighty and majestic, knows best the truth of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in that. And some of the people of knowledge of these meanings mentioned that among the folk of Heraclius is a sect known as al-Arusiyyah, affirming the oneness of Allah, mighty and majestic, confessing the servitude of the Messiah (peace be upon him) to Him, mighty and majestic, saying nothing about him of what the Christians say of his lordship and sonship, holding to the religion of the Messiah (peace be upon him), believing in what is in his Gospel, denying what the Christians say besides that. If that is so, it is permitted to call this sect al-Arisiyyun in nominative and al-Arisiyyin in accusative and genitive, as the companions of hadith held. And thereby it is permitted that this sect be the one mentioned by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Iyad ibn Himar which we narrated in the chapter before this chapter of this book of ours. And it is permitted that Caesar, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to him what he wrote to him, was upon the like of what it is upon. So thereby it is permitted that if he follows the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and enters his religion, Allah gives him his reward twice. And it is permitted that this sect knew of the place of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his religion before Caesar knew it, but they did not follow him nor enter into it, nor affirm his prophethood, though in the Book of Isa (peace be upon him) is his glad tidings of him, as Allah, mighty and majestic, related in His Book, His statement, mighty and majestic: And when Isa son of Maryam said: O Children of Israel, I am the Messenger of Allah to you, confirming what is before me of the Torah and bringing glad tidings of a messenger coming after me whose name is Ahmad. So they exited the religion of Isa (peace be upon him) thereby, for the Isa who is believed in is the Isa who gave glad tidings of Ahmad, not another Isa. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Caesar: If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin who exited the creed of Isa (peace be upon him). Then this questioner said: And how can upon him be the sin of another? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: The sin upon him if he turns away is only the like of the sin of the Arisiyyin, not the very sin of the Arisiyyin. This is like His statement, mighty and majestic: When they are fortified, if they commit indecency, upon them is half of what is upon the fortified women of punishment. It is not that upon them is any of the punishment upon the fortified women, but the like of half the punishment upon the fortified women. Like that is his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If you turn away, upon you is the sin of the Arisiyyin; it is only in the meaning of his statement: Upon you is the like of the sin of the Arisiyyin. Then this questioner said: You narrated to us in what preceded of this book of yours that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, and his statement with that: I fear the enemy will get hold of it; yet in what you narrated in this hadith is his writing to Caesar with something of the Quran which will fall into his hand after his letter reaches him. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: This is not contrary to his forbidding travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy fearing the enemy will get hold of it. Rather this is upon travelling with part of it to the enemy, and what is before it is upon travelling with all of it to the enemy. So their correction is the permissibility of travelling with amulets in which is of the Quran what is in their likes, and the dislike of travelling with its entirety to them, from them, when fearing for it. And from Allah we ask success."
"2270","Chapter 320: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement: When supper is present and prayer is established, begin with supper. 1981 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When supper is placed then prayer is established, begin with supper."
"2271","1982 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: And prayer is established."
"2272","1983 - And al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Hisham, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2273","1984 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman and Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hisham, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2274","1985 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Rafi, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2275","1986 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is at food, let him not hasten from it until he fulfills his need, even if prayer is established."
"2276","1987 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When supper is placed and prayer comes, begin with supper."
"2277","1988 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Simak, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2278","1989 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: And this Simak is Simak ibn Atiyyah."
"2279","1990 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2280","1991 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Yunus ibn Yazid informed me. And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When prayer is established and supper is present, begin with supper before prayer. Abu Jafar said: I heard al-Muzani say, al-Shafii said: He commanded, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), attending prayer in congregation, meaning in other than what we narrated, due to the merit of congregation over praying alone; and he permitted staying back from congregation for a reason, namely that the supper of one of them comes while prayer is established, or prayer is established while he needs ablution with a present need. And he was forbidden to pray while repelling the two filths: stool and urine. Had he prayed, his prayer would suffice him, but he is permitted due to excuse to leave congregation, and it is beloved for him to enter prayer with nothing occupying his heart from it nor hastening him from completing it. And most of what people know is that when he enters it while needing to hasten to relieve the need, he almost combines two matters: hastening from completion and occupation from devotion. And this is feared for whoever his supper comes to, due to people's need for food and their souls' craving for it, especially the people of fasting and need for food. Abu Jafar said: And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he intended by his statement: When supper comes and prayer is established, begin with supper, only the people of fasting, not others."
"2281","1992 - As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Waqid al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab that he heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When prayer is established, and one of you is fasting, let him begin with supper before the Maghrib prayer, and do not hasten from your suppers. So that proved that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by this statement only the fasting, not others. And from Allah we ask success. And what we related in this chapter from al-Shafii suffices us from speaking about it further. And in it, about advancing relieving oneself before prayer, is what suffices us from speaking about it in a chapter we shall bring right after this chapter in this book of ours, if Allah, exalted, wills."
"2282","Chapter 321: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding prayer while repelling stool and urine. 1993 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you intends relieving himself and prayer is established, let him begin with it. Abu Jafar said: Thus Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated this hadith from Hisham, mentioning it from him from his father from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. And more than one of those who narrated it from Hisham opposed him in that, mentioning it from him, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam. Among them is Malik ibn Anas."
"2283","1994 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam, and he was their imam, he said: He established the prayer and said: Put forward a man of you; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When prayer is established and one of you has need of relieving himself, let him begin with it. And among them is Isa ibn Yunus."
"2284","1995 - As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam, then he mentioned the like of it. And among them are Abd Allah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani and Abu Muawiyah al-Darir."
"2285","1996 - As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr and Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And among them is Wuhayb ibn Khalid."
"2286","1997 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from a man, from Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam, then he mentioned the like of it. So those who narrated it as we mentioned, namely Malik and Isa ibn Yunus and Abd Allah ibn Numayr and Abu Muawiyah and Wuhayb ibn Khalid, from Hisham, are more deserving of correctness than what Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated against him. And each one of those who narrated it thus is a proof against Ibn Abi al-Zinad, while Ibn Abi al-Zinad is not a proof against him, so how about them all together? And in the hadith of Wuhayb from Hisham is proof of the corruption of the chain of this hadith at its root, for he inserted in it between Urwah and Abd Allah ibn al-Arqam an unknown man who is not known. When this hadith was corrupted by what we mentioned, we sought it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): do we find it with him from another aspect which the people of knowledge of isnad accept and use as proof in its like?"
"2287","1998 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Yaqub ibn Mujahid, that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to him that Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to them, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: None of you stands for prayer in the presence of food, nor while the two filths, stool and urine, press him."
"2288","1999 - And we found Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, from Abu Hazrah, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, then he mentioned the like of it. And this Abu Hazrah is Yaqub ibn Mujahid mentioned in the hadith of Yunus which we narrated before this hadith. He is praiseworthy in narration, his narration accepted, a proof therein. More than one of the imams narrated from him, among them Yahya al-Qattan, and among them Husayn al-Jufi, and among them Hatim ibn Ismail. And Abd Allah ibn Muhammad mentioned in the hadith of Yunus, from Ibn Wahb, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, is Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, brother of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad."
"2289","2000 - And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating from my grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not repel the two filths, stool and urine, in prayer. So this Sunnah became established with us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and from Abu Hurayrah together. And in the hadith of Yunus from Ibn Wahb, from Yahya ibn Ayyub: None of you stands for prayer in the presence of food. So this was of the kind we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter of this book of ours. And that in our view, and Allah knows best, is upon food which his soul craves, among what if he enters prayer while upon that, his heart is occupied from it until that prevents him from devotion to it and from completing it. So it was more deserving for him to cut that from himself before entering it. And he did not intend thereby in our view, and Allah knows best, consuming all that food; but the departure of his soul's craving for it and his heart's occupation with it from his prayer which he intends to enter, for it is intelligible that something made for a reason is lifted by the removal of that reason. Like that is what is in this hadith, and what is in the chapter we mentioned before it: When supper comes and prayer comes, begin with supper; they in our view are upon this meaning. And into them does not enter being occupied with food whose leaving does not cut off completing prayer nor devotion to it. And the food of the people then, which was for them lunch and supper, its measure is not hidden from people who do its like, of its measure in scantiness, and that it is not like the food of those after them in abundance. And from Allah we ask success."
"2290","Chapter 322: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his statement: The believer eats in one intestine, and the disbeliever eats in seven intestines. 2001 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Waqid, he said: I heard Nafi say: A man came to Ibn Umar and kept offering him food, and he ate much food. He said: O Nafi, do not admit this one to me, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The disbeliever eats in seven intestines."
"2291","2002 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Waqid ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2292","2003 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The disbeliever eats in seven intestines, and the Muslim eats in one intestine."
"2293","2004 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah and Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2294","2005 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2295","2006 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr that he asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The disbeliever eats in seven intestines, and the believer eats in one intestine? He said: Yes."
"2296","2007 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Sulami al-Himsi Abu al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: My father Muhammad ibn Sulayman Abu Damrah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Qays narrated to us, he said: I saw Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr while on his pulpit in Makkah saying: The disbeliever eats in seven intestines, and the believer eats in one intestine; thus I was informed that Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it."
"2297","2008 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, that Abu Khalid al-Walibi mentioned it, from Maymunah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2298","2009 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2299","2010 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and al-Darawardi informed us, they said: al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2300","2011 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, he said: I entered upon Abu Said while he was eating scant food. I said to him: I see you eating scant food. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The believer eats in one intestine, and the disbeliever eats in seven intestines."
"2301","2012 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah and Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2302","2013 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2303","2014 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Salamah al-Khuzai narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah, from Said ibn Yasar, from a man of Juhaynah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2304","2015 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2305","2016 - al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2306","2017 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2307","2018 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: These reports were narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concordant, not discordant. We considered them and found the believer names Allah over his food, so there is blessing in it. And we found the disbeliever does not name Allah over his food, so there is no blessing in it. Yet we found some believers eating much food and some disbelievers eating little food. So we understood he did not intend by what is in these reports all believers nor all disbelievers; rather a specific group of them was intended."
"2308","2019 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was hosted by a disbelieving guest. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a sheep, it was milked, and he drank its milk. Then he ordered another, and he drank it. Then he ordered another, and he drank it. Then he ordered another, and he drank it, until he drank the milk of seven sheep. Then in the morning he submitted and became Muslim. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered for him a sheep, it was milked, and he drank its milk. Then he ordered another for him, but he could not finish it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer drinks in one intestine, and the disbeliever in seven intestines."
"2309","2020 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, he said: I heard Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A man used to come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as a disbeliever, and he ate much food. Then he submitted and ate little food. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned it."
"2310","2021 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Ubayd ibn Salman al-Qurashi, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Jahjah al-Ghifari, he said: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when we finished the prayer, he said: Let each man of you take the hand of his companion. So the people took hold, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I remained. I was a huge tall man whom no one could match. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and went with me to his house. Then he mentioned in its rest the like of what is in the hadith of Yunus which we mentioned before this hadith."
"2311","2022 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: A man narrated to me, he said: We used to host Bedouins, so we set out to Madinah seeking food. We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the pulpit, and he mentioned of this the like of the hadith of Jahjah exactly."
"2312","2023 - And as Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, and Said ibn Ufayr and Hassan ibn Ghalib, some adding over others in the wording of the hadith, they said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Wardan narrated to us, from Abu al-Haytham, namely Sulayman ibn Amr al-Utari, that he asked Abu Basrah about the Islam of Ghifar. He said: Yes, a hardship and lack of rain befell us, so we talked of going to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to obtain food with him, then return to our families. So we set out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while not intending Islam. He said: Who are the people? We said: A group of Banu Ghifar. He said: Muslims or onlookers? We said: Rather onlookers. So we stayed that day; when it was night, then he mentioned the like of the hadith before this hadith in itself."
"2313","2024 - And as Yahya narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hubayrah narrated to me that Abu Tamim al-Jayshani informed him that he heard Abu Basrah informing that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pledge to him upon Islam, but he stayed a night without submitting. Then he mentioned this story in itself as in the hadith we mentioned before this hadith. So we thereby knew that the cause in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the statement we mentioned in the reports we narrated at the beginning of this chapter, and that from him was only about a specific man in his state of disbelief and in his state of Islam. So the hadith in our view had no aspect other than this aspect. And the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The believer eats in one intestine, and the disbeliever eats in seven intestines, came in the form of definiteness. And what comes in the form of definiteness does not extend beyond the one intended by it to others. And of that is the statement of Allah, mighty and majestic: So indeed with hardship is ease, indeed with hardship is ease. The people of knowledge said in that: One hardship does not overcome two eases, deriving that meaning in this verse, because hardship came definite, so it was upon one, while ease came indefinite, so in each one of His statement: Indeed with hardship is ease, is other than in the other of them. And likewise everything coming as definite is upon what we mentioned, unless in it is a proof indicating the intent of more than the one, so it turns to that and its ruling returns to the ruling of the indefinite, like His statement, mighty and majestic: By time, indeed man is in loss, except those who believed and did righteous deeds and advised each other to truth and advised each other to patience. So it was known thereby that the genus was intended, not the single man. And from Allah, mighty and majestic, we ask success. And I heard Ibn Abi Imran say: Some people carried this hadith upon desire for the world, as you say: So-and-so devours the world devouring, meaning: he desires it and is greedy for it. So they made the meaning of his statement (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The believer eats in one intestine, meaning due to his asceticism in the world, and the disbeliever in seven intestines, meaning due to his desire for it. And they did not place that upon food. They said: We have seen a believer with more food than a disbeliever; had that been upon food, the meaning of the hadith would be impossible. And with Allah, mighty and majestic, is success."
"2314","Chapter 323: Explanation of the difficulty in what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Magians, and in what was mentioned from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that they were People of the Book. 2025 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Sad, Abu Jafar said: He is al-Baqqal Said ibn al-Marzuban, from Isa ibn Asim, from Farwah ibn Nawfal, he said: A man stood and said: Wondrous of Ali, taking jizyah from the Magians, though they were commanded, or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fighting, and that jizyah be taken only from the People of the Book. He said: al-Mustawrid al-Tamimi heard him, seized him and took him to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Out with it, and I shall inform you both that the Magians were People of the Book. Then a king of them went and fell upon his sister while intoxicated. When he sobered, his sister said to him: What have you done? You fell upon me, the people saw you, and now they will stone you. He said: Did you not restrain me? She said: And could I? You came like Satan, the people saw you, and they will stone you tomorrow unless you obey me. He said: And how shall I act? She said: Satisfy the greedy, then call the people and say to them: Allah, mighty and majestic, created Adam, and he used to marry his son to his sister, or she said: his son to his daughter. He said: And the reciters came to him. They said: Rise, O enemy of Allah. He said: This is it, and they have come. So those stood to them and trampled them until they died. So from that day was Magianism. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took jizyah from the Magians of Hajar. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith from the statement of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, is: The Magians were People of the Book. And this in our view, and Allah knows best, is among what may mean they were People of a Book which, had it remained for them, their slaughtered animals would be eaten and their women lawful, and they would be in that like the Jews and Christians whose Books we believe in, namely the Torah and the Gospel. But Allah, mighty and majestic, abrogated it and removed it from His Books, lifting its ruling from the people of faith in it, as He abrogated other things of what He had revealed to our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Quran, returning it to non-Quran. Of that is what used to be recited: The old man and old woman, when they commit zina, stone them outright, for what they spent of pleasure. And of that: If the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth, he would seek a third to them, among many things Allah, mighty and majestic, abrogated, removing them from being Quran. And we shall mention what was narrated in that later in this book of ours, if Allah wills. When that was so, it was possible that what was narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, about the Magians, that they had a Book, is as narrated from him, but it was abrogated and exited the Books of Allah, mighty and majestic, so it was not of them. Then a questioner said: How was jizyah taken from them, when Allah, mighty and majestic, said: Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor in the Last Day, nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger forbade, nor practice the religion of truth, of those given the Book, until they give jizyah from hand while subdued? If you say: Due to the taking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of it from them in this hadith of Ali, and in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2315","2026 - As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyaynah say: Amr heard Bajalah saying it: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, had not taken jizyah from the Magians until Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf testified that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from the people of Hajar. And in the hadith of Amr ibn Awf."
"2316","2027 - So he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him that Amr, namely Amr ibn Awf, an ally of Banu Amir ibn Luay who had witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Bahrain to bring its jizyah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had made peace with the people of Bahrain and appointed over them al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami. So Abu Ubaydah came with the wealth of Bahrain. The Ansar heard of the arrival of Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, and attended the Fajr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, he turned, and they presented themselves to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled when he saw them, then said: I think you heard that Abu Ubaydah came with something from Bahrain? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: So rejoice and hope for what delights you. By Allah, it is not poverty I fear for you, but I fear the world will be spread over you as it was spread over those before you, so you compete in it as they competed, and it destroys you as it destroyed them."
"2317","2028 - And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he informed him that Amr ibn Awf, an ally of Banu Amir ibn Luay, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: So it distracts you as it distracted them, in place of: So it destroys you as it destroyed them. It was said to you: In the taking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of jizyah is proof that they have a Book. So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: The taking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of jizyah from them may mean not his affirming they have a Book, but another meaning: Since the people of the two Books, we believe in their Books, and jizyah was taken from them for our settling them with us in the abode of Islam secure, while they are nearer to us than the Magians who have no Book, the Magians who are so, with our settling them in our abode secure, are more deserving of jizyah being taken from them. Abu Jafar said: And there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what confirms taking jizyah from the Magians among what he addressed to his uncle Abu Talib."
"2318","2029 - What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Yahya ibn Umarah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Abu Talib fell ill; Quraysh came to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit him. At his head was the seat of a man, so Abu Jahl stood and sat in it. He said: What is with your nephew mentioning our gods? He said: What is with your people complaining of you? He said: O my uncle, I want them upon a word by which the Arabs submit to them and the non-Arabs pay them jizyah. He said: What is it? He said: There is no god but Allah. He said: Has he made the gods one god? So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Sad, by the Quran full of reminder, to His statement: Indeed this is a wondrous thing. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith is proof of the entry of the Magians among those from whom jizyah is taken, for they are of the non-Arabs. Then this questioner said: And how do you accept this hadith while in its chain is Yahya ibn Umarah, whom you do not know, nor do you know Yahya ibn Umarah among the people of knowledge except Yahya ibn Umarah al-Ansari Abu Amr ibn Yahya, and he does not narrate from Said ibn Jubayr; rather he is of the people of Madinah? So our answer to him in that, by the success of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His help, is: Yahya ibn Umarah mentioned in this hadith is as mentioned, except that we have come upon the defect in it, and it became clear to us that it is miscopied, and that what was intended is Yahya ibn Abbad Abu Hubayrah al-Ansari, a noble man among the Tabiin of Kufah, so it was miscopied and said: Yahya ibn Umarah."
"2319","2030 - As Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Madini, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us with this hadith, and he said in it: Yahya ibn Umarah. So I came to Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi and he narrated to us with it, and he said in it: From Yahya. I said to Abd al-Rahman: Who is Yahya? He said: I shall not add to you upon Yahya. So I looked in the book of al-Ashjai, and there it was from Yahya ibn Abbad Abu Hubayrah. So thereby what we mentioned became clear. And taking jizyah from the Magians was for this meaning, because they are non-Arabs, not because they are People of a Book whose women become lawful thereby and whose slaughtered animals are eaten thereby. And thereby the rightly guided caliphs, the guided, may the pleasure of Allah be upon them, acted with them; among them Umar and Ali, and among them Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them."
"2320","2031 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took jizyah from the Magians of Hajar, and Umar ibn al-Khattab took it from the Magians of al-Sawad, and Uthman took it from Barbar. Abu Jafar said: And likewise al-Hasan wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"2321","2032 - As Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi ibn Artah: As for what follows, ask al-Hasan what prevented the imams before us from coming between the Magians and the women they gather whom none but they gather? So he asked him, and he informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted the jizya from the Magians of Bahrayn and left them upon their Magianism, and the governor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over Bahrayn then was al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami, and Abu Bakr and Umar and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them) did so after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: And likewise was narrated from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali concerning them."
"2322","2033 - As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the Magians of Bahrayn calling them to Islam; whoever of them accepted Islam it was accepted from him, and whoever refused, the jizya was imposed upon him, and their slaughtered animals are not to be eaten, and their women are not to be married. Then this objector said: But it has been narrated from Hudhayfa concerning that, and he mentioned what has been narrated."
"2323","Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Abu Razin, from Abu Musa, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, he said: Were it not that I saw my companions take from the Magians, meaning the jizya, I would not have taken from them, and he recited: Fight those who believe not in Allah nor in the Last Day, the verse. He said: So here is Hudhayfa who said concerning them what is in this hadith. So our answer to him concerning that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that Hudhayfa had not come to know what the rightly-guided rightly-directed caliphs and others whom we mentioned in this chapter came to know of what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning them, so he said what he said for that reason, except that he, may Allah have mercy on him, heard them and obeyed them and knew that they did only what they had to do, may Allah be pleased with them, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2324","Chapter 324 - Explanation of the difficulty in the saying of Allah Almighty: Whatever verse We abrogate or cause to be forgotten, the verse, with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by which that is inferred. Abu Jafar said: The people of knowledge of interpretation said abrogation is of two kinds. One: abrogation of acting upon what is in the abrogated verses while the abrogated verses remain Quran as they are. The other: their removal from the Quran while memorized in hearts or removed from hearts not memorized, and these two kinds are found in the reports narrated in this chapter. As for what is abrogated of the Quran whose acting is abrogated while it remains Quran, it is like the saying of Allah Almighty in Surat al-Anfal: If there be of you twenty steadfast they will overcome two hundred, then Allah abrogated that by His saying: Now Allah has lightened for you and knew weakness is in you, so if there be of you a hundred steadfast they will overcome two hundred. And like His saying in Surat al-Muzzammil: O you enwrapped, stand the night except little, then He abrogated that by His saying: He knew there will be among you sick and others traveling seeking Allah's bounty and others fighting in Allah's way, so recite what is easy of it. This is what its acting is abrogated while remaining Quran as before. As for what is abrogated and removed from the Quran, it divides into two parts. One: it leaves the hearts of the believers so nothing of it remains therein, and of that is what 2034 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Umama ibn Sahl narrated to me, while we were in the gathering of Said ibn al-Musayyab without his denying it, that a man had a sura with him, so he stood at night and recited it but could not retain it, and another stood and recited it but could not retain it, and another stood and recited it but could not retain it, so in the morning they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and gathered with him, and one said: O Messenger of Allah, I stood last night to recite such-and-such sura but could not retain it, and another said: I came only for that, and another said: And I, O Messenger of Allah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It was abrogated last night. Thus Yunus narrated this hadith to us and did not go beyond Abu Umama with it, and the hadith scholars include this among the musnad, because Abu Umama was of those born in the Prophet's time, and his family say the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Asad after Abu Umama Asad ibn Zurara. And Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri and inserted between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Umama a group of the Ansar among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2325","2035 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman and al-Layth ibn Abda narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to me that a group of the Ansar among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that a man of them stood in the middle of the night wanting to open a sura he had memorized, but he could retain nothing of it except In the name of Allah the Most Merciful the Most Compassionate, so he came to the door of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning asking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, then another came, and another, till they gathered, and they asked one another what had brought them together, and they informed one another of the matter of that sura, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave them permission, and they informed him of their tidings and asked him about the sura, so he was silent a while returning nothing to them, then he said: It was abrogated last night, so it was removed from their breasts and from everything it had been in. And the other part is that it leaves the Quran but remains in the breasts of the believers as other than Quran."
"2326","And of that is what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: Did we not find in what Allah sent down to us: Strive as you strove the first time? He said: Yes. He said: But we do not find it. He said: It was dropped among what was dropped of the Quran. He said: Do you fear people will return unbelievers? He said: What Allah wills. He said: If people return unbelievers, their rulers will be Banu so-and-so and their ministers Banu so-and-so."
"2327","And what Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, from al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, he said: Umar said to Abd al-Rahman, then he mentioned the like except that he said: Their rulers will be Banu Umayya and their ministers Banu al-Mughira."
"2328","And of that is what Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi Hind, narrated to us, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, from his father, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, he said: A sura was sent down then lifted, and there was retained of it: If the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth he would seek a third to them, and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam but dust, and Allah turns to whoever repents."
"2329","Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Zayd informed us, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from his father, from Abu Musa, he said: There was sent down, as if meaning a sura like Baraa, then it was lifted, and there was retained of it: Allah supports this religion with peoples who have no share, and if the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth he would seek a third to them, then he mentioned the rest of the first hadith."
"2330","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2331","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama informed us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2332","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Farwa ibn Abi al-Maghra narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, from his father, he said: Abu Musa sent for the reciters of Basra, and there entered to him of them three hundred who had recited the Quran. He said: You are the best of the people of Basra and their reciters, so recite it and let not a long time pass over you lest your hearts harden as hardened the hearts of those before you, and we used to recite a sura we likened in length and severity to Baraa, then we were made to forget it except that I retained of it: If the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth he would seek a third to them, and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam but dust. And we used to recite a sura we likened to one of the Musabbihat, then we were made to forget it except that I retained of it: O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do, so it will be written as testimony upon your necks and you will be asked about it on the Day of Resurrection."
"2333","2036 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Said al-Thaqafi al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, from Abu al-Ala, from Abdullah ibn Burayda, from his father, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in prayer: If the son of Adam had a valley of gold he would wish a second valley, and if he were given a second he would wish a third valley, and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam but dust, and Allah turns to whoever repents."
"2334","And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from Ubayy ibn Kab, he said: We used to consider this wording of the Quran: If the son of Adam had two valleys of wealth he would wish a third, and nothing fills the belly of the son of Adam but dust, then Allah turns to whoever repents, then this sura was sent down: Rivalry in increase distracted you, to its end."
"2335","2037 - And what Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha, from Anas, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against those who killed the companions of Bir Mauna for thirty mornings, supplicating against Ril and Dhakwan, and Usayya disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. Anas said: Allah Almighty sent down concerning those who killed the companions of Bir Mauna a Quran that was abrogated afterwards: Convey to our people that we have met our Lord, and He is pleased with us and we are pleased with Him."
"2336","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Ishaq, meaning Ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talha, narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me that Allah sent down concerning them, meaning the people of Bir Mauna, a Quran: Convey from us to our people that we have met our Lord and He is pleased with us and we are pleased with Him, then it was abrogated and lifted after we had recited it a time, and Allah sent down: And think not those slain in Allah's way dead, rather alive with their Lord provided for. Abu Jafar said: So this is what is abrogated of Allah's Book dividing into the divisions we mentioned in this chapter, and in what we mentioned of that is what confirms what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter of the possibility in Ali's saying, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Magians were People of the Book, that that book was lifted and removed from Allah's books as were removed the verses mentioned in these reports we narrated in this chapter from the Quran, so it became as if it had never been Quran at all, and we ask Allah glory be to Him for guidance."
"2337","Chapter 325 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his ordering Zayd ibn Thabit to learn Syriac and his saying to him with that: I do not trust the Jews with my letters. 2038 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us. Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, they said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Thabit ibn Ubayd, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do you know Syriac well? Letters come to me. I said: No. He said: Then learn it. He said: So I learned it in seventeen days."
"2338","2039 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Kharija ibn Zayd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to learn for him the script of the Jews, and half a month had not passed over me till I learned it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not trust the Jews with my letter, so when I learned it for him I would write to the Jews when he wrote to them, and when they wrote to him I would read their letter to him. Abu Jafar said: So we considered this hadith and found what used to come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Jews' letters in Syriac was read to him only by the Jews who attended him, and they were not trusted against concealing part of it, nor trusted against distorting what is in it to what they want, and what went out of his letters to the Jews in answer to their letters to him was in Arabic, so the Jews to whom they came needed one who knows Arabic to read it to them since they did not know Arabic, so perhaps he would distort what is in his letters to them to what he wants, especially if he were of the idol worshippers in whose hearts toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what is not hidden, and in whose hearts toward the People of the Book is what is therein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Zayd to learn Syriac for him to read their letters when they came to him with a reading by which he is safe from concealment and distortion, and his letter when it comes to the Jews comes to them as a letter their common folk read, safe from concealment of part and distortion to other than what was written, so this is the aspect of this hadith in our view, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2339","Chapter 336 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Were it not for the emigration I would have been a man of the Ansar. 2040 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for the emigration I would have been a man of the Ansar, and if people were to take a valley or mountain pass I would take the valley of the Ansar or their pass. So we considered this hadith and found support was named its people thereby for deserving it by their supporting Allah and His Messenger and fighting for the religion till they attained what they attained, and the emigration before that its people deserved it likewise and by their emigrating their abode for Allah and His Messenger to the abode Allah chose for His Messenger and for them, and made it a home for His Messenger and dwellings for them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the worthiest of the two parties of both causes and the highest in rank therein, and with that he would give choice to whoever combined them with him between them to choose one and make him of its people and suffice with it for the other, and that would only be while the one given choice has the meaning by which he deserves to be of each of them. Of that was what he did with Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman."
"2340","2041 - As Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me choice between emigration and support, and I chose support. Abu Jafar said: And had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chosen support for himself and left emigration, all the people would have become Ansar and none of them would have remained emigrant, so he did not make himself of the Ansar for emigration and support both to remain. And like that is what was narrated from al-Nawwas ibn Siman."
"2341","2042 - As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Nawwas ibn Siman, he said: I stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madina a year, nothing preventing me from emigrating but asking questions, for when one of us emigrated he would not ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about anything. And in that is what indicates he deserved emigration. And in that is what indicates he chose support over emigration, and likewise Jubayr ibn Nufayr attributed him in this hadith."
"2342","2043 - As Nasr ibn Marzuq and Fahd both narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiya ibn Salih narrated to me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr narrated to him, from his father, from Nawwas ibn Siman al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Allah struck a parable of a straight path, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Jafar said: And al-Nawwas is but a man of Banu Kilab who entered among the Ansar through support, and likewise Hudhayfa is a man of Banu Abs who entered among the Ansar through support, so it is established by what we mentioned that among those counted of the Ansar are those who are neither of Aws nor of Khazraj, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2343","Chapter 327 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the complaint of the wife of Safwan ibn al-Muattal against Safwan to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would beat her when she prayed and make her break fast when she fasted and sleep till the sun rose. 2044 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The wife of Safwan ibn al-Muattal came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Safwan ibn al-Muattal beats me when I pray and makes me break fast when I fast and does not pray Fajr till the sun rises, and Safwan was with him. He said: So Safwan said: O Messenger of Allah, as for her saying he beats me when I pray, she stands with my sura which I recite and recites it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it a single sura it would suffice people. And as for her saying: he makes me break fast when I fast, she goes and fasts while I am a young man. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that day: No woman shall fast except with her husband's permission. And as for her saying: he does not pray till the sun rises, we are a household known for that, we do not wake till the sun rises. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you wake, pray. Abu Jafar said: So we considered what is in this hadith of the complaint of Safwan's wife that he beats her when she prays and Safwan's informing that he only does that because she recites his sura, and the Messenger's saying a single sura would suffice people, and we found it possible he thought that if she recited his sura they would obtain only one reward together, seeking that she recite other than what he recites so they obtain two rewards, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that they obtain thereby two rewards, for each one's recitation is other than the other's. And we considered her saying he prevents me from fasting and Safwan's excuse and his prohibition that a woman fast except with her husband's permission, and we understood thereby it was only for her preventing him from herself by her fast, and that indicates when he has no need of her due to his absence or what cuts him from her, there is no harm in her fasting without his permission, and we found this meaning disclosed in another hadith."
"2344","2045 - And it is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Musa ibn Abi Uthman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No woman shall fast while her husband is present except with his permission. So we considered this Musa ibn Abi Uthman who he is and who his father who narrated this hadith from him is, and we found al-Bukhari mentioned he is known as al-Tabban and that he is a client of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, so we knew thereby who he is."
"2345","2046 - And what Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Yahya and Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Musa ibn Abi Uthman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2346","2047 - And what Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali, meaning Ibn Maymun al-Raqqi, narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb, meaning Ibn Abi Hamza, narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. He said: So this hadith indicates the prohibition of fasting was only when her husband needs her for what fasting prevents, not for other than that. And we considered the Prophet's saying when you wake then pray, and we found it possible to be upon praying when waking from sleep even if the sun has not risen high, and if so this would be proof for whoever says a man may pray the obligatory prayer then. Except that we found when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept he and his companions till the sun rose, he did not pray Fajr then till he left that time to the sun's spreading and whiteness, and we shall mention that later in this book if Allah wills. And it was understood from his saying: when you wake then pray, meaning pray as you like to pray in the times prayers are prayed, not in others. Do you not see he did not permit him to pray upon waking without ablution nor uncovered, but permitted him to pray as one ought to pray of purity, covering, facing qibla, and in the times permitted not prohibited, and his address thereby was to Safwan, a man of his companions in whom is learning these things, and perhaps he had been with him on his journey in which he slept past prayer till sunrise and knew what he did then, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sufficed with that without repeating upon him, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2347","Chapter 328 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the servant asking his Lord to punish him in this world with what He would punish him in the Hereafter. 2048 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man become like a chick, so he said: Did you supplicate Allah with anything or ask Him for it? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I used to say: O Allah, whatever You would punish me with in the Hereafter, hasten it for me in this world. So he said: Glory be to Allah, you cannot bear it nor endure it, why did you not say: Our Lord, give us in this world good and in the Hereafter good and shield us from the punishment of the Fire."
"2348","2049 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma informed us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hadith. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you narrated from him?"
"2349","2050 - Then he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahia and Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sinan ibn Sad al-Kindi, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When Allah wants good for His servant He hastens the punishment for him in this world, and when Allah wants evil for His servant He withholds him for his sin till He recompenses him on the Day of Resurrection. This objector said: If the matter is as in this hadith, why was blamed one who asked his Lord to hasten punishment in this world to be safe in the Hereafter? So our answer to him therein, by Allah's guidance and help, was that what he mentioned of the second hadith is as he mentioned, and what is in the first hadith does not contradict it, except that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose for his nation out of compassion and mercy that they supplicate Allah for well-being in this world from the like of that man's state, and that He give them in the Hereafter what secures them from Hereafter punishment, and this state is the highest of all states, so it became clear, praise be to Allah, there is no contradiction in any of these reports nor disagreement, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2350","Chapter 329 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The best of people is a believer between two noble ones. 2051 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad al-Zuhri informed me, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham informed me, from his father, he said: A man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: It is near that there will prevail over this world a worthless son of worthless son of worthless, and the best of people is a believer between two noble ones."
"2351","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me that Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him that one of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like and did not raise it. So we considered this hadith and found his saying: near that a worthless son of worthless will prevail over the world, there is no disagreement in its interpretation among the Arabs that he is the slave or the mean one. And we considered his saying: and the best of people is a believer between two noble ones, and the best we have thereof is that the meaning be: a believer between two noble ones, meaning a believer between a believing father who is his origin and a believing son who is his branch, so he has of faith his place therein by his own faith, and his place therein by his son's faith below him whom Allah raises to his rank to cool his eye thereby, like what we narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, and what some raised from him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book: Allah raises the progeny of the believer to his rank though they be below him in deeds, and he recited: And those who believed and their progeny followed them in faith, We joined to them their progeny. And he has his place also by his father's faith. And of that is what we narrated earlier in this book from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When a man dies his deeds cease except from three: righteous offspring supplicating for him, or knowledge he spread, or ongoing charity. And whoever combines these three has combined what may gather for him the good of this world and the Hereafter, and we only chose in this the interpretation of nobility as piety, for Allah said in His Book: The noblest of you with Allah is the most Godfearing, and because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said:"
"2352","2052 - What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The noble son of the noble son of the noble son of the noble is Yusuf son of Yaqub son of Ishaq son of Ibrahim, Allah's blessings upon them."
"2353","2053 - And what Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar and Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The noble son of the noble son of the noble son of the noble is Yusuf son of Yaqub son of Ishaq son of Ibrahim."
"2354","2054 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Yusuf al-Tamimi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the noblest of people. He said: The most Godfearing of them. They said: O Messenger of Allah, not about this do we ask you. So he said: Yusuf son of Yaqub, a prophet son of a prophet son of a prophet son of the Friend of the Merciful. So they said: Not about this do we ask you. He said: Then about the mines of the Arabs do you ask me? They said: Yes. He said: The best of people are their best in Islam when they understand."
"2355","2055 - And what al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Ala al-Sanani narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Umar, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. And like that is what was narrated from Abdullah ibn Masud of what is known he did not say by opinion, but said it for taking it from one higher than him."
"2356","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, that Asma ibn Kharija insulted a man and said: I am the son of the noble elders. So Abdullah said: The noble elders are Yusuf son of Yaqub, Allah's chosen, son of Ishaq, Allah's sacrificed, son of Ibrahim, Allah's friend. Abu Jafar said: So Allah in His Book and His Messenger in his Sunna returned nobility to piety and to high ranks with Allah Almighty, not to other than that, so thereby the strongest in our hearts was that his saying in the hadith we narrated is upon whoever is of that rank, and Allah knows best what He and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby, and Him we ask for guidance."
"2357","Chapter 330 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one of whom it was said he prays all night then when morning comes he steals, so he said: His prayer will prevent him. 2056 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Harrani known as Suhaym narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So-and-so prays all night then when morning comes he steals. So he said: What you say will forbid him. So we considered this hadith and found Allah said in His Book: Prayer forbids indecency and evil, meaning it forbids its opposites, since its people perform it upon the states ordered: purity, covering, humility, and fulfilling what must be fulfilled, and Allah promised its people what is in the verse we recited, so theft was its opposite and it forbids its opposites, and Allah returns its people to it and removes its opposites from them till He fulfills their reward and settles them the rank of its people. And in that is what indicates that He by His favor, kindness and vast mercy clears that thief of what he stole and returns him to its people till he meets Him on the day he meets Him with no claim preventing him from entering His Paradise by His favor and power, and we ask Allah for guidance and to make us of the rank of accepted prayer, and may Allah bless Muhammad the Prophet and his family and grant much peace."
"2358","Chapter 331 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that stoning is of what Allah sent down in His Book, and what was narrated from others of the Companions of the abrogation thereof from the Quran. 2057 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he informed him, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas say: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said while sitting on the pulpit of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah sent to us Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with truth and sent down upon him the Book, and of what He sent down upon him was the verse of stoning; we recited it, retained it and understood it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned and we stoned after him, and I fear if time grows long over people an utterer will say: By Allah, we do not find stoning in Allah's Book, so they go astray by leaving an obligation Allah sent down, and stoning is in Allah's Book upon whoever commits adultery when married, of men or women, when proof stands, or pregnancy, or confession."
"2359","2058 - And we found Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"2360","2059 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah that Ibn Abbas informed him, then he mentioned from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it and added therein: By Allah, were it not that people would say Umar wrote in Allah's Book what was not sent down, I would have written it."
"2361","2060 - And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuh Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned and we stoned, and Allah sent it down in His Book, and were it not that people would say Umar added to Allah's Book, I would have written it with my handwriting till I join it to the Book. Abu Jafar said: So in this hadith was Umar's saying that stoning is of what Allah sent down in His Book, and this in our view is of the kind we mentioned before in this book of what was sent down as Quran, then Umar stood upon that, then it was abrogated and removed from the Quran, but he had not stood upon that, so he said what he said for this meaning, while others of the Companions stood upon that, among them Abu Bakr, Uthman and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, so they did not write it in the Quran for their knowledge that abrogation had reached it and it was removed from the Quran and returned to the Sunna. Then an objector said: Had Abu Bakr written the Quran? So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that Abu Bakr had gathered the Quran and written it."
"2362","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim and Kharija, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, had gathered the Quran in sheets, and he had asked Zayd ibn Thabit to look into that but he refused till he sought help against him through Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, so he did, and those writings were with Abu Bakr till he died, then with Umar till he died, then with Hafsa wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Uthman sent, but she refused to hand them to him till he covenanted to return them to her, so she sent them to him, and Uthman copied them in these codices then returned them to her, and they remained with her till Marwan ibn al-Hakam sent and took them and burned them."
"2363","And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Sabbaq, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to me and said: I see the Quran be gathered. I said: How do you do something the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do? He said: By Allah it is good. So Umar kept reviewing me therein till Allah expanded my breast therefor and I saw therein what he saw. Zayd said, while Umar sat with him not speaking: Then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You are a young intelligent man and we do not suspect you, and you used to write revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so follow the Quran and gather it. So I followed the Quran and gathered it from shoulder blades, palm stalks, shoulder bones and men's breasts, and the codices in which I gathered the Quran were with Abu Bakr his life, then Allah took him, then with Umar till Allah took him, then with Hafsa daughter of Umar. So in what we narrated was what indicates Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had stood upon the stoning verse being abrogated from the Quran and returned to the Sunna, and Uthman also had stood upon that."
"2364","2061 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Salama, meaning Ibn Kuhayl, from al-Shabi, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, lashed Shuraha on Thursday and stoned her on Friday, and said: I lashed her by Allah's Book and stoned her by the Sunna of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2365","2062 - And Abu Qurra Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Muslim al-Awar, from Habba, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Shuraha came to him and confessed she had committed adultery, so Ali said to her: Perhaps you disobeyed yourself. She said: I was approached willingly not coerced. So he let her out till she gave birth and weaned her child, then he lashed her the hadd by her confession, then buried her in the courtyard to her shoulders and cast the first stone himself, then said: Cast. Then said: I lashed her by Allah's Book and stoned her by the Sunna of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Ali informed by what we narrated from him that stoning in adultery is Sunna not Quran, and Zayd ibn Thabit followed Abu Bakr and Umar upon that, and he is the one who used to write the Quran for Abu Bakr with his old knowledge thereof for writing revelation for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and whoever knows a thing is worthier than whoever does not know it, so the knowledge of Abu Bakr, Uthman and Ali of the stoning verse leaving the Quran and its abrogation was worthier than that going unnoticed by Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and the proof that Umar after standing upon what Abu Bakr did saw therein what Abu Bakr saw, for he did not write it in the codex, and were it not so he would not have left writing it therein, but he left writing it therein because he saw their knowledge of what they knew of what escaped his knowledge was worthier than his writing it, so he rejected that and returned to what they were upon. So it became clear by what we mentioned that the stoning which is the hadd of the married adulterer is a Sunna of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not a now-established verse of Allah's Book, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2366","Chapter 332 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that ten sucklings making unlawful had been sent down in the Quran then abrogated by five sucklings, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while they were of what is recited of the Quran. 2063 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala informed us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amra daughter of Abd al-Rahman, from Aisha mother of the believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: Of what was sent down of the Quran were ten known sucklings making unlawful, then abrogated by five known, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while it was of what is recited of the Quran. Abu Jafar said: And this is of what we know none narrated as we mentioned but Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, and it is in our view an error from him, I mean what is therein of what he related from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while it was of what is recited of the Quran, for had that been so it would have been as the rest of the Quran, and it would have been allowed to be recited therewith in prayers, and far be it from Allah that it be so, or that there remain of the Quran what is not in the codices by which proof stands upon us, and whoever disbelieved in a letter of what is therein would be unbeliever, and had there remained of the Quran other than what is therein, it would have been allowed that what is therein be abrogated not to be acted upon, and what is not therein abrogating to be acted upon, and in that is removal of obligation to act by what is in our hands of what is Quran in our view, and we seek refuge with Allah from this saying and from whoever says it. But the reality of this hadith in our view, and Allah knows best, is what was narrated of the people of knowledge from Amra from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, by one whose rank in knowledge and ضبط thereof is above Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr's, and he is al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq."
"2367","2064 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Of what was sent down of the Quran then dropped was that nothing of suckling makes unlawful but ten sucklings, then there was sent down after, or five sucklings. So this hadith is worthier than the hadith we mentioned before it, and therein is that it was sent down of the Quran then dropped, so that indicates it is of what was removed from the Quran abrogating it therefrom as was removed other than it of the Quran we mentioned before, and returned to the Sunna, and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad was followed, upon dropping what is in Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr's hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and that is of what is recited of the Quran, by an imam of his time, and he is Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari."
"2368","2065 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: There was sent down of the Quran: Nothing makes unlawful but ten sucklings, then there was sent down after, or five sucklings."
"2369","2066 - And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amra, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: There was sent down in the Quran: Ten known sucklings, then five sucklings were sent down. Abu Jafar said: So this is worthier than what Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated, for it is impossible Aisha would know there remained of the Quran something not written in the codices then not alert whoever neglected it. But the reality of the matter therein, and Allah knows best, was that it is of what had been sent down as Quran then abrogated and removed from the Quran and returned as Sunna, like others of this kind we mentioned in this book. And of what indicates the corruption of what Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr added over al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Yahya ibn Said in this hadith is that we know none of the imams of knowledge narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr but Malik ibn Anas, then Malik left it and did not say by it but said its opposite, and went to that little and much of suckling makes unlawful, and had what is in this hadith been sound that it is of Allah's Book, he would not have opposed it nor said by other than it, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2370","Chapter 333 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from Aisha and Hafsa, wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from Umm Kulthum, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning this verse: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, and the Asr prayer. 2067 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Yunus, client of Aisha wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Aisha mother of the believers ordered me to write for her a codex and said: When you reach this verse inform me: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer. He said: So when I reached it I informed her, and she dictated to me: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand for Allah devoutly, then she said: I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2371","2068 - Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali and Nafi client of Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to me that Amr ibn Rafi client of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to them that he used to write codices in the time of the Prophet's wives and said: Hafsa daughter of Umar wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had me write a codex, and she said to me: When you reach this verse of Surat al-Baqara do not write it till you come to me, and I shall dictate it to you as I memorized it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So when I reached it I came to her with the sheet I write, and she said: Write: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer."
"2372","2069 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Amr ibn Rafi the like of it, from Hafsa, except that she did not mention therein the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2373","2070 - Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his mother Umm Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, was asked about Allah's saying: And the middle prayer. So she said: We used to recite it upon the first wording in the Messenger of Allah's time: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand for Allah devoutly. Abu Jafar said: So in what we narrated from Aisha, Hafsa and Umm Kulthum, may Allah be pleased with them, is affirmation of the Asr prayer in recitation, so we looked therein whether there was narrated what indicates its abrogation therefrom and removal from the Quran and return to the Sunna, as we mentioned in others."
"2374","2071 - So we found Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shaqiq ibn Uqba narrated to us, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: There was sent down: Guard the prayers and the Asr prayer; we recited it in the Messenger of Allah's time, then Allah abrogated it and sent down: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer. Abu Jafar said: So we stood thereby upon that and the Asr prayer mentioned in Aisha's, Hafsa's and Umm Kulthum's hadiths is of what had been Quran then abrogated and returned to what is in our codices, and likewise all narrated of what is mentioned as of the Quran while we do not find it in our codices is of what had been Quran then abrogated, removed from the Quran and returned to the Sunna so it became thereof, and we ask Allah Almighty for guidance."
"2375","Chapter 334 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that no two men would tread his heels. 2072 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn Abdullah ibn Amr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was never seen eating reclining, nor would two men tread his heels."
"2376","2073 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So we considered this hadith to stand upon the meaning for which men would not tread the heels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2377","2074 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us, from Nubayh al-Anazi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah in his long hadith in which he mentioned the Messenger of Allah's entering his house, he said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and his companions stood and went out before him, and he would say: Leave my back for the angels."
"2378","2075 - And we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Nubayh al-Anazi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out of his house his companions would walk before him and leave behind him for the angels. So what is in this indicated that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only not having his heels trodden because behind him were angels walking behind him, so the dislike in the first hadith we narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr was for that not for other, and in that is what indicates other than him in that is unlike him, and there is no harm upon him in men treading his heels and walking behind him, and there was narrated from him concerning what he did with one who followed him walking behind him."
"2379","2076 - What Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir narrated to us from his father, he said: al-Sumayt narrated to us, from Abu al-Sawwar, Abu al-Sawwar narrates from his maternal uncle, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking with people following him, so I followed him with them, and the people were wary of me, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon me and struck me, either he said with a palm stalk, rod, tooth-stick or something with him. By Allah it did not please me, and I spent a night saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck me only for something he knew in me. So my soul told me to come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning, then Jibril descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: You are a shepherd, so break not the horns of your flock. So when he prayed dawn, or he said we woke, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People follow me, and it does not please me that they follow me: O Allah, whomever I struck or reviled, make it for him expiation and reward, or he said: forgiveness, or as he said. So in what we narrated before this hadith of Jabir's hadith is what indicates the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked men following him from behind, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2380","Chapter 335 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Traders are the wicked. 2077 - Ali ibn Maabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Rashid, and he is al-Hubrani, that he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Traders are the wicked. So it was said: O Messenger of Allah, has not Allah permitted sale? He said: Yes, but they swear and sin and swear and lie."
"2381","2078 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salama Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya, and he is Ibn Abi Kathir, narrated to us, from Zayd, and he is Ibn Sallam, from Abu Sallam, and he is al-Habashi, from Abu Rashid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl that Muawiya said to him: When you come to my camp stand among people and inform of what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Traders are the wicked. So a man said: O Messenger of Allah, did not Allah permit sale? So he said: They speak and lie and swear and sin. Then an objector said: How do you accept this upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while Allah permitted sale? So He said: And Allah permitted sale and forbade usury, and said: Consume not your wealth among you falsely except it be trade by mutual consent. So how may the people of these two verses be wicked? So our answer therein by Allah's guidance and help was that that in our view, and Allah knows best, is only upon the blameworthy traders in their trades not upon the praiseworthy therein, and language applies the like of this in blame and praise together, and of that is Allah's saying to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And it is a reminder for you and your people and you shall be asked, while of his people are those who did not enter this verse, and they are the unbelievers in him of them denying what he brought them, and His saying: And your people denied it while it is truth, so He did not intend thereby all his people but only the deniers of them apart from the believers, may Allah be pleased with them, and of that is the Prophet's saying in his qunut in Fajr prayer: O Allah, harden Your grip upon Mudar, while he is of Mudar and the best of creation are of Mudar, but he intended thereby only the unbelievers of Mudar not others, so like that is what we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning traders, since the predominant upon them is what he described them with, the إطلاق of the utterance he uttered concerning them was allowed, for he only addressed thereby Arabs understanding his intent whose language is his language, and there was narrated from him also what enters this chapter."
"2382","2079 - What Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiya al-Darir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were brokers, so he named us with a name better than our name and said: O company of traders, sale is attended by idle talk and oaths, so mix it with charity."
"2383","2080 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Abu Wail narrate from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza, Shuba said: And al-Amash informed me he heard Abu Wail narrate from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were in the market selling in markets, and we were named brokers, so he named us with a name better than what we named ourselves, and said: O company of traders, your sale is mixed with oath and idle talk, so mix it, al-Amash said: with charity, and Habib said: with something of charity."
"2384","2081 - And what Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Abu Wail narrate from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza, Shuba said: And al-Amash informed me he heard Abu Wail narrate from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza, Shuba said: And al-Amash informed me he heard Abu Wail narrate from Qays ibn Abi Gharaza, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2385","2082 - And what Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Abi Saghira narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar that al-Bara ibn Azib said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we traded in the market and said: O company of traders, you swear much, so mix this sale of yours with charity, so he named us that day traders. Abu Jafar said: So that was also as we narrated before it, and the speech therein is as the speech in what we spoke before. And this hadith was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from another aspect clarifying whom he meant of the traders and excepting whom he did not mean thereby."
"2386","2083 - As Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah ibn Rifaa, and once he said: Ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to al-Baqi and said: O company of traders, till they craned toward him, so he said: Traders will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection wicked except whoever feared, was truthful and was dutiful. So this hadith clarified for us the traders meant by what is in the first hadiths and that they are other than the traders who employ in their trades truthfulness, piety and dutifulness, and with Allah is guidance. And there was narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning."
"2387","2084 - What Abu Ayyub Ubaydullah ibn Imran al-Tabarani narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Thalaba ibn Yazid ibn Thalaba, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, be not a troublemaker nor a trader except a trader of good, nor a tax collector, for those procrastinate in deeds. So in this hadith was clarification of the blameworthy trader and that he is the procrastinator in deeds, and he is whom his trade busies from deeds, so he is thereby unlike whom Allah praised of the traders in His Book by His saying: Men whom neither trade nor sale diverts from Allah's remembrance and establishing prayer and giving zakat, the verse. So we understood thereby these believing traders are praiseworthy, and the traders unlike them are blameworthy, and we ask Allah تعالى for guidance."
"2388","Chapter 336 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: As for me, I do not eat reclining. 2085 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for me, I do not eat reclining."
"2389","2086 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2390","2087 - And Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, he said: Yaqub al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Then a man said to Shuba: Who narrated to you? So he said: The commander of the believers in hadith, Sufyan ibn Said ibn Masruq."
"2391","2088 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Uqba ibn Mukram narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shuba, from Sufyan, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2392","2089 - And Bakkar ibn Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2393","2090 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us. And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, then they all agreed and said: From Raqaba ibn Masqala, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2394","2091 - And Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us. And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Qutayba ibn Said informed us, then each of them said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So we sought the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to eat reclining, what it is, and the highest we found therein from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was:"
"2395","2092 - What Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Uthman al-Himsi informed me, he said: Baqiyya ibn al-Walid al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, he said: Ibn Abbas used to narrate that Allah sent to His Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an angel with Jibril, peace be upon him, and the angel said: Allah gives you choice between being a servant prophet and being a king. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Jibril as consulting, and Jibril gestured with his hand: Be humble. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather I shall be a servant prophet. And after that utterance he never ate food reclining. Ahmad ibn Shuayb said to us: And we know not this Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas but Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, as if al-Zuhri attributed him to his grandfather, and we know not for him hearing from his grandfather. Abu Jafar said: So this is the highest we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the meaning for which he would not eat reclining, and it is a good meaning, and it may be he left eating reclining because eating reclining is not of the Arabs' custom, but their custom was upon its opposite, and like that is what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2396","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Asim al-Ahwal informed us, from Abu Uthman, he said: The letter of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to us: Be rough and coarse and devoted and be as Mad, and beware luxury and the attire of non-Arabs. He said: So he forbade them the attire of non-Arabs including luxury, and ordered them with Tamaddud, and it is the rough living the Arabs know. So like that in our view, and Allah knows best, is the Messenger's leaving eating reclining; it may be because it is of a people whose custom was not upon it, and confirmed of their custom with him what He ordered him of things by which he is upon what the prophets before him were upon, Allah's blessings upon them, unlike what non-Arabs were upon, and we ask Allah for guidance."
"2397","Chapter 337 - Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding drinking standing. 2093 - Abu al-Qasim Hisham ibn Muhammad ibn Qurra ibn Abi Khalifa al-Ruayni narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abi Imran and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi both narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Ismail al-Talqani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim al-Jadhmi, from al-Jarud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked drinking standing."
"2398","2094 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud ibn al-Mualla, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2399","2095 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-Ayshi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Said, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud, and from Said, from Qatada, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2400","2096 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, they said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us. And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, both said: Qatada narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
